ta-s="Augustine1/2" onclick="event.stopPropagation();showMSInfo(this,'Augustine1/2');">Augustine1/2</span></td><td class="tc-word-cell tc-interactive" style="background:#f5f5f5" data-tip="en medio" data-lemma="μέσος" data-morph="A-NSM" data-strongs="G3319">μέσος</td><td class="tc-word-cell tc-interactive" style="background:#f5f5f5" data-tip="de ustedes" data-lemma="σύ" data-morph="P-2GP" data-strongs="G4771">ὑμῶν</td><td class="tc-word-cell tc-interactive" style="background:#ffe0b2" data-tip="está de pie" data-lemma="στήκω" data-morph="V-PAI-3S" data-strongs="G4739">στήκει</td><td class="tc-word-cell tc-interactive" style="background:#f5f5f5" data-tip="a quien" data-lemma="ὅς, ἥ" data-morph="R-ASM" data-strongs="G3739">ὃν</td><td class="tc-word-cell tc-interactive" style="background:#f5f5f5" data-tip="ustedes" data-lemma="σύ" data-morph="P-2NP" data-strongs="G4771">ὑμεῖς</td></tr></table></div><div style="font-size:0.72rem;color:#555;margin:0.3rem 0;padding:0.3rem 0.5rem;background:#f5f5f5;border-radius:4px"><strong>Lecturas:</strong> <span style="margin-right:0.8rem">#1: <span style="font-family:serif;color:#1b5e20">ἕστηκεν</span></span><span style="margin-right:0.8rem">#2: <span style="font-family:serif;color:#1b5e20">εἱστήκει</span></span><span style="margin-right:0.8rem">#3: <span style="font-family:serif;color:#1b5e20">στήκει</span></span></div><div style="font-size:0.63rem;color:#999;margin-top:2px;display:flex;gap:0.5rem;flex-wrap:wrap"><span style="font-weight:600">MSS:</span> <span>🔴 Papiros</span> <span>🔵 Unciales</span> <span>⚪ Minúsc.</span> <span>🟣 Versiones</span> <span>🟠 Padres</span> <span>🟢 Byz/Lect</span></div><div class="tc-criteria-expanded"><div class="tc-criterion-item"><strong>Apoyo mayoritario</strong><div class="tc-criterion-exp">La forma ἕστηκεν tiene el apoyo más amplio entre manuscritos de diferentes familias textuales. Esta distribución amplia sugiere que es la lectura original.</div></div><div class="tc-criterion-item"><strong>Lectio difficilior</strong><div class="tc-criterion-exp">La forma perfecta ἕστηκεν es morfológicamente más compleja que las alternativas. Los escribas habrían tendido a simplificar hacia formas más regulares como στήκει.</div></div></div><div style="font-size:0.72rem;color:#1565c0;margin-top:0.5rem;cursor:pointer" onclick="document.getElementById('exeg-26')?.classList.remove('collapsed');document.getElementById('exeg-26')?.scrollIntoView({behavior:'smooth'})">↑ Ver análisis exegético de este versículo</div><div style="margin-top:0.8rem;text-align:center"><a href="tc_deep_dive_John_1_26.html" onclick="if(!this.dataset.ready){this.textContent='⏳ Generando análisis profundo...';this.style.opacity=0.6;return true;}" style="display:inline-block;padding:8px 20px;background:linear-gradient(135deg,#1a237e,#0d47a1);color:white;border-radius:8px;text-decoration:none;font-size:0.85rem;font-weight:600;box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(26,35,126,0.3)">🔍 Deep Dive — Análisis completo de esta variante</a><div style="font-size:0.7rem;color:#888;margin-top:3px">Si la página no existe, usa el tool <code>critical_apparatus</code> → <code>tc_deep_dive</code> en el MCP server para generarla.</div></div></div></div><div class="verse-block" id="vb27"><div class="verse-header"><span class="vnum">27</span></div><div class="greek-line" id="greek-27">ὁ ὀπίσω μου ἐρχόμενος οὗ οὐκ εἰμὶ ἐγὼ ἄξιος ἵνα λύσω αὐτοῦ τὸν ἱμάντα τοῦ ὑποδήματος</div><div class="uncial-line" id="uncial-27" style="display:none;font-family:'Noto Serif',serif;font-size:0.9rem;letter-spacing:1.5px;color:#5d4037;background:#efebe9;padding:6px 10px;border-radius:4px;margin-bottom:0.3rem;overflow-wrap:break-word" title="Así se vería este versículo en un manuscrito uncial del siglo IV — todo mayúsculas, sin espacios (puntos medios añadidos como ayuda visual)">Ο·ΟΠΙΣΩ·ΜΟΥ·ΕΡΧΟΜΕΝΟΣ·ΟΥ·ΟΥΚ·ΕΙΜΙ·ΕΓΩ·ΑΞΙΟΣ·ΙΝΑ·ΛΥΣΩ·ΑΥΤΟΥ·ΤΟΝ·ΙΜΑΝΤΑ·ΤΟΥ·ΥΠΟΔΗΜΑΤΟΣ<span style="display:block;font-size:0.65rem;margin-top:4px;letter-spacing:0;opacity:0.7"><a href="https://www.codexsinaiticus.org/en/manuscript.aspx?book=33&chapter=1&verse=27" target="_blank" style="color:#5d4037">Ver en Codex Sinaiticus →</a></span></div><div style="display:flex;gap:0.3rem;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:0.3rem;align-items:center"><div class="rvr-btn" onclick="toggleRVR(27)">RVR60</div><div class="rvr-btn" onclick="document.getElementById('uncial-27').style.display=document.getElementById('uncial-27').style.display==='none'?'':'none'" style="background:#efebe9;color:#5d4037;border-color:#bcaaa4" title="Escritura uncial: así se veía el NT en los manuscritos del s.IV (Vaticanus, Sinaiticus). Todo mayúsculas, sin espacios entre palabras (scriptio continua), sin acentos ni puntuación. La iota subscrita se escribe explícitamente.">Uncial</div><div class="rvr-btn" onclick="document.getElementById('pron-27').style.display=document.getElementById('pron-27').style.display==='none'?'':'none'" style="background:#e3f2fd;color:#1565c0;border-color:#90caf9" title="Pronunciación bizantina aproximada (griego moderno/eclesiástico)">Pronunciación</div><div class="rvr-btn vbtn-patr" onclick="toggleSection('patr-27')" title="15 citas patrísticas disponibles">✝ 15 patrística</div><div class="rvr-btn vbtn-exeg" onclick="toggleSection('exeg-27')" title="2 comentarios exegéticos">📚 2 exégesis</div><div class="rvr-btn vbtn-ver" onclick="document.getElementById('vers-27').classList.toggle('collapsed')" title="9 versiones disponibles">📖 versiones</div><div class="rvr-btn" onclick="copyVerse(27)" title="Copiar versículo al portapapeles" style="background:#f3e5f5;border-color:#ce93d8;color:#6a1b9a">📋</div><div class="rvr-btn" onclick="document.getElementById('notes-27').style.display=document.getElementById('notes-27').style.display==='none'?'':'none'" title="Notas personales" style="background:#fff8e1;border-color:#ffd54f;color:#f57f17">📝</div></div><div id="rvr-27" class="collapsed spanish-line">Este es el que viene después de mí, el que es antes de mí, del cual yo no soy digno de desatar la correa del calzado.</div><div id="vers-27" class="collapsed" style="margin-bottom:0.5rem"><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">RVR60</span>Este es el que viene después de mí, el que es antes de mí, del cual yo no soy digno de desatar la correa del calzado.</div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">RVR1909</span>Este es el que ha de venir tras mí, el cual es antes de mí: del cual yo no soy digno de desatar la correa del zapato.</div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">KJV</span>He it is, who coming after me is preferred before me, whose shoe’s latchet I am not worthy to unloose.</div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">ASV</span>even he that cometh after me, the latchet of whose shoe I am not worthy to unloose. </div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">BSB</span>He is the One who comes after me, the straps of whose sandals I am not worthy to untie.” </div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">Darby</span>he who comes after me, the thong of whose sandal I am not worthy to unloose. </div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">LITV</span>This One it is who has come after me, who has been before me, of whom I am not worthy that I should loose the thong of His sandal. </div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">YLT</span>of whom I am not worthy that I may loose the cord of his sandal.'</div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">Vulgate</span>ipse est qui post me venturus est qui ante me factus est cuius ego non sum dignus ut solvam eius corrigiam calciamenti</div></div><div id="pron-27" style="display:none;font-size:0.85rem;color:#1565c0;background:#e3f2fd;padding:4px 8px;border-radius:4px;margin-bottom:0.3rem;font-style:italic" class="pron-line"></div><div id="notes-27" style="display:none;margin-bottom:0.5rem"><textarea id="note-input-27" style="width:100%;min-height:50px;border:1px solid #ccc;border-radius:6px;padding:4px 8px;font-size:0.82rem;resize:vertical" placeholder="Tus notas para v.27..." oninput="saveNote(27)"></textarea></div><div class="section-toggle" onclick="toggleSection('exeg-27')" role="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="exeg-27" tabindex="0"><span class="section-icon">📜</span> Exégesis del Griego <span class="arrow" id="arrow-exeg-27">▶</span></div><div id="exeg-27" class="collapsed section-content exeg-section" role="region"><div class="exeg-summary"><strong style="color:#1b5e20">Bengel:</strong> <br><strong style="color:#1b5e20">Robertson:</strong> Literally, "of whom I am not worthy that I unloose the latchet (see [Mark 1:7] for ιμας ) of his sandal (see [Matthew 3:11] for υποδημα , bound under the foot).<br></div><div class="comm-item"><div class="comm-header" onclick="document.getElementById('uexv27_0').classList.toggle('collapsed')"><strong>Bengel's Gnomon</strong> <span style=color:#888;font-size:0.75rem>(1742)</span><span class="small-arrow">ver completo ▼</span></div><div id="uexv27_0" class="collapsed comm-body">[John 1:27] . Αὐτός ) Himself .</div></div><div class="comm-item"><div class="comm-header" onclick="document.getElementById('uexv27_1').classList.toggle('collapsed')"><strong>Robertson's Word Pictures</strong> <span style=color:#888;font-size:0.75rem>(1933)</span><span class="small-arrow">ver completo ▼</span></div><div id="uexv27_1" class="collapsed comm-body">Coming after me ( οπισω μου ερχομενος ). No article ( ο ) in Aleph B. John as the forerunner of the Messiah has preceded him in time, but not in rank as he instantly adds. The latchet of whose shoe I am not worthy to unloose ( ου ουκ ειμ αξιος ινα λυσω αυτου τον ιμαντα του υποδηματος ). Literally, "of whom I am not worthy that I unloose the latchet (see [Mark 1:7] for ιμας ) of his sandal (see [Matthew 3:11] for υποδημα , bound under the foot)." Only use of αξιος with ινα in John, though used by Paul in this saying of the Baptist ([Acts 13:25]), ικανος ινα in [Matthew 3:8], but ικανος λυσα (aorist active infinitive instead of λυσω , aorist active subjunctive) in [Mark 1:7] ([Luke 3:16]) and βαστασα in [Matthew 3:11].</div></div></div><div class="section-toggle" onclick="toggleSection('patr-27')" role="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="patr-27" tabindex="0"><span class="section-icon">👨‍🏫</span> Patrística · 3 temas · 15 citas <span class="arrow" id="arrow-patr-27">▶</span></div><div id="patr-27" class="collapsed section-content patr-section" role="region"><div class="patr-theme"><div class="patr-theme-header" onclick="document.getElementById('pt27e24c67').classList.toggle('collapsed');this.querySelector('.arrow').classList.toggle('open')"><strong>Humildad de Juan el Bautista</strong><div class="consensus-bar" style="width:60px"><div style="width:85%;background:#4caf50"></div></div><span class="consensus-label" style="color:#4caf50">alto</span><span class="arrow">▼</span></div><div id="pt27e24c67" class="collapsed" style="padding:0.5rem 0.2rem 0"><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>John Chrysostom</strong> (s.IV (349-407)) <span class="patr-meta">— Patriarca de Constantinopla, "Boca de Oro", discípulo de Diodoro de Tarso (Antioquía/Constantinopla)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Juan_Cris%C3%B3stomo" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.27]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"Whose shoe's latchet I am not worthy to unloose; that is, who is not simply before me, but before me in such a way, that I am not worthy to be numbered among the meanest of His servants. For to loose the shoe is the office of humblest service."</div></div><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>Augustine of Hippo</strong> (s.IV-V (354-430)) <span class="patr-meta">— Obispo de Hipona, Doctor de la Iglesia, discípulo de Ambrosio de Milán (Hipona)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agust%C3%ADn_de_Hipona" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.27]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"To have pronounced himself worthy even of unloosing His shoe's latchet, he would have been thinking too much of himself."</div></div><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>Clement of Alexandria</strong> (s.II-III (150-215)) <span class="patr-meta">— Maestro catequético, discípulo de Panteno (Alejandría)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clemente_de_Alejandr%C3%ADa" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.27]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"I am not worthy to loose the latchet of the Lord's shoe. For he confesses that he is not worthy to baptize so great a Power; for it behooves those, who purify others, to free the soul from the body and its sins, as the foot from the thong."</div></div><div class="patr-resumen">Existe consenso total sobre la profunda humildad de Juan, quien se considera indigno incluso del más humilde servicio hacia Cristo.</div></div></div><div class="patr-theme"><div class="patr-theme-header" onclick="document.getElementById('pt450385b2').classList.toggle('collapsed');this.querySelector('.arrow').classList.toggle('open')"><strong>Superioridad de Cristo sobre Juan</strong><div class="consensus-bar" style="width:60px"><div style="width:85%;background:#4caf50"></div></div><span class="consensus-label" style="color:#4caf50">alto</span><span class="arrow">▼</span></div><div id="pt450385b2" class="collapsed" style="padding:0.5rem 0.2rem 0"><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>Gregory the Dialogist</strong> (s.VI (540-604)) <span class="patr-meta">— Papa, reformador, Doctor de la Iglesia (Roma)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gregorio_I" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.27]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"Made before me, i. e. preferred before me. He comes after me, that is, He is born after me; He is made before me, that is, He is preferred to me."</div></div><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>John Chrysostom</strong> (s.IV (349-407)) <span class="patr-meta">— Patriarca de Constantinopla, "Boca de Oro", discípulo de Diodoro de Tarso (Antioquía/Constantinopla)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Juan_Cris%C3%B3stomo" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.27]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"Now if John was not worthy to unloose the latchet, John, than whom among them that are born of women there hath not risen a greater, where shall we rank ourselves?"</div></div><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>Thomas Aquinas</strong> (s.XIII (1225-1274)) <span class="patr-meta">— Teólogo escolástico, Doctor de la Iglesia, discípulo de Alberto Magno (Italia)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tom%C3%A1s_de_Aquino" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.27]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"He shows the superiority of Christ in comparison to himself both in preaching and in dignity. Now, as to the order of preaching, John was the first to become known. Thus he says, the one who is to come after me, to preach, to baptize and to die."</div></div><div class="patr-resumen">Hay acuerdo unánime en que Cristo es infinitamente superior a Juan, tanto en dignidad como en poder, a pesar de venir después cronológicamente.</div></div></div><div class="patr-theme"><div class="patr-theme-header" onclick="document.getElementById('pt147a31e4').classList.toggle('collapsed');this.querySelector('.arrow').classList.toggle('open')"><strong>Juan como precursor y testigo</strong><div class="consensus-bar" style="width:60px"><div style="width:85%;background:#4caf50"></div></div><span class="consensus-label" style="color:#4caf50">alto</span><span class="arrow">▼</span></div><div id="pt147a31e4" class="collapsed" style="padding:0.5rem 0.2rem 0"><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>Augustine of Hippo</strong> (s.IV-V (354-430)) <span class="patr-meta">— Obispo de Hipona, Doctor de la Iglesia, discípulo de Ambrosio de Milán (Hipona)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agust%C3%ADn_de_Hipona" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.27]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"And yet, just notice how this forerunner of his Lord, of one who is God and man, how much he humbles himself. No one has arisen greater among those born of women than this man, and here he is, questioned about whether he is himself the Christ."</div></div><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>Gregory the Dialogist</strong> (s.VI (540-604)) <span class="patr-meta">— Papa, reformador, Doctor de la Iglesia (Roma)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gregorio_I" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.26]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"Why then doth he baptize, when he doth not remit sins by baptism? To maintain his character of forerunner. As his birth preceded our Lord's birth, so his baptism preceded our Lord's baptism."</div></div><div class="patr-resumen">Los Padres reconocen unánimemente el papel único de Juan como precursor que prepara el camino para Cristo a través de su testimonio y bautismo.</div></div></div></div></div><div class="verse-block" id="vb28"><div class="verse-header"><span class="vnum">28</span></div><div class="greek-line" id="greek-28">Ταῦτα ἐν Βηθανίᾳ ἐγένετο πέραν τοῦ Ἰορδάνου ὅπου ἦν ὁ Ἰωάννης βαπτίζων</div><div class="uncial-line" id="uncial-28" style="display:none;font-family:'Noto Serif',serif;font-size:0.9rem;letter-spacing:1.5px;color:#5d4037;background:#efebe9;padding:6px 10px;border-radius:4px;margin-bottom:0.3rem;overflow-wrap:break-word" title="Así se vería este versículo en un manuscrito uncial del siglo IV — todo mayúsculas, sin espacios (puntos medios añadidos como ayuda visual)">ΤΑΥΤΑ·ΕΝ·ΒΗΘΑΝΙΑΙ·ΕΓΕΝΕΤΟ·ΠΕΡΑΝ·ΤΟΥ·ΙΟΡΔΑΝΟΥ·ΟΠΟΥ·ΗΝ·Ο·ΙΩΑΝΝΗΣ·ΒΑΠΤΙΖΩΝ<span style="display:block;font-size:0.65rem;margin-top:4px;letter-spacing:0;opacity:0.7"><a href="https://www.codexsinaiticus.org/en/manuscript.aspx?book=33&chapter=1&verse=28" target="_blank" style="color:#5d4037">Ver en Codex Sinaiticus →</a></span></div><div style="display:flex;gap:0.3rem;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:0.3rem;align-items:center"><div class="rvr-btn" onclick="toggleRVR(28)">RVR60</div><div class="rvr-btn" onclick="document.getElementById('uncial-28').style.display=document.getElementById('uncial-28').style.display==='none'?'':'none'" style="background:#efebe9;color:#5d4037;border-color:#bcaaa4" title="Escritura uncial: así se veía el NT en los manuscritos del s.IV (Vaticanus, Sinaiticus). Todo mayúsculas, sin espacios entre palabras (scriptio continua), sin acentos ni puntuación. La iota subscrita se escribe explícitamente.">Uncial</div><div class="rvr-btn" onclick="document.getElementById('pron-28').style.display=document.getElementById('pron-28').style.display==='none'?'':'none'" style="background:#e3f2fd;color:#1565c0;border-color:#90caf9" title="Pronunciación bizantina aproximada (griego moderno/eclesiástico)">Pronunciación</div><div class="rvr-btn vbtn-patr" onclick="toggleSection('patr-28')" title="8 citas patrísticas disponibles">✝ 8 patrística</div><div class="rvr-btn vbtn-exeg" onclick="toggleSection('exeg-28')" title="3 comentarios exegéticos">📚 3 exégesis</div><div class="rvr-btn vbtn-ver" onclick="document.getElementById('vers-28').classList.toggle('collapsed')" title="9 versiones disponibles">📖 versiones</div><div class="rvr-btn" onclick="copyVerse(28)" title="Copiar versículo al portapapeles" style="background:#f3e5f5;border-color:#ce93d8;color:#6a1b9a">📋</div><div class="rvr-btn" onclick="document.getElementById('notes-28').style.display=document.getElementById('notes-28').style.display==='none'?'':'none'" title="Notas personales" style="background:#fff8e1;border-color:#ffd54f;color:#f57f17">📝</div></div><div id="rvr-28" class="collapsed spanish-line">Estas cosas sucedieron en Betábara, al otro lado del Jordán, donde Juan estaba bautizando. El Cordero de Dios</div><div id="vers-28" class="collapsed" style="margin-bottom:0.5rem"><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">RVR60</span>Estas cosas sucedieron en Betábara, al otro lado del Jordán, donde Juan estaba bautizando. El Cordero de Dios</div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">RVR1909</span>Estas cosas acontecieron en Betábara, de la otra parte del Jordán, donde Juan bautizaba.</div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">KJV</span>These things were done in Bethabara beyond Jordan, where John was baptizing.</div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">ASV</span>These things were done in Bethany beyond the Jordan, where John was baptizing. </div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">BSB</span>All this happened at Bethany beyond the Jordan, where John was baptizing. </div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">Darby</span>These things took place in Bethany, across the Jordan, where John was baptising. </div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">LITV</span>These things took place in Bethabara beyond the Jordan, where John was baptizing.   </div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">YLT</span>These things came to pass in Bethabara, beyond the Jordan, where John was baptizing,</div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">Vulgate</span>haec in Bethania facta sunt trans Iordanen ubi erat Iohannes baptizans</div></div><div id="pron-28" style="display:none;font-size:0.85rem;color:#1565c0;background:#e3f2fd;padding:4px 8px;border-radius:4px;margin-bottom:0.3rem;font-style:italic" class="pron-line"></div><div id="notes-28" style="display:none;margin-bottom:0.5rem"><textarea id="note-input-28" style="width:100%;min-height:50px;border:1px solid #ccc;border-radius:6px;padding:4px 8px;font-size:0.82rem;resize:vertical" placeholder="Tus notas para v.28..." oninput="saveNote(28)"></textarea></div><div class="section-toggle" onclick="toggleSection('exeg-28')" role="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="exeg-28" tabindex="0"><span class="section-icon">📜</span> Exégesis del Griego <span class="arrow" id="arrow-exeg-28">▶</span></div><div id="exeg-28" class="collapsed section-content exeg-section" role="region"><div class="exeg-summary"><strong style="color:#1b5e20">Alford:</strong> ] The common reading, Βηθαβαρᾷ , is owing to a conjecture of Origen, the grounds of which he thus states: ὅτι μὲν σχεδὸν ἐν πᾶσι τοῖς ἀντιγράφοις κεῖται &ldquo; ταῦτα ἐν Βηθανίᾳ ἐγένετο &rdquo; οὐκ ἀγ…<br><strong style="color:#1b5e20">Bengel:</strong> Ἐν βηθαβαρᾶ , in Bethabara ) Therefore they had come a long way, [John 1:19].<br><strong style="color:#1b5e20">Robertson:</strong> In Bethany beyond Jordan ( εν Βηθανια περαν του Ιορδανου ).<br></div><div class="comm-item"><div class="comm-header" onclick="document.getElementById('uexv28_0').classList.toggle('collapsed')"><strong>Alford's Greek Testament</strong> <span style=color:#888;font-size:0.75rem>(1863)</span><span class="small-arrow">ver completo ▼</span></div><div id="uexv28_0" class="collapsed comm-body">28. ] The common reading, Βηθαβαρᾷ , is owing to a conjecture of Origen, the grounds of which he thus states: ὅτι μὲν σχεδὸν ἐν πᾶσι τοῖς ἀντιγράφοις κεῖται &ldquo; ταῦτα ἐν Βηθανίᾳ ἐγένετο &rdquo; οὐκ ἀγνοοῦμεν , καὶ ἔοικε τοῦτο καὶ ἔτι πρότερον γεγονέναι · καὶ παρὰ Ἡρακλεωνι γοῦν Βηθανίαν ἀνέγνωμεν . ἐπείσθημεν δὲ μὴ δεῖν Βηθανίᾳ ἀναγινώσκειν , ἀλλὰ Βηθαβαρᾷ , γενόμενοι ἐν τοῖς τόποις ἐπὶ ἱστορίαν τῶν ἰχνῶν Ἰησοῦ καὶ τῶν μαθητῶν αὐτοῦ καὶ τῶν προφητῶν . Βηθανία γάρ , ὡς ὁ αὐτὸς εὐαγγελιστής φησι , ἡ πατρὶς Λαζάρου καὶ Μάρθας καὶ Μαρίας , ἀπέχει τῶν Ἱεροσολύμων σταδίους δέκα πέντε · ἧς πόῤῥω ἐστὶν ὁ Ιορδάνης ποταμός , ὡς ἀπὸ σταδίων πλατεῖ λόγῳ ρπ ʼ (180). ἀλλ ʼ οὐδὲ ὁμώνυμος τῇ Βηθανίᾳ τόπος ἐστὶν περὶ τὸν Ἰορδάνην · δείκνυσθαι δὲ λέγουσι παρὰ τῇ ὄχθῃ τοῦ Ἰορδάνου τὰ Βηθαβαρᾶ , ἔνθα ἱστοροῦσι τὸν Ἰωάννην βεβαπτικέναι (In Joan. [John 6:24] , p. 140). He goes on to shew from the etymology of the names that it must have been Bethabara; an argument which modern criticism will not much esteem. It will be seen that his testimony is decisive for the universality and authority of Βηθανίᾳ , while for the other he only produces a tradition, and that only at second-hand; &ldquo; they say that such a place is shewn.&rdquo; That no Bethany beyond Jordan was known in his time proves but little; for 300 eventful years had changed the face of Palestine since these events, and the names and sites of many obscure places may have been forgotten. I abstain from enumerating modern conjectures on the identity of the two, or the etymology of the names, as being indecisive and unprofitable. The objection of Paulus, that πέραν τοῦ Ἰορδάνου the Sanhedrim had no authority, appears not to be founded in fact: see Lücke&rsquo;s Comm. i. 394 ff. The question whether this testimony of the Baptist is identical with that given by the three other Evangelists, especially by Luke ( Luk 3:16 ), is, after all that has been said on it (Lücke, De Wette, Olshausen, &c.), not of great importance. The whole series of transactions here recorded, from Joh 1:15 onwards, certainly happened after the baptism of our Lord; for before that event John did not know Him as ὁ ἐρχόμενος : and μέσος ὑμῶν στήκει Joh 1:26 shews that he had so recognized Him (see below on τῇ ἐπαύρ .): whereas the testimony in Luk 3:16 and [28] , is as certainly given before the baptism. But since the great end of John&rsquo;s mission was to proclaim Him who was coming after him, it is not only probable, but absolutely necessary to suppose, that he should have delivered this testimony often , and under varying circumstances: before the baptism, in the form given by Luke, ἔρχεται ὁ ἰσχυρ . μου κ . τ . λ ., and after it in this form, οὗτος ἦν ὃν εἶπον ( Joh 1:15 ), where his former testimony is distinctly referred to. And among John&rsquo;s disciples and the multitudes who frequented his baptism, many reports of such his sayings would naturally be current. So that there is neither a real nor even an apparent contradiction b</div></div><div class="comm-item"><div class="comm-header" onclick="document.getElementById('uexv28_1').classList.toggle('collapsed')"><strong>Bengel's Gnomon</strong> <span style=color:#888;font-size:0.75rem>(1742)</span><span class="small-arrow">ver completo ▼</span></div><div id="uexv28_1" class="collapsed comm-body">[John 1:28] . Ἐν βηθαβαρᾶ , in Bethabara ) Therefore they had come a long way, [John 1:19] . πέραν ) beyond , in relation to Jerusalem. ὁποῦ , where ) Where he was wont to baptize. [ Βηθανίᾳ is the reading of the mass of authorities, [31][32][33] * [34][35][36] . Βηθαβαρᾶ was a conjecture of Orige [37] . The Bethany here was one beyond Jordan , which had ceased to exist before Orige [38] &rsquo;s time.] [31] the Alexandrine MS.: in Brit. Museum: fifth century: publ. by Woide, 1786 1819: O. and N. Test. defective. [32] the Vatican MS., 1209: in Vat. Iibr., Rome: fourth cent.: O. and N. Test. def. [33] Ephræmi Rescriptus: Royal libr., Paris: fifth or sixth cent.: publ. by Tisch. 1843: O. and N. T. def. [34] Cod. Reg., Paris, of the Gospels: the text akin to that of B: edited by Tisch. [35] Cod. Monacensis, fragments of the Gospels. [36] San Gallensis: in the libr. of St. Gall: the Greek and Latin of the four Gospels. It and G. Boernerianus of Paul&rsquo;s Epp. are severed parts of the same book. [37] rigen (born about 186 A.D., died 253 A.D., a Greek father: two-thirds of the N. Test. are quoted in his writings). Ed. Vinc. Delarue, Paris. 1733, 1740, 1759. [38] rigen (born about 186 A.D., died 253 A.D., a Greek father: two-thirds of the N. Test. are quoted in his writings). Ed. Vinc. Delarue, Paris. 1733, 1740, 1759.</div></div><div class="comm-item"><div class="comm-header" onclick="document.getElementById('uexv28_2').classList.toggle('collapsed')"><strong>Robertson's Word Pictures</strong> <span style=color:#888;font-size:0.75rem>(1933)</span><span class="small-arrow">ver completo ▼</span></div><div id="uexv28_2" class="collapsed comm-body">In Bethany beyond Jordan ( εν Βηθανια περαν του Ιορδανου ). Undoubtedly the correct text, not "in Bethabara" as Origen suggested instead of "in Bethany" of all the known Greek manuscripts under the mistaken notion that the only Bethany was that near Jerusalem. Was baptizing ( ην βαπτιζων ). Periphrastic imperfect, common idiom in John.</div></div><div class="themes-divider">🔍 Temas discutidos</div><div class="theme-card"><div class="theme-word">Βηθανίᾳ / Βηθαβαρᾷ</div><div class="consensus-bar"><div style="width:85%;background:#4caf50"></div></div><div class="opinion" style="border-left-color:#4caf50">✅ <strong>Alford</strong> Βηθαβαρᾷ es una conjetura de Orígenes, quien erróneamente pensó que solo existía la Betania cerca de Jerusalén</div><div class="opinion" style="border-left-color:#4caf50">✅ <strong>Bengel</strong> Βηθανίᾳ es la lectura de la masa de autoridades, Βηθαβαρᾶ fue conjetura de Orígenes - la Betania aquí era una más allá del Jordán</div><div class="opinion" style="border-left-color:#4caf50">✅ <strong>Robertson</strong> Indudablemente el texto correcto es Βηθανίᾳ, no Βηθαβαρᾷ como sugirió Orígenes bajo la noción equivocada</div><div class="theme-summary">Los tres comentaristas están completamente de acuerdo en que Βηθανίᾳ es la lectura correcta y que Βηθαβαρᾷ fue una conjetura errónea de Orígenes.</div></div></div><div class="section-toggle" onclick="toggleSection('patr-28')" role="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="patr-28" tabindex="0"><span class="section-icon">👨‍🏫</span> Patrística · 3 temas · 8 citas <span class="arrow" id="arrow-patr-28">▶</span></div><div id="patr-28" class="collapsed section-content patr-section" role="region"><div class="patr-theme"><div class="patr-theme-header" onclick="document.getElementById('pt741b970a').classList.toggle('collapsed');this.querySelector('.arrow').classList.toggle('open')"><strong>Ubicación geográfica del bautismo de Juan</strong><div class="consensus-bar" style="width:60px"><div style="width:55%;background:#ff9800"></div></div><span class="consensus-label" style="color:#ff9800">medio</span><span class="arrow">▼</span></div><div id="pt741b970a" class="collapsed" style="padding:0.5rem 0.2rem 0"><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> (s.V) <span class="patr-meta">— Patriarca de Alejandría, Concilio de Éfeso (Alejandría)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cirilo_de_Alejandr%C3%ADa" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.28]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"But he says that these things were done in Bethabara beyond Jordan, putting this too as a sign of accurate and careful narration. For we are all accustomed, so to speak, in our accounts of things that require it to mention also the places where they happened."</div></div><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#ff9800"><div class="patr-cite-header">⚠️ <strong>Origen of Alexandria</strong> (c.185-254) <span class="patr-meta">— Maestro catequético, exégeta, discípulo de Clemente de Alejandría (Alejandría/Cesarea)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Or%C3%ADgenes" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.28]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"We are not unaware that 'Bethany' occurs in nearly all the manuscripts.… But since we have been in these places, so far as historical account is concerned, of the footprints of Jesus and his disciples and the prophets, we have been convinced that we ought not to read 'Bethany' but 'Bethabara.'"</div></div><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#ff9800"><div class="patr-cite-header">⚠️ <strong>Thomas Aquinas</strong> (c.1225-1274) <span class="patr-meta">— Teólogo escolástico, Doctor de la Iglesia, discípulo de Alberto Magno (Italia)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tom%C3%A1s_de_Aquino" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.28]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"Since Bethany is on the Mount of Olives, which is near Jerusalem, as is said in John (11:1) and also in Matthew (26:6), how can he say that these things happened beyond the Jordan, which is quite far from Jerusalem? Origen and Chrysostom answer that it should be called Bethabora, not Bethany, which is a village on the far side of the Jordan"</div></div><div class="patr-resumen">Los padres debaten si el lugar correcto es Betania o Betábara, con Orígenes defendiendo Betábara basado en investigación geográfica. La mayoría de manuscritos dice Betania pero algunos padres prefieren Betábara por razones topográficas.</div></div></div><div class="patr-theme"><div class="patr-theme-header" onclick="document.getElementById('ptdfcf8102').classList.toggle('collapsed');this.querySelector('.arrow').classList.toggle('open')"><strong>Significado simbólico de los nombres de lugares</strong><div class="consensus-bar" style="width:60px"><div style="width:85%;background:#4caf50"></div></div><span class="consensus-label" style="color:#4caf50">alto</span><span class="arrow">▼</span></div><div id="ptdfcf8102" class="collapsed" style="padding:0.5rem 0.2rem 0"><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>Origen of Alexandria</strong> (c.185-254) <span class="patr-meta">— Maestro catequético, exégeta, discípulo de Clemente de Alejandría (Alejandría/Cesarea)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Or%C3%ADgenes" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.28]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"Bethabara means house of preparation; which agreeth with the baptism of Him, who was making ready a people prepared for the Lord. Jordan, again, means, 'their descent.' Now what is this river but our Saviour, through Whom coming into this earth all must be cleansed, in that He came down not for His own sake, but for theirs."</div></div><div class="patr-resumen">Orígenes interpreta los nombres geográficos simbólicamente: Betábara como 'casa de preparación' y Jordán como 'descenso', relacionándolos con la misión salvífica de Cristo.</div></div></div><div class="patr-theme"><div class="patr-theme-header" onclick="document.getElementById('pt0263df9e').classList.toggle('collapsed');this.querySelector('.arrow').classList.toggle('open')"><strong>Valentía y libertad en el testimonio de Juan</strong><div class="consensus-bar" style="width:60px"><div style="width:85%;background:#4caf50"></div></div><span class="consensus-label" style="color:#4caf50">alto</span><span class="arrow">▼</span></div><div id="pt0263df9e" class="collapsed" style="padding:0.5rem 0.2rem 0"><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>John Chrysostom</strong> (c.349-407) <span class="patr-meta">— Patriarca de Constantinopla, "Boca de Oro", discípulo de Diodoro de Tarso (Antioquía/Constantinopla)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Juan_Cris%C3%B3stomo" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.28]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"Such an one was John, who regarded not the multitude, nor opinion, nor anything else belonging to men, but trod all this beneath his feet, and proclaimed to all with becoming freedom the things respecting Christ. And therefore the Evangelist marks the very place, to show the boldness of the loud-voiced herald. For it was not in a house, not in a corner, not in the wilderness, but in the midst of the multitude"</div></div><div class="patr-resumen">Crisóstomo enfatiza la valentía de Juan al dar testimonio públicamente sin temor a las opiniones humanas, proclamando libremente sobre Cristo ante las multitudes.</div></div></div></div><div class="section-toggle" onclick="toggleSection('tc-28')" role="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="tc-28" tabindex="0"><span class="section-icon">⚖️</span> Crítica Textual <span style="font-size:0.7rem;color:#ff9800">(2 lecturas)</span> <span class="arrow" id="arrow-tc-28">▶</span></div><div id="tc-28" class="collapsed section-content tc-section"><div class="tc-verdict" style="margin-bottom:0.8rem"><div class="tc-verdict-text"><strong>Veredicto:</strong> La lectura #28.1 (ἐν Βηθανίᾳ ἐγένετο) es probablemente original, apoyada por manuscritos tempranos como P75, A, B, C*. La lectura #28.2 (ἐν Βηθαβαρᾷ ἐγένετο) representa una corrección geográfica basada en la tradición de Orígenes, quien conocía un lugar llamado Betábara donde Juan bautizaba. Esta variante refleja un intento de armonizar con la geografía conocida del ministerio de Juan el Bautista.</div><div class="consensus-bar" style="max-width:200px"><div style="width:60%;background:#ff9800"></div></div><span style="font-size:0.72rem;color:#ff9800;font-weight:600;margin-left:4px">Confianza: media</span><div class="tc-impact"><strong>Impacto:</strong> La diferencia entre Betania y Betábara es geográficamente significativa para ubicar el ministerio de Juan el Bautista. Orígenes favoreció Betábara porque conocía ese lugar, pero esto puede representar una corrección erudita más que la lectura original.</div></div><div style="overflow-x:auto;margin-bottom:0.5rem"><table class="tc-table"><tr class="tc-header"><th class="tc-ms-col">MS</th><th class="tc-ctx">Ταῦτα</th><th class="tc-var-col" title="Columna variante 1: ἐν">⚡1</th><th class="tc-var-col" title="Columna variante 2: Βηθανίᾳ">⚡2</th><th class="tc-var-col" title="Columna variante 3: ἐγένετο">⚡3</th><th class="tc-ctx">πέραν</th><th class="tc-ctx">τοῦ</th></tr><tr><td class="tc-ms-cell" onclick="showMSSPanel('(𝔓66 א* ἐγένετο ἐν Βηθανίᾳ) 𝔓75 A B C* L Wsupp Δ Θ Ψ* 28 157 205 565 579 597 700 892txt 1006 1010 1071 1241 1243 1342 1424 Byzpt [E F G H (N)] Lect itaur, c, f, ff2, l, q (ita, b, e, r1) vg syrp, h, palmss copbo slav Origen Eusebius (Epiphanius) (mssacc. to Chrysostom) Chrysostom; Ambrosiaster Augustine')"><span class="ms-chip" style="background:#b71c1c;color:white;font-weight:700" data-s="𝔓66" onclick="event.stopPropagation();showMSInfo(this,'𝔓66');">𝔓66</span><span class="ms-chip" style="background:#b71c1c;color:white;font-weight:700" data-s="𝔓75" onclick="event.stopPropagation();showMSInfo(this,'𝔓75');">𝔓75</span><span class="ms-chip" style="background:#1565c0;color:white;font-weight:700" data-s="A" onclick="event.stopPropagation();showMSInfo(this,'A');">A</span><span class="ms-chip" style="background:#1565c0;color:white;font-weight:700" data-s="B" onclick="event.stopPropagation();showMSInfo(this,'B');">B</span><span class="ms-chip" style="background:#1565c0;color:white;font-weight:700" data-s="L" onclick="event.stopPropagation();showMSInfo(this,'L');">L</span><span class="ms-chip" style="background:#fff3e0;color:#e65100" data-s="Wsupp" onclick="event.stopPropagation();showMSInfo(this,'Wsupp');">Wsupp</span><span class="ms-chip" style="background:#1565c0;color:white;font-weight:700" data-s="Δ" onclick="event.stopPropagation();showMSInfo(this,'Δ');">Δ</span><span class="ms-chip" style="background:#1565c0;color:white;font-weight:700" data-s="Θ" onclick="event.stopPropagation();showMSInfo(this,'Θ');">Θ</span><span class="ms-chip" style="background:#e3f2fd;color:#1565c0" data-s="28" onclick="event.stopPropagation();showMSInfo(this,'28');">28</span><span class="ms-chip" style="background:#e3f2fd;color:#1565c0" data-s="157" onclick="event.stopPropagation();showMSInfo(this,'157');">157</span><span class="ms-chip" style="background:#e3f2fd;col