iguo original.</div></div></div><div style="font-size:0.72rem;color:#1565c0;margin-top:0.5rem;cursor:pointer" onclick="document.getElementById('exeg-3')?.classList.remove('collapsed');document.getElementById('exeg-3')?.scrollIntoView({behavior:'smooth'})">↑ Ver análisis exegético de este versículo</div><div style="margin-top:0.8rem;text-align:center"><a href="tc_deep_dive_John_1_3.html" onclick="if(!this.dataset.ready){this.textContent='⏳ Generando análisis profundo...';this.style.opacity=0.6;return true;}" style="display:inline-block;padding:8px 20px;background:linear-gradient(135deg,#1a237e,#0d47a1);color:white;border-radius:8px;text-decoration:none;font-size:0.85rem;font-weight:600;box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(26,35,126,0.3)">🔍 Deep Dive — Análisis completo de esta variante</a><div style="font-size:0.7rem;color:#888;margin-top:3px">Si la página no existe, usa el tool <code>critical_apparatus</code> → <code>tc_deep_dive</code> en el MCP server para generarla.</div></div></div></div><div class="verse-block" id="vb4"><div class="verse-header"><span class="vnum">4</span></div><div class="greek-line" id="greek-4">ἐν αὐτῷ ζωὴ ἦν καὶ ἡ ζωὴ ἦν τὸ φῶς τῶν ἀνθρώπων</div><div class="uncial-line" id="uncial-4" style="display:none;font-family:'Noto Serif',serif;font-size:0.9rem;letter-spacing:1.5px;color:#5d4037;background:#efebe9;padding:6px 10px;border-radius:4px;margin-bottom:0.3rem;overflow-wrap:break-word" title="Así se vería este versículo en un manuscrito uncial del siglo IV — todo mayúsculas, sin espacios (puntos medios añadidos como ayuda visual)">ΕΝ·ΑΥΤΩΙ·ΖΩΗ·ΗΝ·ΚΑΙ·Η·ΖΩΗ·ΗΝ·ΤΟ·ΦΩΣ·ΤΩΝ·ΑΝΘΡΩΠΩΝ<span style="display:block;font-size:0.65rem;margin-top:4px;letter-spacing:0;opacity:0.7"><a href="https://www.codexsinaiticus.org/en/manuscript.aspx?book=33&chapter=1&verse=4" target="_blank" style="color:#5d4037">Ver en Codex Sinaiticus →</a></span></div><div style="display:flex;gap:0.3rem;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:0.3rem;align-items:center"><div class="rvr-btn" onclick="toggleRVR(4)">RVR60</div><div class="rvr-btn" onclick="document.getElementById('uncial-4').style.display=document.getElementById('uncial-4').style.display==='none'?'':'none'" style="background:#efebe9;color:#5d4037;border-color:#bcaaa4" title="Escritura uncial: así se veía el NT en los manuscritos del s.IV (Vaticanus, Sinaiticus). Todo mayúsculas, sin espacios entre palabras (scriptio continua), sin acentos ni puntuación. La iota subscrita se escribe explícitamente.">Uncial</div><div class="rvr-btn" onclick="document.getElementById('pron-4').style.display=document.getElementById('pron-4').style.display==='none'?'':'none'" style="background:#e3f2fd;color:#1565c0;border-color:#90caf9" title="Pronunciación bizantina aproximada (griego moderno/eclesiástico)">Pronunciación</div><div class="rvr-btn vbtn-patr" onclick="toggleSection('patr-4')" title="28 citas patrísticas disponibles">✝ 28 patrística</div><div class="rvr-btn vbtn-exeg" onclick="toggleSection('exeg-4')" title="4 comentarios exegéticos">📚 4 exégesis</div><div class="rvr-btn vbtn-ver" onclick="document.getElementById('vers-4').classList.toggle('collapsed')" title="9 versiones disponibles">📖 versiones</div><div class="rvr-btn" onclick="document.getElementById('xrefs-4').classList.toggle('collapsed')" title="8 referencias cruzadas">🔗 8 refs</div><div class="rvr-btn" onclick="copyVerse(4)" title="Copiar versículo al portapapeles" style="background:#f3e5f5;border-color:#ce93d8;color:#6a1b9a">📋</div><div class="rvr-btn" onclick="document.getElementById('notes-4').style.display=document.getElementById('notes-4').style.display==='none'?'':'none'" title="Notas personales" style="background:#fff8e1;border-color:#ffd54f;color:#f57f17">📝</div></div><div id="rvr-4" class="collapsed spanish-line">En él estaba la vida, y la vida era la luz de los hombres.</div><div id="vers-4" class="collapsed" style="margin-bottom:0.5rem"><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">RVR60</span>En él estaba la vida, y la vida era la luz de los hombres.</div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">RVR1909</span>En él estaba la vida, y la vida era la luz de los hombres.</div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">KJV</span>In him was life; and the life was the light of men.</div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">ASV</span>In him was life; and the life was the light of men. </div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">BSB</span>In Him was life, and that life was the light of men. </div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">Darby</span>In him was life, and the life was the light of men. </div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">LITV</span>In Him was life, and the life was the light of men;   </div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">YLT</span>In him was life, and the life was the light of men,</div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">Vulgate</span>in ipso vita erat et vita erat lux hominum</div></div><div id="xrefs-4" class="collapsed xref-container" style="margin-bottom:0.5rem"><span class="xref-pill" onclick="showXrefPopup(this)" data-ref="1 John 1:2" data-es="(Porque la vida fué manifestada, y vimos, y testificamos, y os anunciamos aquella vida eterna, la cual estaba con el Padre, y nos ha aparecido;)" data-gr="καὶ ἡ ζωὴ ἐφανερώθη, καὶ ἑωράκαμεν καὶ μαρτυροῦμεν καὶ ἀπαγγέλλομεν ὑμῖν τὴν ζωὴν τὴν αἰώνιον ἥτις ἦν πρὸς τὸν πατέρα καὶ ἐφανερώθη ἡμῖν—" data-lxx="" title="(Porque la vida fué manifestada, y vimos, y testificamos, y os anunciamos aquell…">1 John 1:2</span><span class="xref-pill" onclick="showXrefPopup(this)" data-ref="1 John 1:5" data-es="Y este es el mensaje que oímos de él, y os anunciamos: Que Dios es luz, y en él no hay ningunas tinieblas." data-gr="Καὶ ἔστιν αὕτη ἡ ἀγγελία ἣν ἀκηκόαμεν ἀπ’ αὐτοῦ καὶ ἀναγγέλλομεν ὑμῖν, ὅτι ὁ θεὸς φῶς ἐστιν καὶ σκοτία ⸂ἐν αὐτῷ οὐκ ἔστιν⸃ οὐδεμία." data-lxx="" title="Y este es el mensaje que oímos de él, y os anunciamos: Que Dios es luz, y en él …">1 John 1:5</span><span class="xref-pill" onclick="showXrefPopup(this)" data-ref="1 John 1:7" data-es="Mas si andamos en luz, como él está en luz, tenemos comunión entre nosotros, y la sangre de Jesucristo su Hijo nos limpia de todo pecado." data-gr="ἐὰν δὲ ἐν τῷ φωτὶ περιπατῶμεν ὡς αὐτός ἐστιν ἐν τῷ φωτί, κοινωνίαν ἔχομεν μετ’ ἀλλήλων καὶ τὸ αἷμα ⸀Ἰησοῦ τοῦ υἱοῦ αὐτοῦ καθαρίζει ἡμᾶς ἀπὸ πάσης ἁμαρτίας." data-lxx="" title="Mas si andamos en luz, como él está en luz, tenemos comunión entre nosotros, y l…">1 John 1:7</span><span class="xref-pill" onclick="showXrefPopup(this)" data-ref="1 John 5:11" data-es="Y este es el testimonio: Que Dios nos ha dado vida eterna; y esta vida está en su Hijo." data-gr="καὶ αὕτη ἐστὶν ἡ μαρτυρία, ὅτι ζωὴν αἰώνιον ἔδωκεν ⸂ὁ θεὸς ἡμῖν⸃, καὶ αὕτη ἡ ζωὴ ἐν τῷ υἱῷ αὐτοῦ ἐστιν." data-lxx="" title="Y este es el testimonio: Que Dios nos ha dado vida eterna; y esta vida está en s…">1 John 5:11</span><span class="xref-pill" onclick="showXrefPopup(this)" data-ref="Acts 26:23" data-es="Que Cristo había de padecer, y ser el primero de la resurrección de los muertos, para anunciar luz al pueblo y á los Gentiles." data-gr="εἰ παθητὸς ὁ χριστός, εἰ πρῶτος ἐξ ἀναστάσεως νεκρῶν φῶς μέλλει καταγγέλλειν τῷ ⸀τε λαῷ καὶ τοῖς ἔθνεσιν." data-lxx="" title="Que Cristo había de padecer, y ser el primero de la resurrección de los muertos,…">Acts 26:23</span><span class="xref-pill" onclick="showXrefPopup(this)" data-ref="Colossians 3:4" data-es="Cuando Cristo, vuestra vida, se manifestare, entonces vosotros también seréis manifestados con él en gloria." data-gr="ὅταν ὁ Χριστὸς φανερωθῇ, ἡ ζωὴ ⸀ὑμῶν, τότε καὶ ὑμεῖς σὺν αὐτῷ φανερωθήσεσθε ἐν δόξῃ." data-lxx="" title="Cuando Cristo, vuestra vida, se manifestare, entonces vosotros también seréis ma…">Colossians 3:4</span><span class="xref-pill" onclick="showXrefPopup(this)" data-ref="Ephesians 5:14" data-es="Por lo cual dice: Despiértate, tú que duermes, y levántate de los muertos, y te alumbrará Cristo." data-gr="πᾶν γὰρ τὸ φανερούμενον φῶς ἐστιν. διὸ λέγει· Ἔγειρε, ὁ καθεύδων, καὶ ἀνάστα ἐκ τῶν νεκρῶν, καὶ ἐπιφαύσει σοι ὁ Χριστός." data-lxx="" title="Por lo cual dice: Despiértate, tú que duermes, y levántate de los muertos, y te …">Ephesians 5:14</span><span class="xref-pill" onclick="showXrefPopup(this)" data-ref="Isaiah 42:16" data-es="Y guiaré los ciegos por camino que no sabían, haréles pisar por las sendas que no habían conocido; delante de ellos tornaré las tinieblas en luz, y los rodeos en llanura. Estas cosas les haré, y no los desampararé." data-gr="וְ/הוֹלַכְתִּ֣י עִוְרִ֗ים בְּ/דֶ֨רֶךְ֙ לֹ֣א יָדָ֔עוּ בִּ/נְתִיב֥וֹת לֹֽא יָדְע֖וּ אַדְרִיכֵ֑/ם אָשִׂים֩ מַחְשָׁ֨ךְ לִ/פְנֵי/הֶ֜ם לָ/א֗וֹר וּ/מַֽעֲקַשִּׁים֙ לְ/מִישׁ֔וֹר אֵ֚לֶּה הַ/דְּבָרִ֔ים עֲשִׂיתִ֖/ם וְ/לֹ֥א עֲזַבְתִּֽי/ם" data-lxx="καὶ ἄξω τυφλοὺς ἐν ὁδῷ ᾗ οὐκ ἔγνωσαν καὶ τρίβους οὓς οὐκ ᾔδεισαν πατῆσαι ποιήσω αὐτούς ποιήσω αὐτοῖς τὸ σκότος εἰς φῶς καὶ τὰ σκολιὰ εἰς εὐθεῖαν ταῦτα τὰ ῥήματα ποιήσω καὶ οὐκ ἐγκαταλείψω αὐτούς" title="Y guiaré los ciegos por camino que no sabían, haréles pisar por las sendas que n…">Isaiah 42:16</span></div><div id="pron-4" style="display:none;font-size:0.85rem;color:#1565c0;background:#e3f2fd;padding:4px 8px;border-radius:4px;margin-bottom:0.3rem;font-style:italic" class="pron-line"></div><div id="notes-4" style="display:none;margin-bottom:0.5rem"><textarea id="note-input-4" style="width:100%;min-height:50px;border:1px solid #ccc;border-radius:6px;padding:4px 8px;font-size:0.82rem;resize:vertical" placeholder="Tus notas para v.4..." oninput="saveNote(4)"></textarea></div><div class="section-toggle" onclick="toggleSection('exeg-4')" role="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="exeg-4" tabindex="0"><span class="section-icon">📜</span> Exégesis del Griego <span class="arrow" id="arrow-exeg-4">▶</span></div><div id="exeg-4" class="collapsed section-content exeg-section" role="region"><div class="exeg-summary"><strong style="color:#1b5e20">Alford:</strong> ἀνθ ] This is not to be understood of the teaching of the Incarnate Logos , but of the enlightening and life-sustaining influence of the eternal Son of God , in Whom was life.<br><strong style="color:#1b5e20">Bengel:</strong> In relation to the several particulars, ὁ λογος , the Speech [Sermo], has the signification suited to each.<br><strong style="color:#1b5e20">Robertson:</strong> Usually in John ζωη means spiritual life, but here the term is unlimited and includes all life; only it is not βιος (manner of life), but the very principle or essence of life.<br><strong style="color:#1b5e20">Vincent:</strong> In Him was life [ εν αυτω ζωη ην].<br></div><div class="comm-item"><div class="comm-header" onclick="document.getElementById('uexv4_0').classList.toggle('collapsed')"><strong>Alford's Greek Testament</strong> <span style=color:#888;font-size:0.75rem>(1863)</span><span class="small-arrow">ver completo ▼</span></div><div id="uexv4_0" class="collapsed comm-body">4. ἐν αὐτῷ ζωὴ ἦν ] Compare [1 John 5:11] ; [1 John 1:1-2] , and ch. [John 6:33] . ζωή is not merely &lsquo; spiritual life ,&rsquo; nor &lsquo; the recovery of blessedness ,&rsquo; as Tholuck, Kuinoel, &c. explain it: the λόγος is the source of all life to the creature, not indeed ultimately, but mediately (see ch. [John 5:26] : 1Jn 5:11 ). κ . ἡ ζωὴ ἦν τ . φῶς τ . ἀνθ ] This is not to be understood of the teaching of the Incarnate Logos , but of the enlightening and life-sustaining influence of the eternal Son of God , in Whom was life. In the material world, light, the offspring of the Word of God, is the condition of life, and without it life degenerates and expires: so also in the spiritual world that life which is in Him, is to the creature the very condition of all development and furtherance of the life of the spirit. All knowledge, all purity, all love, all happiness, spring up and grow from this life, which is the light to them all. It is not φῶς , but τὸ φῶς : because this is the only true light: see [John 1:9] , also [1 John 1:5] .</div></div><div class="comm-item"><div class="comm-header" onclick="document.getElementById('uexv4_1').classList.toggle('collapsed')"><strong>Bengel's Gnomon</strong> <span style=color:#888;font-size:0.75rem>(1742)</span><span class="small-arrow">ver completo ▼</span></div><div id="uexv4_1" class="collapsed comm-body">[John 1:4] . Ἐν , in ) First, John says, In Him was life : (comp. ch. [John 5:26] , &ldquo;For as the Father hath life in Himself, so hath He given to the Son to have life in Himself&rdquo;). Then he calls Him the Life . So in [1 John 1:1-2] , first he calls Him the Word of Life , then the Life ; and in the same chapter, [John 1:5] ; [John 1:7] , God is said to be Light , and to be in the light . John especially imitates the expressions of the Lord Jesus. [12] ΖΩΉ , life ) After the consideration of being [esse], the next consideration is as to living [vivere]. Then [the result of life entering the world] there is no death, there is then no nature devoid of grace. καὶ ἡ ζωή , and the Life ) The Subject: the Life, bestowing life on all things, which were alive. ἦν τὸ φῶς , was the Light ) Light and Life together: ch. [John 8:12] , &ldquo;He that followeth Me shall not walk in darkness, but shall have the light of life : [1 Timothy 6:16] , &ldquo;Who only hath immortality , dwelling in the light , which no man can approach unto:&rdquo; [Philippians 2:15-16] , &ldquo;Ye shine as lights in the world, holding forth the word of life .&rdquo; As on the opposite side, צלמות , Darkness and death . Quickening is, however, prior to illumination. ΤῶΝ ἈΝΘΡΏΠΩΝ , of men ) Of all men in the state of innocency, from which there ought not to be separated the consideration as to the Logos. [13] Men : nowhere is this expression used for Adam and his wife ; so it denotes mankind. The evangelist here is come from the whole to the part from those things which were made, or which were alive, to rational beings. In relation to the several particulars, ὁ λογος , the Speech [Sermo], has the signification suited to each. [12] [John 8:12] . That which thus harmonizes with the intimate relation between the beloved disciple and Jesus, is made a ground of cavil by Rationalists; viz. that elsewhere John puts into Jesus&rsquo; mouth a phraseology which is not Jesus&rsquo; but his own. E. [13] Or, of man in his ideal . E.</div></div><div class="comm-item"><div class="comm-header" onclick="document.getElementById('uexv4_2').classList.toggle('collapsed')"><strong>Robertson's Word Pictures</strong> <span style=color:#888;font-size:0.75rem>(1933)</span><span class="small-arrow">ver completo ▼</span></div><div id="uexv4_2" class="collapsed comm-body">In him was life ( εν αυτω ζωη ην ). That which has come into being (verse [John 1:3]) in the Logos was life. The power that creates and sustains life in the universe is the Logos. This is what Paul means by the perfect passive verb εκτιστα (stands created) in [Colossians 1:16]. This is also the claim of Jesus to Martha ([John 11:25]). This is the idea in [Hebrews 1:3] "bearing (upholding) the all things by the word of his power." Once this language might have been termed unscientific, but not so now after the spiritual interpretation of the physical world by Eddington and Jeans. Usually in John ζωη means spiritual life, but here the term is unlimited and includes all life; only it is not βιος (manner of life), but the very principle or essence of life. That is spiritual behind the physical and to this great scientists today agree. It is also personal intelligence and power. Some of the western documents have εστιν here instead of ην to bring out clearly the timelessness of this phrase of the work of the Λογος . And the life was the light of men ( κα η ζωη ην το φως των ανθρωπων ). Here the article with both ζωη and φως makes them interchangeable. "The light was the life of men" is also true. That statement is curiously like the view of some physicists who find in electricity (both light and power) the nearest equivalent to life in its ultimate physical form. Later Jesus will call himself the light of the world ([John 8:12]). John is fond of these words life and light in Gospel, Epistles, Revelation. He here combines them to picture his conception of the Pre-incarnate Logos in his relation to the race. He was and is the Life of men ( των ανθρωπον , generic use of the article) and the Light of men. John asserts this relation of the Logos to the race of men in particular before the Incarnation.</div></div><div class="comm-item"><div class="comm-header" onclick="document.getElementById('uexv4_3').classList.toggle('collapsed')"><strong>Vincent's Word Studies</strong> <span style=color:#888;font-size:0.75rem>(1887)</span><span class="small-arrow">ver completo ▼</span></div><div id="uexv4_3" class="collapsed comm-body">In Him was life [ εν αυτω ζωη ην] . He was the fountain of life - physical, moral, and eternal - its principle and source. Two words for life are employed in the New Testament : biov and zwh. The primary distinction is that zwh means existence as contrasted with death, and biov, the period, means, or manner of existence. Hence biov is originally the higher word, being used of men, while zwh is used of animals [ ζωα] . We speak therefore of the discussion of the life and habits of animals as zoology; and of accounts of men's lives as biography. Animals have the vital principle in common with men, but men lead lives controlled by intellect and will, and directed to moral and intellectual ends. In the New Testament, biov means either living, i e., means of subsistence ([Mark 12:44]; [Luke 8:43]), or course of life, life regarded as an economy ([Luke 8:14]; [1 Timothy 2:2]; [2 Timothy 2:4]). Zwh occurs in the lower sense of life, considered principally or wholly as existence ([1 Peter 3:10]; [Acts 8:33]; xvi</div></div><div class="themes-divider">🔍 Temas discutidos</div><div class="theme-card"><div class="theme-word">ζωή</div><div class="consensus-bar"><div style="width:55%;background:#ff9800"></div></div><div class="opinion" style="border-left-color:#4caf50">✅ <strong>Alford</strong> ζωή no es meramente 'vida espiritual' sino que el λόγος es fuente de toda vida para la criatura, mediadamente.</div><div class="opinion" style="border-left-color:#ff9800">⚠️ <strong>Robertson</strong> Usualmente en Juan ζωή significa vida espiritual, pero aquí el término es ilimitado e incluye toda vida.</div><div class="opinion" style="border-left-color:#4caf50">✅ <strong>Vincent</strong> Él era la fuente de vida - física, moral y eterna - su principio y fuente, distinguiendo ζωή de βίος.</div><div class="theme-summary">Consenso moderado: ζωή abarca toda vida, no solo espiritual, con Robertson notando que esto difiere del uso típico joánico.</div></div></div><div class="section-toggle" onclick="toggleSection('patr-4')" role="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="patr-4" tabindex="0"><span class="section-icon">👨‍🏫</span> Patrística · 6 temas · 28 citas <span class="arrow" id="arrow-patr-4">▶</span></div><div id="patr-4" class="collapsed section-content patr-section" role="region"><div class="patr-theme"><div class="patr-theme-header" onclick="document.getElementById('pte674ac38').classList.toggle('collapsed');this.querySelector('.arrow').classList.toggle('open')"><strong>Cristo como Vida Universal</strong><div class="consensus-bar" style="width:60px"><div style="width:85%;background:#4caf50"></div></div><span class="consensus-label" style="color:#4caf50">alto</span><span class="arrow">▼</span></div><div id="pte674ac38" class="collapsed" style="padding:0.5rem 0.2rem 0"><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>Augustine of Hippo</strong> (s.IV-V (354-430)) <span class="patr-meta">— Obispo de Hipona, Doctor de la Iglesia, discípulo de Ambrosio de Milán (Hipona)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agust%C3%ADn_de_Hipona" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.4]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"All things, then, brethren, all things were made by Him, and without Him was nothing made. But how were all things made by Him? That, which was made, in Him is life."</div></div><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> (s.V (c.376-444)) <span class="patr-meta">— Patriarca de Alejandría, Concilio de Éfeso (Alejandría)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cirilo_de_Alejandr%C3%ADa" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.4]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"That the Son is by Nature Life and therefore not originate, but of the Essence of God the Father. That which was made, in it was Life."</div></div><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>John Chrysostom</strong> (s.IV (c.349-407)) <span class="patr-meta">— Patriarca de Constantinopla, "Boca de Oro", discípulo de Diodoro de Tarso (Antioquía/Constantinopla)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Juan_Cris%C3%B3stomo" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.4]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"John having spoken of the work of creation, that All things were made by Him, and without Him was not anything made that was made, goes on to speak concerning His Providence, where he saith, In Him was Life."</div></div><div class="patr-resumen">Los Padres concuerdan en que Cristo es la fuente de vida para toda la creación. Esta vida no es meramente biológica sino ontológica y providencial.</div></div></div><div class="patr-theme"><div class="patr-theme-header" onclick="document.getElementById('pt46fb9012').classList.toggle('collapsed');this.querySelector('.arrow').classList.toggle('open')"><strong>Cristo como Luz de los Hombres</strong><div class="consensus-bar" style="width:60px"><div style="width:85%;background:#4caf50"></div></div><span class="consensus-label" style="color:#4caf50">alto</span><span class="arrow">▼</span></div><div id="pt46fb9012" class="collapsed" style="padding:0.5rem 0.2rem 0"><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>Augustine of Hippo</strong> (s.IV-V (354-430)) <span class="patr-meta">— Obispo de Hipona, Doctor de la Iglesia, discípulo de Ambrosio de Milán (Hipona)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agust%C3%ADn_de_Hipona" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.4]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"For this follows: and the life was the light of men; and from this very life are men illuminated. Cattle are not illuminated, because cattle have not rational minds capable of seeing wisdom. But man was made in the image of God, and has a rational mind, by which he can perceive wisdom."</div></div><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>John Chrysostom</strong> (s.IV (c.349-407)) <span class="patr-meta">— Patriarca de Constantinopla, "Boca de Oro", discípulo de Diodoro de Tarso (Antioquía/Constantinopla)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Juan_Cris%C3%B3stomo" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.4]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"First then, the Evangelist hath instructed us respecting the creation, after that he tells us of the goods relating to the soul which He supplied to us by His coming; and these he has darkly described in one sentence, when he says, And the Life was the Light of men. He does not say, was the light of the Jews, but universally of men."</div></div><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>Origen of Alexandria</strong> (s.III (c.185-254)) <span class="patr-meta">— Maestro catequético, exégeta, discípulo de Clemente de Alejandría (Alejandría/Cesarea)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Or%C3%ADgenes" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.4]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"Our Savior is said to be some things not for himself but for others; others again, both for himself and others. When it is said then, That which was made in him was life we must inquire whether the life is for himself and others or for others only; and if for others, for whom? Now the Life and the Light are both the same person: he is the light of men [humanity]; he is therefore their life."</div></div><div class="patr-resumen">Existe consenso en que Cristo es específicamente la luz de los seres humanos racionales, no de los animales. Esta iluminación se relaciona con la capacidad racional del hombre creado a imagen de Dios.</div></div></div><div class="patr-theme"><div class="patr-theme-header" onclick="document.getElementById('pte52ea7c0').classList.toggle('collapsed');this.querySelector('.arrow').classList.toggle('open')"><strong>Puntuación y Lectura del Texto</strong><div class="consensus-bar" style="width:60px"><div style="width:55%;background:#ff9800"></div></div><span class="consensus-label" style="color:#ff9800">medio</span><span class="arrow">▼</span></div><div id="pte52ea7c0" class="collapsed" style="padding:0.5rem 0.2rem 0"><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>Jerome</strong> (s.IV-V (c.347-420)) <span class="patr-meta">— Traductor de la Vulgata, Doctor de la Iglesia, discípulo de Gregorio de Nacianzo (Belén)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jer%C3%B3nimo_de_Estrid%C3%B3n" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.4]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"Many read this inaccurately because they add without any punctuation, that which has been made in him was life. The correct statement is All things were made through him, and without him was made nothing that has been made, meaning that that which has been made without him has not been made."</div></div><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#f44336"><div class="patr-cite-header">❌ <strong>John Chrysostom</strong> (s.IV (c.349-407)) <span class="patr-meta">— Patriarca de Constantinopla, "Boca de Oro", discípulo de Diodoro de Tarso (Antioquía/Constantinopla)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Juan_Cris%C3%B3stomo" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.4]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"We will not put the stop at without Him was not any thing made, as the heretics do. For they wishing to prove the Holy Ghost a creature, read, That which was made in Him, was life. But this cannot be so understood."</div></div><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#f44336"><div class="patr-cite-header">❌ <strong>Thomas Aquinas</strong> (s.XIII (1225-1274)) <span class="patr-meta">— Teólogo escolástico, Doctor de la Iglesia, discípulo de Alberto Magno (Italia)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tom%C3%A1s_de_Aquino" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.4]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"Then he says, What was made in him was life; and this is the third clause. Here we must avoid the false interpretation of Manichaeus, who was led by this to maintain that everything that exists is alive: for example, stones, wood, men, and anything else in the world."</div></div><div class="patr-resumen">Existe debate sobre la puntuación correcta del texto, con implicaciones teológicas importantes. Algunos Padres advierten contra lecturas heréticas que surgen de puntuaciones incorrectas.</div></div></div><div class="patr-theme"><div class="patr-theme-header" onclick="document.getElementById('pte0971fb6').classList.toggle('collapsed');this.querySelector('.arrow').classList.toggle('open')"><strong>Salvación y Seguimiento de Cristo</strong><div class="consensus-bar" style="width:60px"><div style="width:85%;background:#4caf50"></div></div><span class="consensus-label" style="color:#4caf50">alto</span><span class="arrow">▼</span></div><div id="pte0971fb6" class="collapsed" style="padding:0.5rem 0.2rem 0"><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>Clement of Alexandria</strong> (s.II-III (c.150-215)) <span class="patr-meta">— Maestro catequético, discípulo de Panteno (Alejandría)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clemente_de_Alejandr%C3%ADa" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.4]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"Further release from evils is the beginning of salvation. We then alone, who first have touched the confines of life, are already perfect; and we already live who are separated from death. Salvation, accordingly, is the following of Christ: For that which is in Him is life."</div></div><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>Clement of Alexandria</strong> (s.II-III (c.150-215)) <span class="patr-meta">— Maestro catequético, discípulo de Panteno (Alejandría)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clemente_de_Alejandr%C3%ADa" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.4]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"He, then, who denies the Saviour, denies life; for the light was life. He does not term those men of little faith, but faithless and hypocrites, who have the name inscribed on them, but deny that they are really believers."</div></div><div class="patr-resumen">Los Padres enseñan que la salvación consiste en seguir a Cristo como fuente de vida. Negar al Salvador equivale a negar la vida misma.</div></div></div><div class="patr-theme"><div class="patr-theme-header" onclick="document.getElementById('pt88dada59').classList.toggle('collapsed');this.querySelector('.arrow').classList.toggle('open')"><strong>Analogía del Artesano y la Creación</strong><div class="consensus-bar" style="width:60px"><div style="width:55%;background:#ff9800"></div></div><span class="consensus-label" style="color:#ff9800">medio</span><span class="arrow">▼</span></div><div id="pt88dada59" class="collapsed" style="padding:0.5rem 0.2rem 0"><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>Augustine of Hippo</strong> (s.IV-V (354-430)) <span class="patr-meta">— Obispo de Hipona, Doctor de la Iglesia, discípulo de Ambrosio de Milán (Hipona)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agust%C3%ADn_de_Hipona" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.4]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"As far as I can, I shall explain my meaning to you, beloved. A carpenter makes a box. First he has the box in design; for if he had it not in design, how could he produce it by workmanship? But the box in theory is not the very box as it appears to the eyes. It exists invisibly in design, it will be visible in the work."</div></div><div class="patr-resumen">Agustín usa la analogía del artesano para explicar cómo todas las cosas fueron hechas en Cristo como diseño divino antes de su manifestación visible.</div></div></div><div class="patr-theme"><div class="patr-theme-header" onclick="document.getElementById('ptadec3979').classList.toggle('collapsed');this.querySelector('.arrow').classList.toggle('open')"><strong>Providencia Divina y Sustentación</strong><div class="consensus-bar" style="width:60px"><div style="width:85%;background:#4caf50"></div></div><span class="consensus-label" style="color:#4caf50">alto</span><span class="arrow">▼</span></div><div id="ptadec3979" class="collapsed" style="padding:0.5rem 0.2rem 0"><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>John Chrysostom</strong> (s.IV (c.349-407)) <span class="patr-meta">— Patriarca de Constantinopla, "Boca de Oro", discípulo de Diodoro de Tarso (Antioquía/Constantinopla)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Juan_Cris%C3%B3stomo" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.4]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"That no one may doubt how so many and so great things were made by Him, he adds, that In Him was Life. For as with the fountain which is the mother of the great deeps, however much you take away you nothing lessen the fountain; so with the energy of the Only-Begotten, however much you take away, you do not lessen the energy."</div></div><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>John Chrysostom</strong> (s.IV (c.349-407)) <span class="patr-meta">— Patriarca de Constantinopla, "Boca de Oro", discípulo de Diodoro de Tarso (Antioquía/Constantinopla)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Juan_Cris%C3%B3stomo" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.4]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"Nay, if need were that ten thousand, or even an infinite number of such worlds be created, He remains the same, sufficient for them all not merely to produce, but also to control them after their creation. For the word Life here refers not merely to the act of creation, but also to the providence (engaged) about the permanence of the things created."</div></div><div class="patr-resumen">Los Padres enseñan que Cristo no solo creó todas las cosas sino que las sustenta continuamente mediante su providencia. Su poder creativo es inagotable como una fuente.</div></div></div></div><div class="section-toggle" onclick="toggleSection('tc-4')" role="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="tc-4" tabindex="0"><span class="section-icon">⚖️</span> Crítica Textual <span style="font-size:0.7rem;color:#ff9800">(3 lecturas)</span> <span class="arrow" id="arrow-tc-4">▶</span></div><div id="tc-4" class="collapsed section-content tc-section"><div class="tc-verdict" style="margin-bottom:0.8rem"><div class="tc-verdict-text"><strong>Veredicto:</strong> La lectura #2.1 (ἦν) es probablemente original, con amplio apoyo manuscrito incluyendo P66, P75, A, B, C, L y la mayoría de manuscritos. La lectura #2.2 (ἐστιν) representa una actualización del tiempo verbal al presente, posiblemente para enfatizar la naturaleza eterna y presente de la vida en el Logos. La lectura #2.3 que omite completamente la frase es claramente secundaria y probablemente resultado de error escribal.</div><div class="consensus-bar" style="max-width:200px"><div style="width:90%;background:#4caf50"></div></div><span style="font-size:0.72rem;color:#4caf50;font-weight:600;margin-left:4px">Confianza: alta</span><div class="tc-impact"><strong>Impacto:</strong> El cambio de ἦν (era) a ἐστιν (es) tiene implicaciones teológicas sobre la naturaleza temporal de la vida en el Logos. El imperfecto ἦν mantiene la consistencia narrativa con los versículos anteriores, mientras que ἐστιν enfatiza la realidad presente y eterna.</div></div><div style="overflow-x:auto;margin-bottom:0.5rem"><table class="tc-table"><tr class="tc-header"><th class="tc-ms-col">MS</th><th class="tc-ctx">αὐτῷ</th><th class="tc-ctx">ζωὴ</th><th class="tc-var-col" title="Columna variante 1: ἦν">⚡1</th><th class="tc-ctx">καὶ</th><th class="tc-ctx">ἡ</th></tr><tr><td class="tc-ms-cell" onclick="showMSSPanel('𝔓66, 75 A B C L Δ Θ Ψ 050 0141 0234 f1 f13 28 33 180 205 565 579 597 700 892 1006 1010 1071 1241 1243 1292 1342 1424 1505 Byz [E F G H] Lect vg syrp, h, pal copbo arm geo slav Diatessaronarm Irenaeuslatmss Clementfrom Theodotus Clement3/5 Origengr, lat 1/2 Eusebius Didymusdub Macarius/Symeon Epiphanius Chrysostom Cyril Hesychius Theodoret; Victorinus-Rome1/5 Jerome Augustine12/22')"><span class="ms-chip" style="background:#b71c1c;color:white;font-weight:700" data-s="𝔓66" onclick="event.stopPropagation();showMSInfo(this,'𝔓66');">𝔓66</span><span class="ms-chip" style="background:#e3f2fd;color:#1565c0" data-s="75" onclick="event.stopPropagation();showMSInfo(this,'75');">75</span><span class="m