tizing, and the purport of the thing itself, was attracti...&rdquo;" data-s="Chrysostom" onclick="event.stopPropagation();showMSInfo(this,'Chrysostom');">Chrysostom</span><span class="ms-chip" style="background:#fff3e0;color:#e65100" title=": &ldquo;But he says that these things were done in Bethabara beyond Jordan, putting this too as a sign of accurate and careful narration. For we are all accus...&rdquo;" data-s="Cyril" onclick="event.stopPropagation();showMSInfo(this,'Cyril');">Cyril</span></td><td class="tc-word-cell tc-interactive" style="background:#f5f5f5" data-tip="Estas (cosas)" data-lemma="οὗτος" data-morph="D-NPN" data-strongs="G3778">Ταῦτα</td><td class="tc-word-cell tc-interactive" style="background:#ffe0b2" data-tip="en" data-lemma="ἐν" data-morph="PREP" data-strongs="G1722">ἐν</td><td class="tc-word-cell tc-interactive" style="background:#ffe0b2" data-tip="" data-lemma="" data-morph="" data-strongs="">Βηθαβαρᾷ</td><td class="tc-word-cell tc-interactive" style="background:#ffe0b2" data-tip="llegó a ser" data-lemma="γίνομαι" data-morph="V-2ADI-3S" data-strongs="G1096">ἐγένετο</td><td class="tc-word-cell tc-interactive" style="background:#f5f5f5" data-tip="al otro lado de" data-lemma="πέραν" data-morph="PREP" data-strongs="G4008">πέραν</td><td class="tc-word-cell tc-interactive" style="background:#f5f5f5" data-tip="el" data-lemma="ὁ" data-morph="T-GSM" data-strongs="G3588">τοῦ</td></tr></table></div><div style="font-size:0.72rem;color:#555;margin:0.3rem 0;padding:0.3rem 0.5rem;background:#f5f5f5;border-radius:4px"><strong>Lecturas:</strong> <span style="margin-right:0.8rem">#1: <span style="font-family:serif;color:#1b5e20">ἐν Βηθανίᾳ ἐγένετο</span></span><span style="margin-right:0.8rem">#2: <span style="font-family:serif;color:#1b5e20">ἐν Βηθαβαρᾷ ἐγένετο</span></span></div><div style="font-size:0.63rem;color:#999;margin-top:2px;display:flex;gap:0.5rem;flex-wrap:wrap"><span style="font-weight:600">MSS:</span> <span>🔴 Papiros</span> <span>🔵 Unciales</span> <span>⚪ Minúsc.</span> <span>🟣 Versiones</span> <span>🟠 Padres</span> <span>🟢 Byz/Lect</span></div><div class="tc-criteria-expanded"><div class="tc-criterion-item"><strong>Atestación temprana</strong><div class="tc-criterion-exp">Los manuscritos más antiguos apoyan consistentemente Βηθανίᾳ. La variante Βηθαβαρᾷ aparece en manuscritos posteriores influenciados por la corrección de Orígenes.</div></div><div class="tc-criterion-item"><strong>Anti-armonización</strong><div class="tc-criterion-exp">Betania es la lectura más difícil geográficamente, ya que la Betania conocida estaba cerca de Jerusalén. Betábara representa un intento de corrección geográfica para armonizar con el conocimiento del área del Jordán.</div></div></div><div style="font-size:0.72rem;color:#1565c0;margin-top:0.5rem;cursor:pointer" onclick="document.getElementById('exeg-28')?.classList.remove('collapsed');document.getElementById('exeg-28')?.scrollIntoView({behavior:'smooth'})">↑ Ver análisis exegético de este versículo</div><div style="margin-top:0.8rem;text-align:center"><a href="tc_deep_dive_John_1_28.html" onclick="if(!this.dataset.ready){this.textContent='⏳ Generando análisis profundo...';this.style.opacity=0.6;return true;}" style="display:inline-block;padding:8px 20px;background:linear-gradient(135deg,#1a237e,#0d47a1);color:white;border-radius:8px;text-decoration:none;font-size:0.85rem;font-weight:600;box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(26,35,126,0.3)">🔍 Deep Dive — Análisis completo de esta variante</a><div style="font-size:0.7rem;color:#888;margin-top:3px">Si la página no existe, usa el tool <code>critical_apparatus</code> → <code>tc_deep_dive</code> en el MCP server para generarla.</div></div></div></div><div class="verse-block" id="vb29"><div class="verse-header"><span class="vnum">29</span></div><div class="greek-line" id="greek-29">Τῇ ἐπαύριον βλέπει τὸν Ἰησοῦν ἐρχόμενον πρὸς αὐτόν καὶ λέγει Ἴδε ὁ Ἀμνὸς τοῦ Θεοῦ ὁ αἴρων τὴν ἁμαρτίαν τοῦ κόσμου</div><div class="uncial-line" id="uncial-29" style="display:none;font-family:'Noto Serif',serif;font-size:0.9rem;letter-spacing:1.5px;color:#5d4037;background:#efebe9;padding:6px 10px;border-radius:4px;margin-bottom:0.3rem;overflow-wrap:break-word" title="Así se vería este versículo en un manuscrito uncial del siglo IV — todo mayúsculas, sin espacios (puntos medios añadidos como ayuda visual)">ΤΗΙ·ΕΠΑΥΡΙΟΝ·ΒΛΕΠΕΙ·ΤΟΝ·ΙΗΣΟΥΝ·ΕΡΧΟΜΕΝΟΝ·ΠΡΟΣ·ΑΥΤΟΝ·ΚΑΙ·ΛΕΓΕΙ·ΙΔΕ·Ο·ΑΜΝΟΣ·ΤΟΥ·ΘΕΟΥ·Ο·ΑΙΡΩΝ·ΤΗΝ·ΑΜΑΡΤΙΑΝ·ΤΟΥ·ΚΟΣΜΟΥ<span style="display:block;font-size:0.65rem;margin-top:4px;letter-spacing:0;opacity:0.7"><a href="https://www.codexsinaiticus.org/en/manuscript.aspx?book=33&chapter=1&verse=29" target="_blank" style="color:#5d4037">Ver en Codex Sinaiticus →</a></span></div><div style="display:flex;gap:0.3rem;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:0.3rem;align-items:center"><div class="rvr-btn" onclick="toggleRVR(29)">RVR60</div><div class="rvr-btn" onclick="document.getElementById('uncial-29').style.display=document.getElementById('uncial-29').style.display==='none'?'':'none'" style="background:#efebe9;color:#5d4037;border-color:#bcaaa4" title="Escritura uncial: así se veía el NT en los manuscritos del s.IV (Vaticanus, Sinaiticus). Todo mayúsculas, sin espacios entre palabras (scriptio continua), sin acentos ni puntuación. La iota subscrita se escribe explícitamente.">Uncial</div><div class="rvr-btn" onclick="document.getElementById('pron-29').style.display=document.getElementById('pron-29').style.display==='none'?'':'none'" style="background:#e3f2fd;color:#1565c0;border-color:#90caf9" title="Pronunciación bizantina aproximada (griego moderno/eclesiástico)">Pronunciación</div><div class="rvr-btn vbtn-patr" onclick="toggleSection('patr-29')" title="43 citas patrísticas disponibles">✝ 43 patrística</div><div class="rvr-btn vbtn-exeg" onclick="toggleSection('exeg-29')" title="3 comentarios exegéticos">📚 3 exégesis</div><div class="rvr-btn vbtn-ver" onclick="document.getElementById('vers-29').classList.toggle('collapsed')" title="9 versiones disponibles">📖 versiones</div><div class="rvr-btn" onclick="document.getElementById('xrefs-29').classList.toggle('collapsed')" title="8 referencias cruzadas">🔗 8 refs</div><div class="rvr-btn" onclick="copyVerse(29)" title="Copiar versículo al portapapeles" style="background:#f3e5f5;border-color:#ce93d8;color:#6a1b9a">📋</div><div class="rvr-btn" onclick="document.getElementById('notes-29').style.display=document.getElementById('notes-29').style.display==='none'?'':'none'" title="Notas personales" style="background:#fff8e1;border-color:#ffd54f;color:#f57f17">📝</div></div><div id="rvr-29" class="collapsed spanish-line">El siguiente día vio Juan a Jesús que venía a él, y dijo: He aquí el Cordero de Dios, que quita el pecado del mundo.</div><div id="vers-29" class="collapsed" style="margin-bottom:0.5rem"><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">RVR60</span>El siguiente día vio Juan a Jesús que venía a él, y dijo: He aquí el Cordero de Dios, que quita el pecado del mundo.</div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">RVR1909</span>El siguiente día ve Juan á Jesús que venía á él, y dice: He aquí el Cordero de Dios, que quita el pecado del mundo.</div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">KJV</span>The next day John seeth Jesus coming unto him, and saith, Behold the Lamb of God, which taketh away the sin of the world.</div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">ASV</span>On the morrow he seeth Jesus coming unto him, and saith, Behold, the Lamb of God, that taketh away the sin of the world! </div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">BSB</span>  The next day John saw Jesus coming toward him and said, “Look, the Lamb of God, who takes away the sin of the world! </div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">Darby</span>On the morrow he sees Jesus coming to him, and says, Behold the Lamb ofGod, who takes away the sin of the world. </div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">LITV</span>On the morrow, John sees Jesus coming toward him and said, Behold! The Lamb of God, taking away the sin of the world! </div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">YLT</span>on the morrow John seeth Jesus coming unto him, and saith, `Lo, the Lamb of God, who is taking away the sin of the world;</div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">Vulgate</span>altera die videt Iohannes Iesum venientem ad se et ait ecce agnus Dei qui tollit peccatum mundi</div></div><div id="xrefs-29" class="collapsed xref-container" style="margin-bottom:0.5rem"><span class="xref-pill" onclick="showXrefPopup(this)" data-ref="1 Corinthians 15:3" data-es="Porque primeramente os he enseñado lo que asimismo recibí: Que Cristo fué muerto por nuestros pecados conforme á las Escrituras;" data-gr="Παρέδωκα γὰρ ὑμῖν ἐν πρώτοις, ὃ καὶ παρέλαβον, ὅτι Χριστὸς ἀπέθανεν ὑπὲρ τῶν ἁμαρτιῶν ἡμῶν κατὰ τὰς γραφάς," data-lxx="" title="Porque primeramente os he enseñado lo que asimismo recibí: Que Cristo fué muerto…">1 Corinthians 15:3</span><span class="xref-pill" onclick="showXrefPopup(this)" data-ref="1 John 2:2" data-es="Y él es la propiciación por nuestros pecados: y no solamente por los nuestros, sino también por los de todo el mundo." data-gr="καὶ αὐτὸς ἱλασμός ἐστιν περὶ τῶν ἁμαρτιῶν ἡμῶν, οὐ περὶ τῶν ἡμετέρων δὲ μόνον ἀλλὰ καὶ περὶ ὅλου τοῦ κόσμου." data-lxx="" title="Y él es la propiciación por nuestros pecados: y no solamente por los nuestros, s…">1 John 2:2</span><span class="xref-pill" onclick="showXrefPopup(this)" data-ref="1 John 3:5" data-es="Y sabéis que él apareció para quitar nuestros pecados, y no hay pecado en él." data-gr="καὶ οἴδατε ὅτι ἐκεῖνος ἐφανερώθη ἵνα τὰς ⸀ἁμαρτίας ἄρῃ, καὶ ἁμαρτία ἐν αὐτῷ οὐκ ἔστιν." data-lxx="" title="Y sabéis que él apareció para quitar nuestros pecados, y no hay pecado en él.">1 John 3:5</span><span class="xref-pill" onclick="showXrefPopup(this)" data-ref="1 John 4:10" data-es="En esto consiste el amor: no que nosotros hayamos amado á Dios, sino que él nos amó á nosotros, y ha enviado á su Hijo en propiciación por nuestros pecados." data-gr="ἐν τούτῳ ἐστὶν ἡ ἀγάπη, οὐχ ὅτι ἡμεῖς ⸀ἠγαπήκαμεν τὸν θεόν, ἀλλ’ ὅτι αὐτὸς ἠγάπησεν ἡμᾶς καὶ ἀπέστειλεν τὸν υἱὸν αὐτοῦ ἱλασμὸν περὶ τῶν ἁμαρτιῶν ἡμῶν." data-lxx="" title="En esto consiste el amor: no que nosotros hayamos amado á Dios, sino que él nos …">1 John 4:10</span><span class="xref-pill" onclick="showXrefPopup(this)" data-ref="1 Peter 1:19" data-es="Sino con la sangre preciosa de Cristo, como de un cordero sin mancha y sin contaminación:" data-gr="ἀλλὰ τιμίῳ αἵματι ὡς ἀμνοῦ ἀμώμου καὶ ἀσπίλου Χριστοῦ," data-lxx="" title="Sino con la sangre preciosa de Cristo, como de un cordero sin mancha y sin conta…">1 Peter 1:19</span><span class="xref-pill" onclick="showXrefPopup(this)" data-ref="1 Peter 2:24" data-es="El cual mismo llevó nuestros pecados en su cuerpo sobre el madero, para que nosotros siendo muertos á los pecados, vivamos á la justicia: por la herida del cual habéis sido sanados." data-gr="ὃς τὰς ἁμαρτίας ἡμῶν αὐτὸς ἀνήνεγκεν ἐν τῷ σώματι αὐτοῦ ἐπὶ τὸ ξύλον, ἵνα ταῖς ἁμαρτίαις ἀπογενόμενοι τῇ δικαιοσύνῃ ζήσωμεν· οὗ τῷ ⸀μώλωπι ἰάθητε." data-lxx="" title="El cual mismo llevó nuestros pecados en su cuerpo sobre el madero, para que noso…">1 Peter 2:24</span><span class="xref-pill" onclick="showXrefPopup(this)" data-ref="1 Peter 3:18" data-es="Porque también Cristo padeció una vez por los injustos, para llevarnos á Dios, siendo á la verdad muerto en la carne, pero vivificado en espíritu;" data-gr="ὅτι καὶ Χριστὸς ἅπαξ περὶ ἁμαρτιῶν ⸀ἔπαθεν, δίκαιος ὑπὲρ ἀδίκων, ἵνα ⸀ὑμᾶς προσαγάγῃ τῷ θεῷ, θανατωθεὶς μὲν σαρκὶ ζῳοποιηθεὶς δὲ πνεύματι·" data-lxx="" title="Porque también Cristo padeció una vez por los injustos, para llevarnos á Dios, s…">1 Peter 3:18</span><span class="xref-pill" onclick="showXrefPopup(this)" data-ref="1 Timothy 2:6" data-es="El cual se dió á sí mismo en precio del rescate por todos, para testimonio en sus tiempos:" data-gr="ὁ δοὺς ἑαυτὸν ἀντίλυτρον ὑπὲρ πάντων, τὸ μαρτύριον καιροῖς ἰδίοις·" data-lxx="" title="El cual se dió á sí mismo en precio del rescate por todos, para testimonio en su…">1 Timothy 2:6</span></div><div id="pron-29" style="display:none;font-size:0.85rem;color:#1565c0;background:#e3f2fd;padding:4px 8px;border-radius:4px;margin-bottom:0.3rem;font-style:italic" class="pron-line"></div><div id="notes-29" style="display:none;margin-bottom:0.5rem"><textarea id="note-input-29" style="width:100%;min-height:50px;border:1px solid #ccc;border-radius:6px;padding:4px 8px;font-size:0.82rem;resize:vertical" placeholder="Tus notas para v.29..." oninput="saveNote(29)"></textarea></div><div class="section-toggle" onclick="toggleSection('exeg-29')" role="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="exeg-29" tabindex="0"><span class="section-icon">📜</span> Exégesis del Griego <span class="arrow" id="arrow-exeg-29">▶</span></div><div id="exeg-29" class="collapsed section-content exeg-section" role="region"><div class="exeg-summary"><strong style="color:#1b5e20">Alford:</strong> ( γ ) Can the reference be to the lamb of the daily morning and evening sacrifice? or to the sacrificial lamb generally? With the same reservation as above, I think not: for (1) this expression is too…<br><strong style="color:#1b5e20">Bengel:</strong> Εῤχόμενον πρὸς αὐτόν , coming to him ) after His baptism, as we have seen [ and indeed not on the very day of His baptism, on which Jesus was immediately led up into the wilderness ([Matthew 4:1].<br><strong style="color:#1b5e20">Robertson:</strong> On the morrow ( τη επαυριον ).<br></div><div class="comm-item"><div class="comm-header" onclick="document.getElementById('uexv29_0').classList.toggle('collapsed')"><strong>Alford's Greek Testament</strong> <span style=color:#888;font-size:0.75rem>(1863)</span><span class="small-arrow">ver completo ▼</span></div><div id="uexv29_0" class="collapsed comm-body">29. ] τῇ ἐπαύριον , the day after. Those who wish to introduce the Temptation between [John 1:28-29] , interpret it, &lsquo; on some day after .&rsquo; Thus Euthym [29] τῇ ἐπ ., μετὰ τὴν ἀπὸ ἐρήμου κάθοδον αὐτοῦ δηλονότι . But this sense of τῇ ἐπ ., although certainly found in the LXX, see [Genesis 30:33] , is not according to the usage of John (see reff.), and would be quite alien from the precision of this whole portion of the narrative, which, [John 1:40] , specifies even the hours of the day. I understand it therefore literally, both here and in [John 1:35] ; [John 1:44] . [29] Euthymius Zigabenus, 1116 ἐρχ . π . αὐτ . ] It is not said whence , or why , or whether for the purpose of an interview, or not; the fact merely is related, for the sake of the testimony which follows. I mention this, because on these points difficulties have been raised. ἴδε ὁ ἀμν . τ . θ . ] This is one of the most important and difficult sayings in the N.T. The question to be answered is, in calling Jesus by so definite a name as ὁ ἀμνὸς τοῦ θεοῦ , to what did John refer? And this question is intimately connected with that of the meaning of the following words, ὁ αἴρων τὴν ἁμαρτίαν τοῦ κόσμου . ( α ) The title must refer to some known and particular lamb , and cannot be a mere figure for a just and holy man, as Kuinoel and Gabler suppose. It is inconceivable, that ὁ ἀμνὸς τοῦ θεοῦ should in a testimony so precise and formal as this of the Baptist, be nothing but an hyperbole , and that one wholly unprecedented , and to his hearers unintelligible . Had no doctrinal considerations been at stake, we may safely say that this interpretation would never have been proposed. In its bearing on the latter clause of the verse, it is equally untenable. These interpreters make ὁ αἴρων τ . ἁμ . τ . κόσ . to mean, &ldquo;qui pravitatem hominum per vitam suam graviter quidem etsi innocens experietur, sed agni instar mala sibi inflicta patiente et mansueto animo sustinebit&rdquo; (Gabler); or, &ldquo;Hic removebit peccata hominum, i. e, pravitatem e terra,&rdquo; The first of these meanings of αἴρειν is altogether without example: that cited from 1Ma 13:17 , not being applicable. The second, though common enough in other connexions, is never found with ἁμαρτίαν : see reff. The commonsense account of this part of the matter is: John wished to point out Jesus as the Messiah: he designates Him as the Lamb of God; he therefore referred to some definite lamb, revealed by God, sent by God, pleasing to God, or in some meaning especially, τοῦ θεοῦ . Whence did this idea come? ( β ) Can John have referred to the paschal lamb? Further than that the very use of the name brings in with it the general typical use of the animal, and that thus this particular use may lie in the background, I think not , and for this reason: The dominant idea in the paschal sacrifice has no connexion, in any sense of the words, with αἴρειν τὴν ἁμαρτίαν . However by the light now thrown back on it since the Spirit h</div></div><div class="comm-item"><div class="comm-header" onclick="document.getElementById('uexv29_1').classList.toggle('collapsed')"><strong>Bengel's Gnomon</strong> <span style=color:#888;font-size:0.75rem>(1742)</span><span class="small-arrow">ver completo ▼</span></div><div id="uexv29_1" class="collapsed comm-body">[John 1:29] . Εῤχόμενον πρὸς αὐτόν , coming to him ) after His baptism, as we have seen [ and indeed not on the very day of His baptism, on which Jesus was immediately led up into the wilderness ([Matthew 4:1] .) In this place, it seems, Jesus began to walk publickly , [John 1:36] ; [John 1:43] , after His return from the wilderness full of victory (we say flushed with victory , victoriæ plenum) Jesus came to John in such a way, that John could point Him out close at hand: and yet Jesus did not begin the conversation with him . V. g.] ὁ ἀμνὸς τοῦ Θεοῦ , the Lamb of God ) He calls Him the Iamb , [as being] innocent, [and] about to be immolated;[One] who renders active and passive obedience, [1 Peter 1:19] [the precious blood of Christ, as of a lamb without blemish and without spot]. Ὁ , the article has respect to the prophecy delivered concerning Him under this figure, [Isaiah 53:7] [He is brought as a lamb to the slaughter, and as a sheep before her shearers is dumb, so He opened not His mouth]; also under the type of the Paschal lamb. Moreover the passover itself was then near, ch. [John 2:13] . John being divinely instructed, calls Him the Lamb of God : although at that early time the exact understanding of this appellation would escape, if not John himself, at least his hearers. [ Having first asserted his knowledge as to the exalted nature of the person of Jesus Christ, to wit, as to the Word which was made Flesh; next John describes His office and His chief benefit. In like manner Jesus Christ first presented Himself to be acknowledged by the disciples as Son of God; then He instructed them as to His sufferings, etc. V. g.] τοῦ Θεοῦ , of God ) The Lamb of God, whom God gave and approved of; and concerning whom He Himself bears such testimony, This is the only Lamb, this is the only victim pleasing to Me, [Hebrews 10:5] , etc. &ldquo;Sacrifice and offering Thou wouldst not, but a body hast Thou prepared Me: In burnt offerings and sacrifices for sin Thou hast had no pleasure, Then said I, Lo I come to do Thy will O God.&rdquo; So [Psalms 51:17] , The sacrifices of God [mean those] which God acknowledges [as pleasing to Him], [Luke 2:26] , the Lord&rsquo;s Christ . ὁ αἴρων ,) Chrysost. John says, ἀμνὸν ΚΑΙ ΟΤΙ αἴρει , κ . τ . λ . &ldquo;Behold the Lamb, and that He takes away,&rdquo; etc. The Vulgate has Ecce Agnus Dei, ECCE qui tollit , etc. [Behold the Lamb, behold Him who takes away]. Both understood the words ὁ ἀμνὸς , ὁ αἴρων , not in the construction of substantive and adjective, but as in apposition. The Lamb of God , i.e., He who takes away , etc. And this second clause was added by either the Baptist, or the Evangelist, as ch. [John 4:25] [Messias cometh, which is called Christ]. The Lamb of God first took the load of sin off the world on Himself, then rolled it off from Himself. [The same expression evidently, as [1 John 3:5] (He was manifested to take away our sins). V. g.] τὴν ἁμαρτίαν , the sin ) The singular number, with the artic</div></div><div class="comm-item"><div class="comm-header" onclick="document.getElementById('uexv29_2').classList.toggle('collapsed')"><strong>Robertson's Word Pictures</strong> <span style=color:#888;font-size:0.75rem>(1933)</span><span class="small-arrow">ver completo ▼</span></div><div id="uexv29_2" class="collapsed comm-body">On the morrow ( τη επαυριον ). Locative case with ημηρα (day) understood after the adverb επαυριον . "Second day of this spiritual diary" (Bernard) from verse [John 1:19]. Seeth Jesus coming ( βλεπε τον Ιησουν ερχομενον ). Dramatic historical present indicative ( βλεπε ) with vivid present middle participle ( ερχομενον ). Graphic picture. Behold the Lamb of God ( ιδε ο αμνος του θεου ). Exclamation ιδε like ιδου , not verb, and so nominative αμνος . Common idiom in John ([John 1:36]; [John 3:26], etc.). For "the Lamb of God" see [1 Corinthians 5:7] (cf. [John 19:36]) and [1 Peter 1:19]. The passage in [Isaiah 53:6] is directly applied to Christ by Philip in [Acts 8:32]. See also [Matthew 8:17]; [1 Peter 2:22]; [Hebrews 9:28]. But the Jews did not look for a suffering Messiah ([John 12:34]) nor did the disciples at first ([Mark 9:32]; [Luke 24:21]). But was it not possible for John, the Forerunner of the Messiah, to have a prophetic insight concerning the Messiah as the Paschal Lamb, already in [John 1:53], even if the rabbis did not see it there? Symeon had it dimly ([Luke 2:35]), but John more clearly. So Westcott rightly. Bernard is unwilling to believe that John the Baptist had more insight on this point than current Judaism. Then why and how did he recognize Jesus as Messiah at all? Certainly the Baptist did not have to be as ignorant as the rabbis. Which taketh away the sin of the world ( ο αιρων την αμαρτιαν του κοσμου ). Note singular αμαρτιαν not plural αμαρτιας ([1 John 3:5]) where same verb αιρω , to bear away, is used. The future work of the Lamb of God here described in present tense as in [1 John 1:7] about the blood of Christ. He is the Lamb of God for the world, not just for Jews.</div></div></div><div class="section-toggle" onclick="toggleSection('patr-29')" role="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="patr-29" tabindex="0"><span class="section-icon">👨‍🏫</span> Patrística · 7 temas · 43 citas <span class="arrow" id="arrow-patr-29">▶</span></div><div id="patr-29" class="collapsed section-content patr-section" role="region"><div class="patr-theme"><div class="patr-theme-header" onclick="document.getElementById('pt85de496a').classList.toggle('collapsed');this.querySelector('.arrow').classList.toggle('open')"><strong>Cristo como Cordero de Dios</strong><div class="consensus-bar" style="width:60px"><div style="width:85%;background:#4caf50"></div></div><span class="consensus-label" style="color:#4caf50">alto</span><span class="arrow">▼</span></div><div id="pt85de496a" class="collapsed" style="padding:0.5rem 0.2rem 0"><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> (s.V) <span class="patr-meta">— Patriarca de Alejandría, Concilio de Éfeso (Alejandría)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cirilo_de_Alejandr%C3%ADa" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.29]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"Behold, therefore, saith he, the Lamb of God Which taketh away the sin of the world, Whom the Prophet Isaiah did signify to us, saying"</div></div><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>Augustine of Hippo</strong> (354-430) <span class="patr-meta">— Obispo de Hipona, Doctor de la Iglesia, discípulo de Ambrosio de Milán (Hipona)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agust%C3%ADn_de_Hipona" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.29]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"It was Christ that was represented by a ram, Christ by a lamb, Christ by a calf, Christ by a goat—everything was Christ. He was represented by the ram because it leads the flock."</div></div><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>Cyprian</strong> (c.200-258) <span class="patr-meta">— Obispo de Cartago, mártir, discípulo de Tertuliano (escritos) (Cartago)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cipriano_de_Cartago" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.29]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"That Christ is called a sheep and a lamb who was to be slain, and concerning the sacrament (mystery) of the passion. In Isaiah: 'He was led as a sheep to the slaughter, and as a lamb before his shearer is dumb, so He opened not His mouth.'"</div></div><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>Melito of Sardis</strong> (s.II) <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.29]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"The sheep was corruptible, but the Lord is incorruptible, who was crushed as a lamb, but who was resurrected as God. For although he was led to sacrifice as a sheep"</div></div><div class="patr-resumen">Existe consenso universal entre los padres sobre Cristo como el Cordero de Dios prefigurado en el Antiguo Testamento. Los padres conectan esta imagen con las profecías de Isaías y los sacrificios del templo.</div></div></div><div class="patr-theme"><div class="patr-theme-header" onclick="document.getElementById('pta0f6cbdb').classList.toggle('collapsed');this.querySelector('.arrow').classList.toggle('open')"><strong>Quitar el pecado del mundo</strong><div class="consensus-bar" style="width:60px"><div style="width:85%;background:#4caf50"></div></div><span class="consensus-label" style="color:#4caf50">alto</span><span class="arrow">▼</span></div><div id="pta0f6cbdb" class="collapsed" style="padding:0.5rem 0.2rem 0"><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>Augustine of Hippo</strong> (354-430) <span class="patr-meta">— Obispo de Hipona, Doctor de la Iglesia, discípulo de Ambrosio de Milán (Hipona)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agust%C3%ADn_de_Hipona" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.29]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"Behold the Lamb of God; behold Him who taketh away the sin of the world! Let no one so arrogate to himself as to say that he taketh away the sin of the world."</div></div><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> (s.V) <span class="patr-meta">— Patriarca de Alejandría, Concilio de Éfeso (Alejandría)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cirilo_de_Alejandr%C3%ADa" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.29]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"But now he who of old was dimly pictured, the very Lamb, the spotless Sacrifice, is led to the slaughter for all, that he might drive away the sin of the world, that he might overturn the destroyer of the earth, that dying for all he might annihilate death"</div></div><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#ff9800"><div class="patr-cite-header">⚠️ <strong>Gregory the Dialogist</strong> (c.540-604) <span class="patr-meta">— Papa, reformador, Doctor de la Iglesia (Roma)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gregorio_I" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.29]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"But then only will sin be entirely taken away from the human race, when our corruption has been turned to a glorious incorruption. We cannot be free from sin, so long as we are held in the death of the body."</div></div><div class="patr-resumen">Los padres afirman que solo Cristo puede quitar el pecado del mundo, aunque Gregorio Magno matiza que la eliminación completa del pecado ocurrirá en la resurrección final.</div></div></div><div class="patr-theme"><div class="patr-theme-header" onclick="document.getElementById('pt9edf2ead').classList.toggle('collapsed');this.querySelector('.arrow').classList.toggle('open')"><strong>Inocencia del Cordero versus pecaminosidad humana</strong><div class="consensus-bar" style="width:60px"><div style="width:85%;background:#4caf50"></div></div><span class="consensus-label" style="color:#4caf50">alto</span><span class="arrow">▼</span></div><div id="pt9edf2ead" class="collapsed" style="padding:0.5rem 0.2rem 0"><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>Augustine of Hippo</strong> (354-430) <span class="patr-meta">— Obispo de Hipona, Doctor de la Iglesia, discípulo de Ambrosio de Milán (Hipona)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agust%C3%ADn_de_Hipona" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.29]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"If the Lamb of God is innocent, and John is the lamb, must he not be innocent? But all men come of that stock of which David sings sorrowing, Behold, I was conceived in wickedness. He then alone was the Lamb, who was not thus conceived; for He was not conceived in wickedness, nor in sin did His mother bear Him in her womb, Whom a virgin conceived, a virgin brought forth"</div></div><div class="patr-resumen">Agustín distingue claramente entre la inocencia única de Cristo como Cordero y la condición pecaminosa universal de la humanidad, incluyendo a Juan Bautista.</div></div></div><div class="patr-theme"><div class="patr-theme-header" onclick="document.getElementById('ptb984151c').classList.toggle('collapsed');this.querySelector('.arrow').classList.toggle('open')"><strong>Juan como profeta y más que profeta</strong><div class="consensus-bar" style="width:60px"><div style="width:85%;background:#4caf50"></div></div><span class="consensus-label" style="color:#4caf50">alto</span><span class="arrow">▼</span></div><div id="ptb984151c" class="collapsed" style="padding:0.5rem 0.2rem 0"><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> (s.V) <span class="patr-meta">— Patriarca de Alejandría, Concilio de Éfeso (Alejandría)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cirilo_de_Alejandr%C3%ADa" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.29]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"In a very little time, the Baptist is declared to be Prophet alike and Apostle. For Whom he was heralding as coming, Him now come he points out. Therefore, he bounded beyond even the measure of prophets, as the Saviour Himself saith when discoursing with the Jews concerning him, What went ye out into the wilderness for to see? A prophet, yea, I say unto you and more than a prophet."</div></div><div class="patr-resumen">Cirilo de Alejandría destaca que Juan supera a los profetas anteriores porque no solo anuncia la venida de Cristo sino que lo señala presente, siendo tanto profeta como apóstol.</div></div></div><div class="patr-theme"><div class="patr-theme-header" onclick="document.getElementById('ptbe92a654').classList.toggle('collapsed');this.querySelector('.arrow').classList.toggle('open')"><strong>Propósito de la venida de Cristo a Juan</strong><div class="consensus-bar" style="width:60px"><div style="width:85%;background:#4caf50"></div></div><span class="consensus-label" style="color:#4caf50">alto</span><span class="arrow">▼</span></div><div id="ptbe92a654" class="collapsed" style="padding:0.5rem 0.2rem 0"><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>John Chrysostom</strong> (c.349-407) <span class="patr-meta">— Patriarca de Constantinopla, "Boca de Oro", discípulo de Diodoro de Tarso (Antioquía/Constantinopla)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Juan_Cris%C3%B3stomo" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.29]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"Wherefore then did He come to John? In order that since John had baptized Him with many (others), no one might suppose that He had hastened to John for the same reason as the rest to confess sins, and to wash in the river unto repentance. For this He comes, to give John an opportunity of setting this opinion right again"</div></div><div class="patr-resumen">Crisóstomo explica que Cristo vino a Juan no para confesar pecados como los demás, sino para dar oportunidad a Juan de aclarar su verdadera identidad como Cordero de Dios.</div></div></div><div class="patr-theme"><div class="patr-theme-header" onclick="document.getElementById('pt5d3b53b7').classList.toggle('collapsed');this.querySelector('.arrow').classList.toggle('open')"><strong>Conocimiento de Juan sobre la misión de Cristo</strong><div class="consensus-bar" style="width:60px"><div style="width:55%;background:#ff9800"></div></div><span class="consensus-label" style="color:#ff9800">medio</span><span class="arrow">▼</span></div><div id="pt5d3b53b7" class="collapsed" style="padding:0.5rem 0.2rem 0"><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#f44336"><div class="patr-cite-header">❌ <strong>John Chrysostom</strong> (c.349-407) <span class="patr-meta">— Patriarca de Constantinopla, "Boca de Oro", discípulo de Diodoro de Tarso (Antioquía/Constantinopla)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Juan_Cris%C3%B3stomo" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.29]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"What then do some affirm? That this which we have stated was not the cause, but that John was in ignorance, yet not in ignorance of all; but that He was the Christ, he knew, but whether He was also to die for mankind, he knew not, therefore he said, Are You He that should come? But this is not tenable; for neither of this was John ignorant. This at least he proclaimed even before all the others, and bare record of this first, Behold, says he, the Lamb of God"</div></div><div class="patr-resumen">Crisóstomo refuta la idea de que Juan ignoraba la misión sacrificial de Cristo, argumentando que el título 'Cordero de Dios' demuestra su pleno conocimiento del propósito redentor.</div></div></div><div class="patr-theme"><div class="patr-theme-header" onclick="document.getElementById('pt5ffc5399').classList.toggle('collapsed');this.querySelector('.arrow').classList.toggle('open')"><strong>Tipología del Antiguo Testamento</strong><div class="consensus-bar" style="width:60px"><div style="width:85%;background:#4caf50"></div></div><span class="consensus-label" style="color:#4caf50">alto</span><span class="arrow">▼</span></div><div id="pt5ffc5399" class="collapsed" style="padding:0.5rem 0.2rem 0"><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>Ambrose of Milan</strong> (c.340-397) <span class="patr-meta">— Obispo de Milán, Doctor de la Iglesia (Milán)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ambrosio_de_Mil%C3%A1n" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.29]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"Abel knew how to divide when he offered a sacrifice from 'the firstlings of his flock,' teaching that the gifts of the earth, which had degenerated in the sinner, will not please God. But those in which the grace of the divine mystery shone forth will please him. And so he prophesied that we were to be redeemed from fault through the passion of the Lord, of whom it is written: 'Here is the lamb of God who takes away the sin of the world.'"</div></div><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>Tertullian</strong> (c.160-225) <span class="patr-meta">— Padre de la teología latina (Cartago)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tertuliano" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.29]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"He was the burning and shining lamp; as being he who not merely 'prepared His ways in the desert,' but withal, by pointing out 'the Lamb of God,' illumined the minds of men by his heralding, so that they understood Him to be that Lamb whom Moses was wont to announce as destined to suffer."</div></div><div class="patr-resumen">Los padres ven en Cristo el cumplimiento de las figuras del Antiguo Testamento, especialmente los sacrificios de Abel y las profecías mosaicas sobre el cordero que habría de sufrir.</div></div></div></div></div><div class="verse-block" id="vb30"><div class="verse-header"><span class="vnum">30</span></div><div class="greek-line" id="greek-30">οὗτός ἐστιν ὑπὲρ οὗ ἐγὼ εἶπον Ὀπίσω μου ἔρχεται ἀνὴρ ὃς ἔμπροσθέν μου γέγονεν ὅτι πρῶτός μου ἦν</div><div class="uncial-line" id="uncial-30" style="display:none;font-family:'Noto Serif',serif;font-size:0.9rem;letter-spacing:1.5px;color:#5d4037;background:#efebe9;padding:6px 10px;border-radius:4px;margin-bottom:0.3rem;overflow-wrap:break-word" title="Así se vería este versículo en un manuscrito uncial del siglo IV — todo mayúsculas, sin espacios (puntos medios añadidos como ayuda visual)">ΟΥΤΟΣ·ΕΣΤΙΝ·ΥΠΕΡ·ΟΥ·ΕΓΩ·ΕΙΠΟΝ·ΟΠΙΣΩ·ΜΟΥ·ΕΡΧΕΤΑΙ·ΑΝΗΡ·ΟΣ·ΕΜΠΡΟΣΘΕΝ·ΜΟΥ·ΓΕΓΟΝΕΝ·ΟΤΙ·ΠΡΩΤΟΣ·ΜΟΥ·ΗΝ<span style="display:block;font-size:0.65rem;margin-top:4px;letter-spacing:0;opacity:0.7"><a href="https://www.codexsinaiticus.org/en/manuscript.aspx?book=33&chapter=1&verse=30" target="_blank" style="color:#5d4037">Ver en Codex Sinaiticus →</a></span></div><div style="display:flex;gap:0.3rem;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:0.3rem;align-items:center"><div class="rvr-btn" onclick="toggleRVR(30)">RVR60</div><div class="rvr-btn" onclick="document.getElementById('uncial-30').style.display=document.getElementById('uncial-30').style.display==='none'?'':'none'" style="background:#efebe9;color:#5d4037;border-color:#bcaaa4" title="Escritura uncial: así se veía el NT en los manuscritos del s.IV (Vaticanus, Sinaiticus). Todo mayúsculas, sin espacios entre palabras (scriptio continua), sin acentos ni puntuación. La iota subscrita se escribe explícitamente.">Uncial</div><div class="rvr-btn" onclick="document.getElementById('pron-30').style.display=document.getElementById('pron-30').style.display==='none'?'':'none'" style="background:#e3f2fd;color:#1565c0;border-color:#90caf9" title="Pronunciación bizantina aproximada (griego moderno/eclesiástico)">Pronunciación</div><div class="rvr-btn vbtn-patr" onclick="toggleSection('patr-30')" title="11 citas patrísticas disponibles">✝ 11 patrística</div><div class="rvr-btn vbtn-exeg" onclick="toggleSection('exeg-30')" title="3 comentarios exegéticos">📚 3 exégesis</div><div class="rvr-btn vbtn-ver" onclick="document.getElementById('vers-30').classList.toggle('collapsed')" title="9 versiones disponibles">📖 versiones</div><div class="rvr-btn" onclick="copyVerse(30)" title="Copiar versículo al portapapeles" style="background:#f3e5f5;border-color:#ce93d8;color:#6a1b9a">📋</div><div class="rvr-btn" onclick="document.getElementById('notes-30').style.display=document.getElementById('notes-30').style.display==='none'?'':'none'" title="Notas personales" style="background:#fff8e1;border-color:#ffd54f;color:#f57f17">📝</div></div><div id="rvr-30" class="collapsed spanish-line">Este es aquel de quien yo dije: Después de mí viene un varón, el cual es antes de mí; porque era primero que yo.</div><div id="vers-30" class="collapsed" style="margin-bottom:0.5rem"><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">RVR60</span>Este es aquel de quien yo dije: Después de mí viene un varón, el cual es antes de mí; porque era primero que yo.</div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">RVR1909</span>Este es del que dije: Tras mí viene un varón, el cual es antes de mí: porque era primero que yo.</div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">KJV</span>This is he of whom I said, After me cometh a man which is preferred before me: for he was before me.</div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">ASV</span>This is he of whom I said, After me cometh a man who is become before me: for he was before me. </div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">BSB</span>This is He of whom I said, ‘A man who comes after me has surpassed me because He was before me.’ </div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">Darby</span>He it is of whom I said, A man comes after me who takes a place before me, because he was before me; </div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">LITV</span>This is He about whom I said, After me comes a Man who has been before me, for He was preceding me. </div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">YLT</span>this is he concerning whom I said, After me doth come a man, who hath come before me, because he was before me:</div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">Vulgate</span>hic est de quo dixi post me venit vir qui ante me factus est quia prior me erat</div></div><div id="pron-30" style="display:none;font-size:0.85rem;color:#1565c0;background:#e3f2fd;padding:4px 8px;border-radius:4px;margin-bottom:0.3rem;font-style:italic" class="pron-line"></div><div id="notes-30" style="display:none;margin-bottom:0.5rem"><textarea id="note-input-30" style="width:100%;min-height:50px;border:1px solid #ccc;border-radius:6px;padding:4px 8px;font-size:0.82rem;resize:vertical" placeholder="Tus notas para v.30..." oninput="saveNote(30)"></textarea></div><div class="section-toggle" onclick="toggleSection('exeg-30')" role="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="exeg-30" tabindex="0"><span class="section-icon">📜</span> Exégesis del Griego <span class="arrow" id="arrow-exeg-30">▶</span></div><div id="exeg-30" class="collapsed section-content exeg-section" role="region"><div class="exeg-summary"><strong style="color:#1b5e20">Alford:</strong> <br><strong style="color:#1b5e20">Bengel:</strong> Ἀνήρ , a man ) Great, peerless.<br><strong style="color:#1b5e20">Robertson:</strong> " John points to Jesus as he speaks: "This is he.<br></div><div class="comm-item"><div class="comm-header" onclick="document.getElementById('uexv30_0').classList.toggle('collapsed')"><strong>Alford's Greek Testament</strong> <span style=color:#888;font-size:0.75rem>(1863)</span><span class="small-arrow">ver completo ▼</span></div><div id="uexv30_0" class="collapsed comm-body">30. ] See on [John 1:15] .</div></div><div class="comm-item"><div class="comm-header" onclick="document.getElementById('uexv30_1').classList.toggle('collapsed')"><strong>Bengel's Gnomon</strong> <span style=color:#888;font-size:0.75rem>(1742)</span><span class="small-arrow">ver completo ▼</span></div><div id="uexv30_1" class="collapsed comm-body">[John 1:30] . Ἀνήρ , a man ) Great, peerless. πρῶτος , prior [to me]) Notes, [John 1:15] .</div></div><div class="comm-item"><div class="comm-header" onclick="document.getElementById('uexv30_2').classList.toggle('collapsed')"><strong>Robertson's Word Pictures</strong> <span style=color:#888;font-size:0.75rem>(1933)</span><span class="small-arrow">ver completo ▼</span></div><div id="uexv30_2" class="collapsed comm-body">Of whom ( υπερ ου ). Not περ , but υπερ . "On behalf of whom." John points to Jesus as he speaks: "This is he." There he is. See verse [John 1:15] for discussion of these words of John.</div></div></div><div class="section-toggle" onclick="toggleSection('patr-30')" role="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="patr-30" tabindex="0"><span class="section-icon">👨‍🏫</span> Patrística · 2 temas · 11 citas <span class="arrow" id="arrow-patr-30">▶</span></div><div id="patr-30" class="collapsed section-content patr-section" role="region"><div class="patr-theme"><div class="patr-theme-header" onclick="document.getElementById('pta3118cd6').classList.toggle('collapsed');this.querySelector('.arrow').classList.toggle('open')"><strong>Preexistencia y eternidad de Cristo</strong><div class="consensus-bar" style="width:60px"><div style="width:85%;background:#4caf50"></div></div><span class="consensus-label" style="color:#4caf50">alto</span><span class="arrow">▼</span></div><div id="pta3118cd6" class="collapsed" style="padding:0.5rem 0.2rem 0"><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>Augustine of Hippo</strong> (s.IV-V (354-430)) <span class="patr-meta">— Obispo de Hipona, Doctor de la Iglesia, discípulo de Ambrosio de Milán (Hipona)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agust%C3%ADn_de_Hipona" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.30]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"This is He of whom I spake, After me cometh a Man who was made before me; for He was before me. Cometh after me, because He was born later; was made before me, because preferred before me; He was before me, because, In the beginning was the Word, and the Word was with God, and the Word was God."</div></div><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>Gregory the Dialogist</strong> (s.VI (c.540-604)) <span class="patr-meta">— Papa, reformador, Doctor de la Iglesia (Roma)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gregorio_I" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.30]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"He explains the reason of this superiority, in what follows: For He was before me; as if his meaning was; And this is the reason of His being superior to me, though born after me, viz. that He is not circumscribed by the time of His nativity. He Who was born of His mother in time, was begotten of His Father out of time."</div></div><div class="patr-resumen">Los padres afirman unánimemente que Cristo preexiste eternamente como Verbo divino, explicando cómo puede ser 'después' de Juan en nacimiento pero 'antes' en dignidad y existencia eterna.</div></div></div><div class="patr-theme"><div class="patr-theme-header" onclick="document.getElementById('ptee8b6560').classList.toggle('collapsed');this.querySelector('.arrow').classList.toggle('open')"><strong>Tipología matrimonial de Cristo y la Iglesia</strong><div class="consensus-bar" style="width:60px"><div style="width:55%;background:#ff9800"></div></div><span class="consensus-label" style="color:#ff9800">medio</span><span class="arrow">▼</span></div><div id="ptee8b6560" class="collapsed" style="padding:0.5rem 0.2rem 0"><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>Ephrem the Syrian</strong> (s.IV (c.306-373)) <span class="patr-meta">— Diácono, poeta teológico (Nísibis/Edesa)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Efr%C3%A9n_el_Sirio" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.30]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"Eliezar sought Rebekah as a bride at a well of water. Jacob sought Rachel at a well of water, as Moses did so with Zipporah. Thus, all of these were types of the Lord, who sought his church as a bride by the baptism at the Jordan River. And just as Eliezar made Rebekah known to his master when he came to meet her in the field, so also John made our Savior known at the Jordan: See, the Lamb of God, for he takes away the sin of the world."</div></div><div class="patr-resumen">Algunos padres, como Efrén, desarrollan una tipología donde el bautismo de Cristo prefigura su unión esponsal con la Iglesia, conectando con los matrimonios bíblicos junto a pozos de agua.</div></div></div></div></div><div class="verse-block" id="vb31"><div class="verse-header"><span class="vnum">31</span></div><div class="greek-line" id="greek-31">κἀγὼ οὐκ ᾔδειν αὐτόν ἀλλ᾽ ἵνα φανερωθῇ τῷ Ἰσραὴλ διὰ τοῦτο ἦλθον ἐγὼ ἐν ὕδατι βαπτίζων</div><div class="uncial-line" id="uncial-31" style="display:none;font-family:'Noto Serif',serif;font-size:0.9rem;letter-spacing:1.5px;color:#5d4037;background:#efebe9;padding:6px 10px;border-radius:4px;margin-bottom:0.3rem;overflow-wrap:break-word" title="Así se vería este versículo en un manuscrito uncial del siglo IV — todo mayúsculas, sin espacios (puntos medios añadidos como ayuda visual)">ΚΑΓΩ·ΟΥΚ·ΗΙΔΕΙΝ·ΑΥΤΟΝ·ΑΛΛ·ΙΝΑ·ΦΑΝΕΡΩΘΗΙ·ΤΩΙ·ΙΣΡΑΗΛ·ΔΙΑ·ΤΟΥΤΟ·ΗΛΘΟΝ·ΕΓΩ·ΕΝ·ΥΔΑΤΙ·ΒΑΠΤΙΖΩΝ<span style="display:block;font-size:0.65rem;margin-top:4px;letter-spacing:0;opacity:0.7"><a href="https://www.codexsinaiticus.org/en/manuscript.aspx?book=33&chapter=1&verse=31" target="_blank" style="color:#5d4037">Ver en Codex Sinaiticus →</a></span></div><div style="display:flex;gap:0.3rem;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:0.3rem;align-items:center"><div class="rvr-btn" onclick="toggleRVR(31)">RVR60</div><div class="rvr-btn" onclick="document.getElementById('uncial-31').style.display=document.getElementById('uncial-31').style.display==='none'?'':'none'" style="background:#efebe9;color:#5d4037;border-color:#bcaaa4" title="Escritura uncial: así se veía el NT en los manuscritos del s.IV (Vaticanus, Sinaiticus). Todo mayúsculas, sin espacios entre palabras (scriptio continua), sin acentos ni puntuación. La iota subscrita se escribe explícitamente.">Uncial</div><div class="rvr-btn" onclick="document.getElementById('pron-31').style.display=document.getElementById('pron-31').style.display==='none'?'':'none'" style="background:#e3f2fd;color:#1565c0;border-color:#90caf9" title="Pronunciación bizantina aproximada (griego moderno/eclesiástico)">Pronunciación</div><div class="rvr-btn vbtn-patr" onclick="toggleSection('patr-31')" title="5 citas patrísticas disponibles">✝ 5 patrística</div><div class="rvr-btn vbtn-exeg" onclick="toggleSection('exeg-31')" title="3 comentarios exegéticos">📚 3 exégesis</div><div class="rvr-btn vbtn-ver" onclick="document.getElementById('vers-31').classList.toggle('collapsed')" title="9 versiones disponibles">📖 versiones</div><div class="rvr-btn" onclick="copyVerse(31)" title="Copiar versículo al portapapeles" style="background:#f3e5f5;border-color:#ce93d8;color:#6a1b9a">📋</div><div class="rvr-btn" onclick="document.getElementById('notes-31').style.display=document.getElementById('notes-31').style.display==='none'?'':'none'" title="Notas personales" style="background:#fff8e1;border-color:#ffd54f;color:#f57f17">📝</div></div><div id="rvr-31" class="collapsed spanish-line">Y yo no le conocía; mas para que fuese manifestado a Israel, por esto vine yo bautizando con agua.</div><div id="vers-31" class="collapsed" style="margin-bottom:0.5rem"><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">RVR60</span>Y yo no le conocía; mas para que fuese manifestado a Israel, por esto vine yo bautizando con agua.</div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">RVR1909</span>Y yo no le conocía; más para que fuese manifestado á Israel, por eso vine yo bautizando con agua.</div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">KJV</span>And I knew him not: but that he should be made manifest to Israel, therefore am I come baptizing with water.</div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">ASV</span>And I knew him not; but that he should be made manifest to Israel, for this cause came I baptizing in water. </div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">BSB</span>I myself did not know Him, but the reason I came baptizing with water was that He might be revealed to Israel.” </div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">Darby</span>and I knew him not; but that he might be manifested to Israel, therefore have I come baptising with water. </div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">LITV</span>And I did not know Him; but that He be revealed to Israel, for this reason I came baptizing in water. </div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">YLT</span>and I knew him not, but, that he might be manifested to Israel, because of this I came with the water baptizing.</div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">Vulgate</span>et ego nesciebam eum sed ut manifestaretur Israhel propterea veni ego in aqua baptizans</div></div><div id="pron-31" style="display:none;font-size:0.85rem;color:#1565c0;background:#e3f2fd;padding:4px 8px;border-radius:4px;margin-bottom:0.3rem;font-style:italic" class="pron-line"></div><div id="notes-31" style="display:none;margin-bottom:0.5rem"><textarea id="note-input-31" style="width:100%;min-height:50px;border:1px solid #ccc;border-radius:6px;padding:4px 8px;font-size:0.82rem;resize:vertical" placeholder="Tus notas para v.31..." oninput="saveNote(31)"></textarea></div><div class="section-toggle" onclick="toggleSection('exeg-31')" role="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="exeg-31" tabindex="0"><span class="section-icon">📜</span> Exégesis del Griego <span class="arrow" id="arrow-exeg-31">▶</span></div><div id="exeg-31" class="collapsed section-content exeg-section" role="region"><div class="exeg-summary"><strong style="color:#1b5e20">Alford:</strong> Both accounts are entirely consistent with the supposition that John had been from youth upwards acquainted with our Lord, and indeed may have in his own mind believed Him to be the Christ: but having…<br><strong style="color:#1b5e20">Bengel:</strong> ἵνα , that ) expresses not the sole end, but still the primary one, why he came baptizing with water; [Acts 19:4] : &ldquo;John verily baptized with the baptism of repentance, saying unto the people, …<br><strong style="color:#1b5e20">Robertson:</strong> And I knew him not ( καγω ουκ ηιδειν αυτον ).<br></div><div class="comm-item"><div class="comm-header" onclick="document.getElementById('uexv31_0').classList.toggle('collapsed')"><strong>Alford's Greek Testament</strong> <span style=color:#888;font-size:0.75rem>(1863)</span><span class="small-arrow">ver completo ▼</span></div><div id="uexv31_0" class="collapsed comm-body">31. ] On the apparent discrepancy between this statement, οὐκ ῃδειν αὐτόν , and St. Matthew&rsquo;s narrative, I have stated my view on [Matthew 3:14] . Both accounts are entirely consistent with the supposition that John had been from youth upwards acquainted with our Lord, and indeed may have in his own mind believed Him to be the Christ: but having ( Joh 1:33 ) a special sign appointed him , by which to recognize Him as such, until that sign was given, he, like the rest of the people ( κἀγώ , I also, see Joh 1:26 ), had no certain knowledge of Him. Lücke&rsquo;s whole note proceeds upon the unworthy view of the historical character of the Gospels which his school has adopted. The same may be said of Neander, Leben Jesu, pp. 86 ff. De Wette gives the sense well: &ldquo;This testimony ( Joh 1:30 ) does not rest upon my long personal acquaintance with Him, but on that which happened during my work of baptizing.&rdquo; ἀλλ ʼ ἵνα φαν . ] Justin Martyr represents Trypho the Jew saying, χριστὸς δὲ εἰ καὶ γεγέννηται , καὶ ἔστι που , ἄγνωστός ἐστι , καὶ οὐδὲ αὐτός πω ἑαυτὸν ἐπίσταται , οὐδὲ ἔχει δύναμίν τινα , μέχρις ἂν ἐλθὼν Ἡλίας χρίσῃ αὐτὸν καὶ φανερόν πᾶσι ποιήσῃ , § 8, p. 110. But our narrative is not built upon any such Jewish belief, for it is evidently only as a spiritual preparation , through repentance, for the knowledge of Him, that John regarded his baptism, not as any thing ἐκεῖνον φανερὸν πᾶσι ποιοῦν . ἐν [ τῷ ] ὕδ ., hardly distinguishable in English from ἐν ὕδ ., but importing, &lsquo;in the water which it is my custom to use,&rsquo; &lsquo;in the water in which you see I do baptize.&rsquo;</div></div><div class="comm-item"><div class="comm-header" onclick="document.getElementById('uexv31_1').classList.toggle('collapsed')"><strong>Bengel's Gnomon</strong> <span style=color:#888;font-size:0.75rem>(1742)</span><span class="small-arrow">ver completo ▼</span></div><div id="uexv31_1" class="collapsed comm-body">[John 1:31] . Οὐκ ᾔδειν ) I knew Him not by face, just as yourselves [knew Him not], [John 1:26] . &ldquo;There standeth one among you, whom ye know not;&rdquo; at the time that I said, There cometh after me : see [Matthew 3:14] , notes. This manifestly tends to prove that John was divinely instructed to testify as to Christ Jesus. ἵνα , that ) expresses not the sole end, but still the primary one, why he came baptizing with water; [Acts 19:4] : &ldquo;John verily baptized with the baptism of repentance, saying unto the people, that they should believe on Him which should come after him, that is, on Christ Jesus.&rdquo; βαπτίζων , baptizing ) The connecting link of [ i.e. of the previous words with] the words of John the Baptist, after the parenthesis of the Evangelist, presently to be observed.</div></div><div class="comm-item"><div class="comm-header" onclick="document.getElementById('uexv31_2').classList.toggle('collapsed')"><strong>Robertson's Word Pictures</strong> <span style=color:#888;font-size:0.75rem>(1933)</span><span class="small-arrow">ver completo ▼</span></div><div id="uexv31_2" class="collapsed comm-body">And I knew him not ( καγω ουκ ηιδειν αυτον ). Repeated in verse [John 1:33]. Second past perfect of οιδα as imperfect. He had predicted the Messiah and described him before he met him and baptized him. See the Synoptics for that story. Whether John knew Jesus personally before the baptism we do not know. But that he should be made manifest to Israel ( αλλ' ινα φανερωθη τω Ισραηλ ). Final clause with ινα and first aorist passive subjunctive of φανεροω . The purpose of John's ministry was to manifest to Israel with their spiritual privileges ([John 1:49]) the presence of the Messiah. Hence he was baptizing in water those who confessed their sins, he means, as in [Mark 1:5]. The Synoptic account is presupposed all along here.</div></div><div class="themes-divider">🔍 Temas discutidos</div><div class="theme-card"><div class="theme-word">οὐκ ᾔδειν αὐτόν</div><div class="consensus-bar"><div style="width:85%;background:#4caf50"></div></div><div class="opinion" style="border-left-color:#4caf50">✅ <strong>Alford</strong> Juan conocía a Jesús desde la juventud pero necesitaba la señal divina para reconocerlo como Mesías con certeza</div><div class="opinion" style="border-left-color:#4caf50">✅ <strong>Bengel</strong> No lo conocía por rostro, al igual que el pueblo no lo conocía, pero fue divinamente instruido para testificar</div><div class="opinion" style="border-left-color:#4caf50">✅ <strong>Robertson</strong> Había predicho al Mesías antes de conocerlo y bautizarlo, pero no sabemos si lo conocía personalmente antes del bautismo</div><div class="theme-summary">Los comentaristas concuerdan en que Juan no tenía conocimiento cierto de Jesús como Mesías hasta recibir la señal divina.</div></div></div><div class="section-toggle" onclick="toggleSection('patr-31')" role="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="patr-31" tabindex="0"><span class="section-icon">👨‍🏫</span> Patrística · 2 temas · 5 citas <span class="arrow" id="arrow-patr-31">▶</span></div><div id="patr-31" class="collapsed section-content patr-section" role="region"><div class="patr-theme"><div class="patr-theme-header" onclick="document.getElementById('pt7f63bae0').classList.toggle('collapsed');this.querySelector('.arrow').classList.toggle('open')"><strong>Conocimiento de Juan sobre Cristo</strong><div class="consensus-bar" style="width:60px"><div style="width:55%;background:#ff9800"></div></div><span class="consensus-label" style="color:#ff9800">medio</span><span class="arrow">▼</span></div><div id="pt7f63bae0" class="collapsed" style="padding:0.5rem 0.2rem 0"><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#ff9800"><div class="patr-cite-header">⚠️ <strong>John Chrysostom</strong> (s.IV (c.349-407)) <span class="patr-meta">— Patriarca de Constantinopla, "Boca de Oro", discípulo de Diodoro de Tarso (Antioquía/Constantinopla)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Juan_Cris%C3%B3stomo" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.31]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"And I knew Him not, he saith. Here he renders his testimony free from suspicion, by showing that it was not from human friendship, but had been caused by divine revelation. I knew Him not, he saith. How then couldest thou be a trustworthy witness? How shalt thou teach others, while thou thyself art ignorant? He did not say I know Him not, but, I knew Him not; so that in this way he would be shown most trustworthy; for why should he have shown favor to one of whom he was ignorant?"</div></div><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#ff9800"><div class="patr-cite-header">⚠️ <strong>Augustine of Hippo</strong> (s.IV-V (354-430)) <span class="patr-meta">— Obispo de Hipona, Doctor de la Iglesia, discípulo de Ambrosio de Milán (Hipona)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agust%C3%ADn_de_Hipona" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.33]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"Did John hear this that he might know Him whom he had not known, or that he might more fully know Him whom he had already known? For if he had been entirely ignorant of Him, he would not have said to Him when He came to the river to be baptized, I have need to be baptized of Thee, and comest Thou to me? He knew Him therefore."</div></div><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#ff9800"><div class="patr-cite-header">⚠️ <strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> (s.V (c.376-444)) <span class="patr-meta">— Patriarca de Alejandría, Concilio de Éfeso (Alejandría)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cirilo_de_Alejandr%C3%ADa" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.31]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"He that leaped in the depth of the womb of his mother at the voice of the Holy Virgin while yet bearing the Lord, prophet before the travail-pang, disciple in the womb, says of the Saviour, I knew Him not, and says truly, for he does not lie. For God knows all things of Himself and untaught, but the creature, by being taught."</div></div><div class="patr-resumen">Existe debate sobre qué exactamente Juan no conocía de Cristo: algunos padres sugieren ignorancia total previa, otros que conocía su identidad pero no su autoridad bautismal específica.</div></div></div><div class="patr-theme"><div class="patr-theme-header" onclick="document.getElementById('pt03dc3cc0').classList.toggle('collapsed');this.querySelector('.arrow').classList.toggle('open')"><strong>Propósito del bautismo de Juan</strong><div class="consensus-bar" style="width:60px"><div style="width:85%;background:#4caf50"></div></div><span class="consensus-label" style="color:#4caf50">alto</span><span class="arrow">▼</span></div><div id="pt03dc3cc0" class="collapsed" style="padding:0.5rem 0.2rem 0"><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>Augustine of Hippo</strong> (s.IV-V (354-430)) <span class="patr-meta">— Obispo de Hipona, Doctor de la Iglesia, discípulo de Ambrosio de Milán (Hipona)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agust%C3%ADn_de_Hipona" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.31]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"And I knew Him not, he said; but that He might be made manifest to Israel, therefore came I baptizing with water. Give heed for a little, beloved. When did John learn Christ? For he was sent to baptize with water. They asked, Wherefore? That He might be made manifest to Israel, he said."</div></div><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>Augustine of Hippo</strong> (s.IV-V (354-430)) <span class="patr-meta">— Obispo de Hipona, Doctor de la Iglesia, discípulo de Ambrosio de Milán (Hipona)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agust%C3%ADn_de_Hipona" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.31]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"Now when our Lord became known, it was unnecessary to prepare a way for Him; for to those who knew Him, He became His own way. And therefore John's baptism did not last long, but only so long as to show our Lord's humility."</div></div><div class="patr-resumen">Los padres concuerdan que el bautismo de Juan tenía como propósito principal manifestar a Cristo a Israel y preparar el camino, siendo temporal y preparatorio.</div></div></div></div></div><div class="verse-block" id="vb32"><div class="verse-header"><span class="vnum">32</span></div><div class="greek-line" id="greek-32">Καὶ ἐμαρτύρησεν Ἰωάννης λέγων ὅτι Τεθέαμαι τὸ Πνεῦμα καταβαῖνον ὡς περιστερὰν ἐξ οὐρανοῦ καὶ ἔμεινεν ἐπ᾽ αὐτόν</div><div class="uncial-line" id="uncial-32" style="display:none;font-family:'Noto Serif',serif;font-size:0.9rem;letter-spacing:1.5px;color:#5d4037;background:#efebe9;padding:6px 10px;border-radius:4px;margin-bottom:0.3rem;overflow-wrap:break-word" title="Así se vería este versículo en un manuscrito uncial del siglo IV — todo mayúsculas, sin espacios (puntos medios añadidos como ayuda visual)">ΚΑΙ·ΕΜΑΡΤΥΡΗΣΕΝ·ΙΩΑΝΝΗΣ·ΛΕΓΩΝ·ΟΤΙ·ΤΕΘΕΑΜΑΙ·ΤΟ·ΠΝΕΥΜΑ·ΚΑΤΑΒΑΙΝΟΝ·ΩΣ·ΠΕΡΙΣΤΕΡΑΝ·ΕΞ·ΟΥΡΑΝΟΥ·ΚΑΙ·ΕΜΕΙΝΕΝ·ΕΠ·ΑΥΤΟΝ<span style="display:block;font-size:0.65rem;margin-top:4px;letter-spacing:0;opacity:0.7"><a href="https://www.codexsinaiticus.org/en/manuscript.aspx?book=33&chapter=1&verse=32" target="_blank" style="color:#5d4037">Ver en Codex Sinaiticus →</a></span></div><div style="display:flex;gap:0.3rem;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:0.3rem;align-items:center"><div class="rvr-btn" onclick="toggleRVR(32)">RVR60</div><div class="rvr-btn" onclick="document.getElementById('uncial-32').style.display=document.getElementById('uncial-32').style.display==='none'?'':'none'" style="background:#efebe9;color:#5d4037;border-color:#bcaaa4" title="Escritura uncial: así se veía el NT en los manuscritos del s.IV (Vaticanus, Sinaiticus). Todo mayúsculas, sin espacios entre palabras (scriptio continua), sin acentos ni puntuación. La iota subscrita se escribe explícitamente.">Uncial</div><div class="rvr-btn" onclick="document.getElementById('pron-32').style.display=document.getElementById('pron-32').style.display==='none'?'':'none'" style="background:#e3f2fd;color:#1565c0;border-color:#90caf9" title="Pronunciación bizantina aproximada (griego moderno/eclesiástico)">Pronunciación</div><div class="rvr-btn vbtn-patr" onclick="toggleSection('patr-32')" title="23 citas patrísticas disponibles">✝ 23 patrística</div><div class="rvr-btn vbtn-exeg" onclick="toggleSection('exeg-32')" title="2 comentarios exegéticos">📚 2 exégesis</div><div class="rvr-btn vbtn-ver" onclick="document.getElementById('vers-32').classList.toggle('collapsed')" title="9 versiones disponibles">📖 versiones</div><div class="rvr-btn" onclick="document.getElementById('xrefs-32').classList.toggle('collapsed')" title="2 referencias cruzadas">🔗 2 refs</div><div class="rvr-btn" onclick="copyVerse(32)" title="Copiar versículo al portapapeles" style="background:#f3e5f5;border-color:#ce93d8;color:#6a1b9a">📋</div><div class="rvr-btn" onclick="document.getElementById('notes-32').style.display=document.getElementById('notes-32').style.display==='none'?'':'none'" title="Notas personales" style="background:#fff8e1;border-color:#ffd54f;color:#f57f17">📝</div></div><div id="rvr-32" class="collapsed spanish-line">También dio Juan testimonio, diciendo: Vi al Espíritu que descendía del cielo como paloma, y permaneció sobre él.</div><div id="vers-32" class="collapsed" style="margin-bottom:0.5rem"><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">RVR60</span>También dio Juan testimonio, diciendo: Vi al Espíritu que descendía del cielo como paloma, y permaneció sobre él.</div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">RVR1909</span>Y Juan dió testimonio, diciendo: Vi al Espíritu que descendía del cielo como paloma, y reposó sobre él.</div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">KJV</span>And John bare record, saying, I saw the Spirit descending from heaven like a dove, and it abode upon him.</div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">ASV</span>And John bare witness, saying, I have beheld the Spirit descending as a dove out of heaven; and it abode upon him. </div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">BSB</span>Then John testified, “I saw the Spirit descending from heaven like a dove and resting on Him. </div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">Darby</span>And John bore witness, saying, I beheld the Spirit descending as a dove from heaven, and it abode upon him. </div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">LITV</span>And John witnessed, saying, I have seen the Spirit coming down as a dove out of Heaven, and He abode on Him. </div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">YLT</span>And John testified, saying--`I have seen the Spirit coming down, as a dove, out of heaven, and it remained on him;</div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">Vulgate</span>et testimonium perhibuit Iohannes dicens quia vidi Spiritum descendentem quasi columbam de caelo et mansit super eum</div></div><div id="xrefs-32" class="collapsed xref-container" style="margin-bottom:0.5rem"><span class="xref-pill" onclick="showXrefPopup(this)" data-ref="Luke 3:22" data-es="Y descendió el Espíritu Santo sobre él en forma corporal, como paloma, y fué hecha una voz del cielo que decía: Tú eres mi Hijo amado, en ti me he complacido." data-gr="καὶ καταβῆναι τὸ πνεῦμα τὸ ἅγιον σωματικῷ εἴδει ⸀ὡς περιστερὰν ἐπ’ αὐτόν, καὶ φωνὴν ἐξ οὐρανοῦ ⸀γενέσθαι· Σὺ εἶ ὁ υἱός μου ὁ ἀγαπητός, ἐν σοὶ εὐδόκησα." data-lxx="" title="Y descendió el Espíritu Santo sobre él en forma corporal, como paloma, y fué hec…">Luke 3:22</span><span class="xref-pill" onclick="showXrefPopup(this)" data-ref="Mark 1:10" data-es="Y luego, subiendo del agua, vió abrirse los cielos, y al Espíritu como paloma, que descendía sobre él." data-gr="καὶ ⸀εὐθὺς ἀναβαίνων ⸀ἐκ τοῦ ὕδατος εἶδεν σχιζομένους τοὺς οὐρανοὺς καὶ τὸ πνεῦμα ⸀ὡς περιστερὰν καταβαῖνον ⸀εἰς αὐτόν·" data-lxx="" title="Y luego, subiendo del agua, vió abrirse los cielos, y al Espíritu como paloma, q…">Mark 1:10</span></div><div id="pron-32" style="display:none;font-size:0.85rem;color:#1565c0;background:#e3f2fd;padding:4px 8px;border-radius:4px;margin-bottom:0.3rem;font-style:italic" class="pron-line"></div><div id="notes-32" style="display:none;margin-bottom:0.5rem"><textarea id="note-input-32" style="width:100%;min-height:50px;border:1px solid #ccc;border-radius:6px;padding:4px 8px;font-size:0.82rem;resize:vertical" placeholder="Tus notas para v.32..." oninput="saveNote(32)"></textarea></div><div class="section-toggle" onclick="toggleSection('exeg-32')" role="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="exeg-32" tabindex="0"><span class="section-icon">📜</span> Exégesis del Griego <span class="arrow" id="arrow-exeg-32">▶</span></div><div id="exeg-32" class="collapsed section-content exeg-section" role="region"><div class="exeg-summary"><strong style="color:#1b5e20">Bengel:</strong> The Evangelist interposes this in the midst of the connected words of the Baptist, as a sort of parenthesis; ἐμαρτύρησεν ὅτι , John bare record, saying.<br><strong style="color:#1b5e20">Robertson:</strong> Bare witness ( εμαρτυρησεν ).<br></div><div class="comm-item"><div class="comm-header" onclick="document.getElementById('uexv32_0').classList.toggle('collapsed')"><strong>Bengel's Gnomon</strong> <span style=color:#888;font-size:0.75rem>(1742)</span><span class="small-arrow">ver completo ▼</span></div><div id="uexv32_0" class="collapsed comm-body">[John 1:32] . Ἐμαρτύρησεν , bare record ) as concerning a fact seen by himself and not by the people. The words which follow [comp. Joh 1:34 ] were part of his record [testimony]: the words which are spoken, [John 1:29] , etc. [comp. [John 1:15] ; Joh 1:27 ], were part of his demonstration [the clear proof of Jesus&rsquo; Messiahship, which John demonstrated] from the record. The Evangelist interposes this in the midst of the connected words of the Baptist, as a sort of parenthesis; ἐμαρτύρησεν ὅτι , John bare record, saying . τεθέαμαι ) I beheld . ἐξούρανοῦ , from heaven ) Construe this with καταβαῖνον , descending. The descent , at least in its last and lowest part, was equally determinate [in its direction] towards Jesus, as its abiding on Him. ἔμεινεν , it abode ) with a considerable stay [continuance].</div></div><div class="comm-item"><div class="comm-header" onclick="document.getElementById('uexv32_1').classList.toggle('collapsed')"><strong>Robertson's Word Pictures</strong> <span style=color:#888;font-size:0.75rem>(1933)</span><span class="small-arrow">ver completo ▼</span></div><div id="uexv32_1" class="collapsed comm-body">Bare witness ( εμαρτυρησεν ). First aorist active indicative of μαρτυρεω . Another specimen of John's witness to the Messiah ([John 1:7]; [John 1:15]; [John 1:19]; [John 1:29]; [John 1:35]; [John 1:36]). I have beheld ( τεθεαμα ). Perfect middle indicative of θεαομα , the realization of the promise of the sign (verse [John 1:33]) by which he should recognize the Messiah. As a matter of fact, we know that he so recognized Jesus as Messiah when he came for baptism before the Holy Spirit came ([Matthew 3:14]). But this sight of the Spirit descending as a dove upon Jesus at his baptism ([Mark 1:10]; [Matthew 3:16]; [Luke 3:22]) became permanent proof to him. John's allusion assumes the Synoptic record. The Semites regarded the dove as a symbol of the Spirit.</div></div><div class="themes-divider">🔍 Temas discutidos</div><div class="theme-card"><div class="theme-word">τεθέαμαι</div><div class="consensus-bar"><div style="width:85%;background:#4caf50"></div></div><div class="opinion" style="border-left-color:#4caf50">✅ <strong>Bengel</strong> El perfecto τεθέαμαι indica que Juan contempló personalmente el hecho que el pueblo no vio</div><div class="opinion" style="border-left-color:#4caf50">✅ <strong>Robertson</strong> Perfecto medio de θεάομαι, la realización de la promesa de la señal para reconocer al Mesías</div><div class="theme-summary">Ambos comentaristas están de acuerdo en que el tiempo perfecto enfatiza la experiencia personal y permanente de Juan.</div></div></div><div class="section-toggle" onclick="toggleSection('patr-32')" role="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="patr-32" tabindex="0"><span class="section-icon">👨‍🏫</span> Patrística · 4 temas · 23 citas <span class="arrow" id="arrow-patr-32">▶</span></div><div id="patr-32" class="collapsed section-content patr-section" role="region"><div class="patr-theme"><div class="patr-theme-header" onclick="document.getElementById('pt6d327d90').classList.toggle('collapsed');this.querySelector('.arrow').classList.toggle('open')"><strong>Descenso del Espíritu Santo como paloma</strong><div class="consensus-bar" style="width:60px"><div style="width:85%;background:#4caf50"></div></div><span class="consensus-label" style="color:#4caf50">alto</span><span class="arrow">▼</span></div><div id="pt6d327d90" class="collapsed" style="padding:0.5rem 0.2rem 0"><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>Augustine of Hippo</strong> (s.IV-V (354-430)) <span class="patr-meta">— Obispo de Hipona, Doctor de la Iglesia, discípulo de Ambrosio de Milán (Hipona)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agust%C3%ADn_de_Hipona" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.32]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"We do not attribute only to Christ the possession of a real body and say that the Holy Spirit assumed a false appearance to people's eyes. For the Holy Spirit could no more, in consistency with his nature, deceive people than could the Son of God. The almighty God, who made every creature out of nothing, could as easily form a real body of a dove, without the instrumentality of other doves, as he made a real body in the womb of the Virgin without the seed of the male."</div></div><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#ff9800"><div class="patr-cite-header">⚠️ <strong>Theodore of Mopsuestia</strong> (s.IV-V (c.350-428)) <span class="patr-meta">— Obispo, exégeta antioqueno, discípulo de Diodoro de Tarso (Mopsuestia)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Teodoro_de_Mopsuestia" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.32]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"Here it is evident that the Spirit descending like a dove on the baptized Lord was not seen by all those present, but by John only in a sort of spiritual vision. Similarly, the prophets amid many people were used to seeing those things that were invisible to all the others. It would have been useless to say that John testified and said, I saw the Spirit, if all those present had been participants in that vision as well."</div></div><div class="patr-resumen">Los padres concuerdan en la realidad del descenso del Espíritu, aunque difieren sobre si fue visible para todos o solo para Juan en visión espiritual.</div></div></div><div class="patr-theme"><div class="patr-theme-header" onclick="document.getElementById('pt58d7f697').classList.toggle('collapsed');this.querySelector('.arrow').classList.toggle('open')"><strong>Simbolismo de la paloma</strong><div class="consensus-bar" style="width:60px"><div style="width:85%;background:#4caf50"></div></div><span class="consensus-label" style="color:#4caf50">alto</span><span class="arrow">▼</span></div><div id="pt58d7f697" class="collapsed" style="padding:0.5rem 0.2rem 0"><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>Augustine of Hippo</strong> (s.IV-V (354-430)) <span class="patr-meta">— Obispo de Hipona, Doctor de la Iglesia, discípulo de Ambrosio de Milán (Hipona)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agust%C3%ADn_de_Hipona" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.32]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"Now if the dove's note is a moaning, as we all know it to be, and doves moan in love, hear what the apostle says, and wonder not that the Holy Ghost willed to be manifested in the form of a dove: For what we should pray for as we ought, says he, we know not; but the Spirit Himself intercedes for us with groanings which cannot be uttered."</div></div><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>Augustine of Hippo</strong> (s.IV-V (354-430)) <span class="patr-meta">— Obispo de Hipona, Doctor de la Iglesia, discípulo de Ambrosio de Milán (Hipona)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agust%C3%ADn_de_Hipona" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.32]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"Hence in this manner it behoved the Holy Spirit to be manifested when coming upon the Lord, that every one might understand that if he has the Holy Spirit he ought to be simple as the dove, to have true peace with his brethren, that peace which the kisses of doves signify."</div></div><div class="patr-resumen">Los padres interpretan unánimemente la paloma como símbolo de simplicidad, paz, amor y unidad, enseñando virtudes cristianas esenciales.</div></div></div><div class="patr-theme"><div class="patr-theme-header" onclick="document.getElementById('pta5be60fb').classList.toggle('collapsed');this.querySelector('.arrow').classList.toggle('open')"><strong>Cristo no necesitaba el Espíritu Santo</strong><div class="consensus-bar" style="width:60px"><div style="width:85%;background:#4caf50"></div></div><span class="consensus-label" style="color:#4caf50">alto</span><span class="arrow">▼</span></div><div id="pta5be60fb" class="collapsed" style="padding:0.5rem 0.2rem 0"><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>Augustine of Hippo</strong> (s.IV-V (354-430)) <span class="patr-meta">— Obispo de Hipona, Doctor de la Iglesia, discípulo de Ambrosio de Milán (Hipona)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agust%C3%ADn_de_Hipona" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.32]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"Christ was certainly not then anointed with the Holy Spirit when the Spirit as a dove descended upon him at his baptism. For here he condescends to prefigure his body, that is, his church, in which preeminently the baptized receive the Holy Spirit. For it would be most absurd to believe that he received the Holy Spirit when he was near thirty years of age."</div></div><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>John Chrysostom</strong> (s.IV (c.349-407)) <span class="patr-meta">— Patriarca de Constantinopla, "Boca de Oro", discípulo de Diodoro de Tarso (Antioquía/Constantinopla)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Juan_Cris%C3%B3stomo" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.32]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"But again, test any one should suppose that he was in need of the Spirit as we are, hear how he removes the suspicion, by showing that the descent of the Spirit was only to declare Christ."</div></div><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>Gregory the Dialogist</strong> (s.VI (c.540-604)) <span class="patr-meta">— Papa, reformador, Doctor de la Iglesia (Roma)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gregorio_I" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.32]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"For the Spirit descends on all the faithful. But he only remains on the mediator—and does so in a special way. For he has never left the Son's human nature even as he proceeds from his divine nature."</div></div><div class="patr-resumen">Los padres concuerdan que Cristo no necesitaba recibir el Espíritu Santo, sino que el descenso fue para manifestar su identidad y prefigurar la experiencia de la Iglesia.</div></div></div><div class="patr-theme"><div class="patr-theme-header" onclick="document.getElementById('pt3027d163').classList.toggle('collapsed');this.querySelector('.arrow').classList.toggle('open')"><strong>Manifestación de la Trinidad</strong><div class="consensus-bar" style="width:60px"><div style="width:85%;background:#4caf50"></div></div><span class="consensus-label" style="color:#4caf50">alto</span><span class="arrow">▼</span></div><div id="pt3027d163" class="collapsed" style="padding:0.5rem 0.2rem 0"><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>Augustine of Hippo</strong> (s.IV-V (354-430)) <span class="patr-meta">— Obispo de Hipona, Doctor de la Iglesia, discípulo de Ambrosio de Milán (Hipona)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agust%C3%ADn_de_Hipona" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.32]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"See, the dove descended upon the Lord, upon the Lord when baptized: and thereupon was manifested that holy and real Trinity, which to us is one God. For the Lord went up out of the water, as we read in the Gospel: And, lo, the heavens were opened."</div></div><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>Cyril of Jerusalem</strong> (s.IV (c.313-386)) <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.33]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"This Holy Spirit came down when the Lord was baptized so that the dignity of him who was baptized might not be hidden. The heavens too were opened because of the dignity of him who descended."</div></div><div class="patr-resumen">Los padres ven en el bautismo de Cristo una teofanía trinitaria donde se manifiestan claramente las tres personas divinas en su obra salvífica.</div></div></div></div></div><div class="verse-block" id="vb33"><div class="verse-header"><span class="vnum">33</span></div><div class="greek-line" id="greek-33">κἀγὼ οὐκ ᾔδειν αὐτόν ἀλλ᾽ ὁ πέμψας με βαπτίζειν ἐν ὕδατι ἐκεῖνός μοι εἶπεν Ἐφ᾽ ὃν ἂν ἴδῃς τὸ Πνεῦμα καταβαῖνον καὶ μένον ἐπ᾽ αὐτόν οὗτός ἐστιν ὁ βαπτίζων ἐν Πνεύματι Ἁγίῳ</div><div class="uncial-line" id="uncial-33" style="display:none;font-family:'Noto Serif',serif;font-size:0.9rem;letter-spacing:1.5px;color:#5d4037;background:#efebe9;padding:6px 10px;border-radius:4px;margin-bottom:0.3rem;overflow-wrap:break-word" title="Así se vería este versículo en un manuscrito uncial del siglo IV — todo mayúsculas, sin espacios (puntos medios añadidos como ayuda visual)">ΚΑΓΩ·ΟΥΚ·ΗΙΔΕΙΝ·ΑΥΤΟΝ·ΑΛΛ·Ο·ΠΕΜΨΑΣ·ΜΕ·ΒΑΠΤΙΖΕΙΝ·ΕΝ·ΥΔΑΤΙ·ΕΚΕΙΝΟΣ·ΜΟΙ·ΕΙΠΕΝ·ΕΦ·ΟΝ·ΑΝ·ΙΔΗΙΣ·ΤΟ·ΠΝΕΥΜΑ·ΚΑΤΑΒΑΙΝΟΝ·ΚΑΙ·ΜΕΝΟΝ·ΕΠ·ΑΥΤΟΝ·ΟΥΤΟΣ·ΕΣΤΙΝ·Ο·ΒΑΠΤΙΖΩΝ·ΕΝ·ΠΝΕΥΜΑΤΙ·ΑΓΙΩΙ<span style="display:block;font-size:0.65rem;margin-top:4px;letter-spacing:0;opacity:0.7"><a href="https://www.codexsinaiticus.org/en/manuscript.aspx?book=33&chapter=1&verse=33" target="_blank" style="color:#5d4037">Ver en Codex Sinaiticus →</a></span></div><div style="display:flex;gap:0.3rem;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:0.3rem;align-items:center"><div class="rvr-btn" onclick="toggleRVR(33)">RVR60</div><div class="rvr-btn" onclick="document.getElementById('uncial-33').style.display=document.getElementById('uncial-33').style.display==='none'?'':'none'" style="background:#efebe9;color:#5d4037;border-color:#bcaaa4" title="Escritura uncial: así se veía el NT en los manuscritos del s.IV (Vaticanus, Sinaiticus). Todo mayúsculas, sin espacios entre palabras (scriptio continua), sin acentos ni puntuación. La iota subscrita se escribe explícitamente.">Uncial</div><div class="rvr-btn" onclick="document.getElementById('pron-33').style.display=document.getElementById('pron-33').style.display==='none'?'':'none'" style="background:#e3f2fd;color:#1565c0;border-color:#90caf9" title="Pronunciación bizantina aproximada (griego moderno/eclesiástico)">Pronunciación</div><div class="rvr-btn vbtn-patr" onclick="toggleSection('patr-33')" title="43 citas patrísticas disponibles">✝ 43 patrística</div><div class="rvr-btn vbtn-exeg" onclick="toggleSection('exeg-33')" title="2 comentarios exegéticos">📚 2 exégesis</div><div class="rvr-btn vbtn-ver" onclick="document.getElementById('vers-33').classList.toggle('collapsed')" title="9 versiones disponibles">📖 versiones</div><div class="rvr-btn" onclick="document.getElementById('xrefs-33').classList.toggle('collapsed')" title="1 referencias cruzadas">🔗 1 refs</div><div class="rvr-btn" onclick="copyVerse(33)" title="Copiar versículo al portapapeles" style="background:#f3e5f5;border-color:#ce93d8;color:#6a1b9a">📋</div><div class="rvr-btn" onclick="document.getElementById('notes-33').style.display=document.getElementById('notes-33').style.display==='none'?'':'none'" title="Notas personales" style="background:#fff8e1;border-color:#ffd54f;color:#f57f17">📝</div></div><div id="rvr-33" class="collapsed spanish-line">Y yo no le conocía; pero el que me envió a bautizar con agua, aquél me dijo: Sobre quien veas descender el Espíritu y que permanece sobre él, ése es el que bautiza con el Espíritu Santo.</div><div id="vers-33" class="collapsed" style="margin-bottom:0.5rem"><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">RVR60</span>Y yo no le conocía; pero el que me envió a bautizar con agua, aquél me dijo: Sobre quien veas descender el Espíritu y que permanece sobre él, ése es el que bautiza con el Espíritu Santo.</div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">RVR1909</span>Y yo no le conocía; mas el que me envió á bautizar con agua, aquél me dijo: Sobre quien vieres descender el Espíritu, y que reposa sobre él, éste es el que bautiza con Espíritu Santo.</div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">KJV</span>And I knew him not: but he that sent me to baptize with water, the same said unto me, Upon whom thou shalt see the Spirit descending, and remaining on him, the same is he which baptizeth with the Holy Ghost.</div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">ASV</span>And I knew him not: but he that sent me to baptize in water, he said unto me, Upon whomsoever thou shalt see the Spirit descending, and abiding upon him, the same is he that baptizeth in the Holy Spirit. </div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">BSB</span>I myself did not know Him, but the One who sent me to baptize with water told me, ‘The man on whom you see the Spirit descend and rest is He who will baptize with the Holy Spirit.’ </div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">Darby</span>And I knew him not; but he who sent me to baptise with water, he said to me, Upon whom thou shalt see the Spirit descending and abiding on him, he it is who baptises with [the] Holy Spirit. </div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">LITV</span>And I did not know Him, but the One sending me to baptize in water, that One said to me, On whomever you see the Spirit coming down and abiding on Him, this is the One baptizing in the Holy Spirit. </div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">YLT</span>and I did not know him, but he who sent me to baptize with water, He said to me, On whomsoever thou mayest see the Spirit coming down, and remaining on him, this is he who is baptizing with the Holy Spirit;</div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">Vulgate</span>et ego nesciebam eum sed qui misit me baptizare in aqua ille mihi dixit super quem videris Spiritum descendentem et manentem super eum hic est qui baptizat in Spiritu Sancto</div></div><div id="xrefs-33" class="collapsed xref-container" style="margin-bottom:0.5rem"><span class="xref-pill" onclick="showXrefPopup(this)" data-ref="Acts 1:5" data-es="Porque Juan á la verdad bautizó con agua, mas vosotros seréis bautizados con el Espíritu Santo no muchos días después de estos." data-gr="ὅτι Ἰωάννης μὲν ἐβάπτισεν ὕδατι, ὑμεῖς δὲ ⸂ἐν πνεύματι βαπτισθήσεσθε⸃ ἁγίῳ οὐ μετὰ πολλὰς ταύτας ἡμέρας." data-lxx="" title="Porque Juan á la verdad bautizó con agua, mas vosotros seréis bautizados con el …">Acts 1:5</span></div><div id="pron-33" style="display:none;font-size:0.85rem;color:#1565c0;background:#e3f2fd;padding:4px 8px;border-radius:4px;margin-bottom:0.3rem;font-style:italic" class="pron-line"></div><div id="notes-33" style="display:none;margin-bottom:0.5rem"><textarea id="note-input-33" style="width:100%;min-height:50px;border:1px solid #ccc;border-radius:6px;padding:4px 8px;font-size:0.82rem;resize:vertical" placeholder="Tus notas para v.33..." oninput="saveNote(33)"></textarea></div><div class="section-toggle" onclick="toggleSection('exeg-33')" role="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="exeg-33" tabindex="0"><span class="section-icon">📜</span> Exégesis del Griego <span class="arrow" id="arrow-exeg-33">▶</span></div><div id="exeg-33" class="collapsed section-content exeg-section" role="region"><div class="exeg-summary"><strong style="color:#1b5e20">Bengel:</strong> Οὐκ ᾔδειν ) I knew Him not , before that I saw the Spirit descending.<br><strong style="color:#1b5e20">Robertson:</strong> He said ( εκεινος ειπεν ).<br></div><div class="comm-item"><div class="comm-header" onclick="document.getElementById('uexv33_0').classList.toggle('collapsed')"><strong>Bengel's Gnomon</strong> <span style=color:#888;font-size:0.75rem>(1742)</span><span class="small-arrow">ver completo ▼</span></div><div id="uexv33_0" class="collapsed comm-body">[John 1:33] . Οὐκ ᾔδειν ) I knew Him not , before that I saw the Spirit descending. ὁ πέμψας με , He that sent me ) God.</div></div><div class="comm-item"><div class="comm-header" onclick="document.getElementById('uexv33_1').classList.toggle('collapsed')"><strong>Robertson's Word Pictures</strong> <span style=color:#888;font-size:0.75rem>(1933)</span><span class="small-arrow">ver completo ▼</span></div><div id="uexv33_1" class="collapsed comm-body">He said ( εκεινος ειπεν ). Explicit and emphatic pronoun as in verse [John 1:8], referring to God as the one who sent John (verse [John 1:6]). With the Holy Spirit ( εν πνευματ αγιω ). "In the Holy Spirit." Here again one needs the background of the Synoptics for the contrast between John's baptism in water ([John 1:26]) and that of the Messiah in the Holy Spirit ([Mark 1:8]; [Matthew 3:11]; [Luke 3:16]).</div></div></div><div class="section-toggle" onclick="toggleSection('patr-33')" role="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="patr-33" tabindex="0"><span class="section-icon">👨‍🏫</span> Patrística · 4 temas · 43 citas <span class="arrow" id="arrow-patr-33">▶</span></div><div id="patr-33" class="collapsed section-content patr-section" role="region"><div class="patr-theme"><div class="patr-theme-header" onclick="document.getElementById('ptbcd7606e').classList.toggle('collapsed');this.querySelector('.arrow').classList.toggle('open')"><strong>Conocimiento de Juan sobre Cristo</strong><div class="consensus-bar" style="width:60px"><div style="width:55%;background:#ff9800"></div></div><span class="consensus-label" style="color:#ff9800">medio</span><span class="arrow">▼</span></div><div id="ptbcd7606e" class="collapsed" style="padding:0.5rem 0.2rem 0"><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#ff9800"><div class="patr-cite-header">⚠️ <strong>John Chrysostom</strong> (s.IV (c.349-407)) <span class="patr-meta">— Patriarca de Constantinopla, "Boca de Oro", discípulo de Diodoro de Tarso (Antioquía/Constantinopla)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Juan_Cris%C3%B3stomo" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.31]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"And I knew Him not, he saith. Here he renders his testimony free from suspicion, by showing that it was not from human friendship, but had been caused by divine revelation. I knew Him not, he saith. How then couldest thou be a trustworthy witness? How shalt thou teach others, while thou thyself art ignorant? He did not say I know Him not, but, I knew Him not; so that in this way he would be shown most trustworthy; for why should he have shown favor to one of whom he was ignorant?"</div></div><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#ff9800"><div class="patr-cite-header">⚠️ <strong>Augustine of Hippo</strong> (s.IV-V (354-430)) <span class="patr-meta">— Obispo de Hipona, Doctor de la Iglesia, discípulo de Ambrosio de Milán (Hipona)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agust%C3%ADn_de_Hipona" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.33]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"Did John hear this that he might know Him whom he had not known, or that he might more fully know Him whom he had already known? For if he had been entirely ignorant of Him, he would not have said to Him when He came to the river to be baptized, I have need to be baptized of Thee, and comest Thou to me? He knew Him therefore."</div></div><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#ff9800"><div class="patr-cite-header">⚠️ <strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> (s.V (c.376-444)) <span class="patr-meta">— Patriarca de Alejandría, Concilio de Éfeso (Alejandría)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cirilo_de_Alejandr%C3%ADa" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.31]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"He that leaped in the depth of the womb of his mother at the voice of the Holy Virgin while yet bearing the Lord, prophet before the travail-pang, disciple in the womb, says of the Saviour, I knew Him not, and says truly, for he does not lie. For God knows all things of Himself and untaught, but the creature, by being taught."</div></div><div class="patr-resumen">Existe debate sobre qué exactamente Juan no conocía de Cristo: algunos padres sugieren ignorancia total previa, otros que conocía su identidad pero no su autoridad bautismal específica.</div></div></div><div class="patr-theme"><div class="patr-theme-header" onclick="document.getElementById('pt054e64bc').classList.toggle('collapsed');this.querySelector('.arrow').classList.toggle('open')"><strong>Autoridad bautismal de Cristo</strong><div class="consensus-bar" style="width:60px"><div style="width:85%;background:#4caf50"></div></div><span class="consensus-label" style="color:#4caf50">alto</span><span class="arrow">▼</span></div><div id="pt054e64bc" class="collapsed" style="padding:0.5rem 0.2rem 0"><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>Augustine of Hippo</strong> (s.IV-V (354-430)) <span class="patr-meta">— Obispo de Hipona, Doctor de la Iglesia, discípulo de Ambrosio de Milán (Hipona)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agust%C3%ADn_de_Hipona" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.33]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"Now this it was in Him that John saw, and came to know which he did not know. Not that he did not know Him to be the Son of God, or that he did not know Him to be the Lord, or not know Him to be the Christ; or that he did not know this too, that it was He who should baptize with water and with the Holy Ghost. This he did know; but that he should do this so as to retain the authority to Himself and transfer it to none of His ministers, this is what he learnt in the dove."</div></div><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>Augustine of Hippo</strong> (s.IV-V (354-430)) <span class="patr-meta">— Obispo de Hipona, Doctor de la Iglesia, discípulo de Ambrosio de Milán (Hipona)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agust%C3%ADn_de_Hipona" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.33]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"Therefore, lest as many baptisms should be spoken of as there are servants who received power from the Lord to baptize, the Lord kept to Himself the power of baptizing, and gave to His servants the ministry. The servant says that he baptizes; he says so rightly, as the apostle says, And I baptized also the household of Stephanas; but as a servant."</div></div><div class="patr-resumen">Los padres enseñan que Cristo retiene la autoridad bautismal mientras delega el ministerio, asegurando que todo bautismo válido es efectivamente de Cristo independientemente del ministro.</div></div></div><div class="patr-theme"><div class="patr-theme-header" onclick="document.getElementById('pt342cd32c').classList.toggle('collapsed');this.querySelector('.arrow').classList.toggle('open')"><strong>Manifestación de la Trinidad</strong><div class="consensus-bar" style="width:60px"><div style="width:85%;background:#4caf50"></div></div><span class="consensus-label" style="color:#4caf50">alto</span><span class="arrow">▼</span></div><div id="pt342cd32c" class="collapsed" style="padding:0.5rem 0.2rem 0"><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>Augustine of Hippo</strong> (s.IV-V (354-430)) <span class="patr-meta">— Obispo de Hipona, Doctor de la Iglesia, discípulo de Ambrosio de Milán (Hipona)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agust%C3%ADn_de_Hipona" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.32]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"See, the dove descended upon the Lord, upon the Lord when baptized: and thereupon was manifested that holy and real Trinity, which to us is one God. For the Lord went up out of the water, as we read in the Gospel: And, lo, the heavens were opened."</div></div><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>Cyril of Jerusalem</strong> (s.IV (c.313-386)) <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.33]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"This Holy Spirit came down when the Lord was baptized so that the dignity of him who was baptized might not be hidden. The heavens too were opened because of the dignity of him who descended."</div></div><div class="patr-resumen">Los padres ven en el bautismo de Cristo una teofanía trinitaria donde se manifiestan claramente las tres personas divinas en su obra salvífica.</div></div></div><div class="patr-theme"><div class="patr-theme-header" onclick="document.getElementById('ptad9a1622').classList.toggle('collapsed');this.querySelector('.arrow').classList.toggle('open')"><strong>Validez del bautismo independiente del ministro</strong><div class="consensus-bar" style="width:60px"><div style="width:85%;background:#4caf50"></div></div><span class="consensus-label" style="color:#4caf50">alto</span><span class="arrow">▼</span></div><div id="ptad9a1622" class="collapsed" style="padding:0.5rem 0.2rem 0"><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>Augustine of Hippo</strong> (s.IV-V (354-430)) <span class="patr-meta">— Obispo de Hipona, Doctor de la Iglesia, discípulo de Ambrosio de Milán (Hipona)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agust%C3%ADn_de_Hipona" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.33]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"Therefore, if even he be bad, and he happen to have the ministration of baptism, and if men do not know him, but God knows him, God, who has kept the power of baptizing to Himself, baptizes by means of such a minister."</div></div><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>Augustine of Hippo</strong> (s.IV-V (354-430)) <span class="patr-meta">— Obispo de Hipona, Doctor de la Iglesia, discípulo de Ambrosio de Milán (Hipona)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agust%C3%ADn_de_Hipona" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.33]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"Let not the dove shrink from the ministration of the bad, but have regard to the power of the Lord. Whether the minister be good or bad, the power is the Lord's, the ministry is the servant's."</div></div><div class="patr-resumen">Los padres, especialmente Agustín, defienden que la validez del bautismo depende de Cristo, no de la santidad del ministro, estableciendo un principio sacramental fundamental.</div></div></div></div></div><div class="verse-block" id="vb34"><div class="verse-header"><span class="vnum">34</span></div><div class="greek-line" id="greek-34">κἀγὼ ἑώρακα καὶ μεμαρτύρηκα ὅτι οὗτός ἐστιν ὁ Υἱὸς τοῦ Θεοῦ</div><div class="uncial-line" id="uncial-34" style="display:none;font-family:'Noto Serif',serif;font-size:0.9rem;letter-spacing:1.5px;color:#5d4037;background:#efebe9;padding:6px 10px;border-radius:4px;margin-bottom:0.3rem;overflow-wrap:break-word" title="Así se vería este versículo en un manuscrito uncial del siglo IV — todo mayúsculas, sin espacios (puntos medios añadidos como ayuda visual)">ΚΑΓΩ·ΕΩΡΑΚΑ·ΚΑΙ·ΜΕΜΑΡΤΥΡΗΚΑ·ΟΤΙ·ΟΥΤΟΣ·ΕΣΤΙΝ·Ο·ΥΙΟΣ·ΤΟΥ·ΘΕΟΥ<span style="display:block;font-size:0.65rem;margin-top:4px;letter-spacing:0;opacity:0.7"><a href="https://www.codexsinaiticus.org/en/manuscript.aspx?book=33&chapter=1&verse=34" target="_blank" style="color:#5d4037">Ver en Codex Sinaiticus →</a></span></div><div style="display:flex;gap:0.3rem;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:0.3rem;align-items:center"><div class="rvr-btn" onclick="toggleRVR(34)">RVR60</div><div class="rvr-btn" onclick="document.getElementById('uncial-34').style.display=document.getElementById('uncial-34').style.display==='none'?'':'none'" style="background:#efebe9;color:#5d4037;border-color:#bcaaa4" title="Escritura uncial: así se veía el NT en los manuscritos del s.IV (Vaticanus, Sinaiticus). Todo mayúsculas, sin espacios entre palabras (scriptio continua), sin acentos ni puntuación. La iota subscrita se escribe explícitamente.">Uncial</div><div class="rvr-btn" onclick="document.getElementById('pron-34').style.display=document.getElementById('pron-34').style.display==='none'?'':'none'" style="background:#e3f2fd;color:#1565c0;border-color:#90caf9" title="Pronunciación bizantina aproximada (griego moderno/eclesiástico)">Pronunciación</div><div class="rvr-btn vbtn-patr" onclick="toggleSection('patr-34')" title="6 citas patrísticas disponibles">✝ 6 patrística</div><div class="rvr-btn vbtn-exeg" onclick="toggleSection('exeg-34')" title="3 comentarios exegéticos">📚 3 exégesis</div><div class="rvr-btn vbtn-ver" onclick="document.getElementById('vers-34').classList.toggle('collapsed')" title="9 versiones disponibles">📖 versiones</div><div class="rvr-btn" onclick="copyVerse(34)" title="Copiar versículo al portapapeles" style="background:#f3e5f5;border-color:#ce93d8;color:#6a1b9a">📋</div><div class="rvr-btn" onclick="document.getElementById('notes-34').style.display=document.getElementById('notes-34').style.display==='none'?'':'none'" title="Notas personales" style="background:#fff8e1;border-color:#ffd54f;color:#f57f17">📝</div></div><div id="rvr-34" class="collapsed spanish-line">Y yo le vi, y he dado testimonio de que éste es el Hijo de Dios. Los primeros discípulos</div><div id="vers-34" class="collapsed" style="margin-bottom:0.5rem"><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">RVR60</span>Y yo le vi, y he dado testimonio de que éste es el Hijo de Dios. Los primeros discípulos</div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">RVR1909</span>Y yo le vi, y he dado testimonio que éste es el Hijo de Dios.</div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">KJV</span>And I saw, and bare record that this is the Son of God.</div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">ASV</span>And I have seen, and have borne witness that this is the Son of God. </div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">BSB</span>I have seen and testified that this is the Son of God.” </div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">Darby</span>And I have seen and borne witness that this is the Son ofGod. </div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">LITV</span>And I have seen and have witnessed that this One is the Son of God. </div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">YLT</span>and I have seen, and have testified, that this is the Son of God.'</div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">Vulgate</span>et ego vidi et testimonium perhibui quia hic est Filius Dei</div></div><div id="pron-34" style="display:none;font-size:0.85rem;color:#1565c0;background:#e3f2fd;padding:4px 8px;border-radius:4px;margin-bottom:0.3rem;font-style:italic" class="pron-line"></div><div id="notes-34" style="display:none;margin-bottom:0.5rem"><textarea id="note-input-34" style="width:100%;min-height:50px;border:1px solid #ccc;border-radius:6px;padding:4px 8px;font-size:0.82rem;resize:vertical" placeholder="Tus notas para v.34..." oninput="saveNote(34)"></textarea></div><div class="section-toggle" onclick="toggleSection('exeg-34')" role="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="exeg-34" tabindex="0"><span class="section-icon">📜</span> Exégesis del Griego <span class="arrow" id="arrow-exeg-34">▶</span></div><div id="exeg-34" class="collapsed section-content exeg-section" role="region"><div class="exeg-summary"><strong style="color:#1b5e20">Alford:</strong> The token must have been given to the Baptist by a special revelation , which also revealed to him his own errand and office; so [Luke 3:2] , ἐγένετο ῥῆμα θεοῦ ἐπὶ Ἰωάννην τὸν Ζαχ.<br><strong style="color:#1b5e20">Bengel:</strong> Ἑώρακα ) I saw the Spirit descending.<br><strong style="color:#1b5e20">Robertson:</strong> I have seen ( εωρακα ).<br></div><div class="comm-item"><div class="comm-header" onclick="document.getElementById('uexv34_0').classList.toggle('collapsed')"><strong>Alford's Greek Testament</strong> <span style=color:#888;font-size:0.75rem>(1863)</span><span class="small-arrow">ver completo ▼</span></div><div id="uexv34_0" class="collapsed comm-body">34. ] A solemn reiteration of his testimony, after the mention of the giving of this token by Him who sent him; And I have seen ( accordingly ) &c. The token must have been given to the Baptist by a special revelation , which also revealed to him his own errand and office; so [Luke 3:2] , ἐγένετο ῥῆμα θεοῦ ἐπὶ Ἰωάννην τὸν Ζαχ . υἱὸν ἐν τῇ ἐρήμῳ . μεμαρτύρηκα is stronger than μαρτυρῶ I have seen (on the perf. see above, Joh 1:32 ) and have borne testimony it is a reference to his testimony at the time, as a thing on record in their memories, and as still continuing. ὁ υἱ . τ . θεοῦ (see Joh 1:18 ) = the λόγος made flesh, the Messiah. On the import of the descent of the Spirit on Jesus at His baptism, those who can do so should consult Lücke&rsquo;s very able Excursus, i. 433 443. In this commentary, see notes on [Luke 2:41-52] . I may just remark, that the Personal Logos, Who σὰρξ ἐγένετο in our Lord, and was subjected to all the laws of human development in infancy, childhood, youth, evermore in an especial degree under the leading of the Holy Spirit, by whose agency the Incarnation had taken place, was the Recipient ( τὸ δεχόμενον ) of this fulness of the indwelling of the Holy Ghost: and that herein consisted the real depth and propriety of this sign; the abiding of the Spirit without measure (ch. Joh 3:34 ) on Him indicated beyond doubt that He was the λόγος σὰρξ γεγονώς , for no mere human intelligence could be thus receptive of the Holy Spirit of God; we receive Him only as we can , only as far as our receptivity extends, by measure; but HE, into the very fulness and infinite capacities of His Divine Being.</div></div><div class="comm-item"><div class="comm-header" onclick="document.getElementById('uexv34_1').classList.toggle('collapsed')"><strong>Bengel's Gnomon</strong> <span style=color:#888;font-size:0.75rem>(1742)</span><span class="small-arrow">ver completo ▼</span></div><div id="uexv34_1" class="collapsed comm-body">[John 1:34] . Ἑώρακα ) I saw the Spirit descending. καὶ ) and thence [in consequence]. μεμαρτύρηκα ) I became a witness [I bare record]. ὁ Ὑιὸς τοῦ Θεοῦ , the Son of God ) And so the Messiah. The reference is to that which is stated: [Matthew 3:7] . [ Namely, how Jesus in His baptism, teas proclaimed the Son of GOD, and in His temptation asserted Himself to be the Son of GOD: thus this very truth, that He is the Son of GOD, is explained more at length in the first verses. Also these verses have a fitting place here, as intended to designate that Person, of whom John the Baptist bare record, by the mission divinely entrusted to him. The events which precede the entrance (the coming forward) of John the Baptist, namely, the nativity and baptism of Christ, etc., these the Evangelist has most dexterously interwoven with the rest . Harm ., p. 154.]</div></div><div class="comm-item"><div class="comm-header" onclick="document.getElementById('uexv34_2').classList.toggle('collapsed')"><strong>Robertson's Word Pictures</strong> <span style=color:#888;font-size:0.75rem>(1933)</span><span class="small-arrow">ver completo ▼</span></div><div id="uexv34_2" class="collapsed comm-body">I have seen ( εωρακα ). Present perfect active of οραω . John repeats the statement of verse [John 1:32] ( τεθεαμα ). Have borne witness ( μεμαρτυρηκα ). Perfect active indicative of μαρτυρεω for which verb see [John 1:32]. This is the Son of God ( ο υιος του θεου ). The Baptist saw the Spirit come on Jesus at his baptism and undoubtedly heard the Father's voice hail him as "My Beloved Son" ([Mark 1:11]; [Matthew 3:17]; [Luke 3:22]). Nathanael uses it as a Messianic title ([John 1:49]) as does Martha ([John 11:27]). The Synoptics use it also of Christ ([Mark 3:11]; [Matthew 14:33]; [Luke 22:70]). Caiaphas employs it to Christ as a Messianic title ([Matthew 26:63]) and Jesus confessed under oath that he was (verse [Matthew 26:64]), thus applying the term to himself as he does in John's Gospel ([John 5:25]; [John 10:36]; [John 11:4]) and by implication (the Father, the Son) in [Matthew 11:27] ([Luke 10:22]). Hence in the Synoptics also Jesus calls himself the Son of God. The phrase means more than just Messiah and expresses the peculiar relation of the Son to the Father ([John 3:18]; [John 5:25]; [John 17:5]; [John 19:7]; [John 20:31]) like that of the Logos with God in [John 1:1].</div></div></div><div class="section-toggle" onclick="toggleSection('patr-34')" role="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="patr-34" tabindex="0"><span class="section-icon">👨‍🏫</span> Patrística · 1 temas · 6 citas <span class="arrow" id="arrow-patr-34">▶</span></div><div id="patr-34" class="collapsed section-content patr-section" role="region"><div class="patr-theme"><div class="patr-theme-header" onclick="document.getElementById('ptefdf3820').classList.toggle('collapsed');this.querySelector('.arrow').classList.toggle('open')"><strong>Testimonio del Bautista sobre la filiación divina de Cristo</strong><div class="consensus-bar" style="width:60px"><div style="width:85%;background:#4caf50"></div></div><span class="consensus-label" style="color:#4caf50">alto</span><span class="arrow">▼</span></div><div id="ptefdf3820" class="collapsed" style="padding:0.5rem 0.2rem 0"><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>Augustine of Hippo</strong> (s.IV-V (354-430)) <span class="patr-meta">— Obispo de Hipona, Doctor de la Iglesia, discípulo de Ambrosio de Milán (Hipona)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agust%C3%ADn_de_Hipona" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.34]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"John bare record because he saw. What record did he bare? "That this is the Son of God." It behoved, then, that He should baptize who is God's only Son, not His adopted son. Adopted sons are the ministers of the only Son: the only Son has power; the adopted, the ministry."</div></div><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> (s.V (c.376-444)) <span class="patr-meta">— Patriarca de Alejandría, Concilio de Éfeso (Alejandría)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cirilo_de_Alejandr%C3%ADa" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.34]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"Sure is the witness; who, what he hath actually seen, that he also speaketh. For haply he was not ignorant of that which is written, That which thine eyes have seen, tell. I saw then, says he, the sign, and understood That Which was signified by it. I bear record that this is the Son of God, Who was proclaimed by the Law that is through Moses, and heralded by the voice of the holy Prophets."</div></div><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>Thomas Aquinas</strong> (s.XIII (1225-1274)) <span class="patr-meta">— Teólogo escolástico, Doctor de la Iglesia, discípulo de Alberto Magno (Italia)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tom%C3%A1s_de_Aquino" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.34]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"Then he shows what the Baptist understood from this vision, that is, that Christ is the Son of God. And this is what he says, Now I have seen for myself, that is, the Spirit coming down on him, and have given testimony that he, that is, Christ, is the Son of God, that is, the true and natural Son."</div></div><div class="patr-resumen">Los Padres concuerdan unánimemente en que Juan el Bautista dio testimonio de que Jesús es el verdadero Hijo de Dios, no adoptivo. Este testimonio se basa en la visión del Espíritu descendiendo sobre Cristo.</div></div></div></div><div class="section-toggle" onclick="toggleSection('tc-34')" role="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="tc-34" tabindex="0"><span class="section-icon">⚖️</span> Crítica Textual <span style="font-size:0.7rem;color:#ff9800">(3 lecturas)</span> <span class="arrow" id="arrow-tc-34">▶</span></div><div id="tc-34" class="collapsed section-content tc-section"><div class="tc-verdict" style="margin-bottom:0.8rem"><div class="tc-verdict-text"><strong>Veredicto:</strong> La lectura #10.1 (ὁ υἱός) es probablemente original, con fuerte apoyo de manuscritos tempranos como P66, P75, P120, א2, A, B. La lectura #10.2 (ὁ ἐκλεκτός) representa una variante teológicamente significativa que enfatiza la elección divina, posiblemente influenciada por Lucas 9:35. La lectura #10.3 (ὁ ἐκλεκτὸς υἱός) es una combinación conflacionista que intenta preservar ambas tradiciones.</div><div class="consensus-bar" style="max-width:200px"><div style="width:90%;background:#4caf50"></div></div><span style="font-size:0.72rem;color:#4caf50;font-weight:600;margin-left:4px">Confianza: alta</span><div class="tc-impact"><strong>Impacto:</strong> La diferencia entre 'Hijo de Dios' y 'Elegido de Dios' refleja diferentes énfasis cristológicos: filiación divina versus elección mesiánica. Ambos títulos son apropiados para Cristo, pero 'Hijo' es más característico del vocabulario joánico.</div></div><div style="overflow-x:auto;margin-bottom:0.5rem"><table class="tc-table"><tr class="tc-header"><th class="tc-ms-col">MS</th><th class="tc-ctx">καὶ</th><th class="tc-ctx">μεμαρτύρηκα</th><th class="tc-var-col" title="Columna variante 1: ὁ">⚡1</th><th class="tc-var-col" title="Columna variante 2: υἱός">⚡2</th><th class="tc-var-col" title="Columna variante 3: υἱός">⚡3</th><th class="tc-ctx">ὁ</th><th class="tc-ctx">Υἱὸς</th></tr><tr><td class="tc-ms-cell" onclick="showMSSPanel('𝔓66, 75, 120 א2 A B C L Wsupp Δ Θ Ψ 083 0141 0233vid f1 f13 28 33 157 180 205 565 579 597 700 892 1006 1010 1071 1241 1243 1292 1342 1424 1505 Byz [E F G H N P] Lect itaur, c, f, l, q vg syrp, h, palmss copbo arm eth geo slav Origen Asterius Chrysostom Cyril John-Damascus; Augustine3/4')"><span class="ms-chip" style="background:#b71c1c;color:white;font-weight:700" data-s="𝔓66" onclick="event.stopPropagation();showMSInfo(this,'𝔓66');">𝔓66</span><span class="ms-chip" style="background:#e3f2fd;color:#1565c0" data-s="75" onclick="event.stopPropagation();showMSInfo(this,'75');">75</span><span class="ms-chip" style="background:#e3f2fd;color:#1565c0" data-s="120" onclick="event.stopPropagation();showMSInfo(this,'120');">120</span><span class="ms-chip" style="background:#1565c0;color:white;font-w