);showMSInfo(this,'1424');">1424</span><span class="ms-chip" style="background:#f3e5f5;color:#6a1b9a" data-s="vgms" onclick="event.stopPropagation();showMSInfo(this,'vgms');">vgms</span><span class="ms-chip" style="background:#f3e5f5;color:#6a1b9a" data-s="syrc" onclick="event.stopPropagation();showMSInfo(this,'syrc');">syrc</span><span class="ms-chip" style="background:#fff3e0;color:#e65100" title=": &ldquo;We have declared, then, why it was at the tenth hour. Let us see what follows: &quot;One of the two which heard John speak, and followed Him, was Andr...&rdquo;" data-s="Augustine" onclick="event.stopPropagation();showMSInfo(this,'Augustine');">Augustine</span></td><td class="tc-word-cell tc-interactive" style="background:#f5f5f5" data-tip="Está hallando" data-lemma="εὑρίσκω" data-morph="V-PAI-3S" data-strongs="G2147">εὑρίσκει</td><td class="tc-word-cell tc-interactive" style="background:#f5f5f5" data-tip="este" data-lemma="οὗτος" data-morph="D-NSM" data-strongs="G3778">οὗτος</td><td class="tc-word-cell" style="background:#eee"></td><td class="tc-word-cell tc-interactive" style="background:#f5f5f5" data-tip="a el" data-lemma="ὁ" data-morph="T-ASM" data-strongs="G3588">τὸν</td><td class="tc-word-cell tc-interactive" style="background:#f5f5f5" data-tip="hermano" data-lemma="ἀδελφός" data-morph="N-ASM" data-strongs="G80">ἀδελφὸν</td></tr></table></div><div style="font-size:0.72rem;color:#555;margin:0.3rem 0;padding:0.3rem 0.5rem;background:#f5f5f5;border-radius:4px"><strong>Lecturas:</strong> <span style="margin-right:0.8rem">#1: <span style="font-family:serif;color:#1b5e20">πρῶτον</span></span><span style="margin-right:0.8rem">#2: <span style="font-family:serif;color:#1b5e20">πρῶτος</span></span><span style="margin-right:0.8rem">#3: <span style="font-family:serif;color:#1b5e20">πρωΐ</span></span><span style="margin-right:0.8rem">#4: <span style="font-family:serif;color:#1b5e20">[omisión]</span></span></div><div style="font-size:0.63rem;color:#999;margin-top:2px;display:flex;gap:0.5rem;flex-wrap:wrap"><span style="font-weight:600">MSS:</span> <span>🔴 Papiros</span> <span>🔵 Unciales</span> <span>⚪ Minúsc.</span> <span>🟣 Versiones</span> <span>🟠 Padres</span> <span>🟢 Byz/Lect</span></div><div class="tc-criteria-expanded"><div class="tc-criterion-item"><strong>Atestación temprana</strong><div class="tc-criterion-exp">Los manuscritos más antiguos y de mejor calidad apoyan πρῶτον. Las otras variantes aparecen en tradiciones textuales más limitadas.</div></div><div class="tc-criterion-item"><strong>Lectio difficilior</strong><div class="tc-criterion-exp">πρῶτον es más difícil porque deja implícito qué hizo Andrés 'después'. Las otras lecturas intentan clarificar o cambiar este sentido ambiguo.</div></div></div><div style="font-size:0.72rem;color:#1565c0;margin-top:0.5rem;cursor:pointer" onclick="document.getElementById('exeg-41')?.classList.remove('collapsed');document.getElementById('exeg-41')?.scrollIntoView({behavior:'smooth'})">↑ Ver análisis exegético de este versículo</div><div style="margin-top:0.8rem;text-align:center"><a href="tc_deep_dive_John_1_41.html" onclick="if(!this.dataset.ready){this.textContent='⏳ Generando análisis profundo...';this.style.opacity=0.6;return true;}" style="display:inline-block;padding:8px 20px;background:linear-gradient(135deg,#1a237e,#0d47a1);color:white;border-radius:8px;text-decoration:none;font-size:0.85rem;font-weight:600;box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(26,35,126,0.3)">🔍 Deep Dive — Análisis completo de esta variante</a><div style="font-size:0.7rem;color:#888;margin-top:3px">Si la página no existe, usa el tool <code>critical_apparatus</code> → <code>tc_deep_dive</code> en el MCP server para generarla.</div></div></div></div><div class="verse-block" id="vb42"><div class="verse-header"><span class="vnum">42</span> <span style="font-size:0.65rem;color:#c62828;font-weight:600" title="Palabras de Cristo (Verba Christi)">✝</span></div><div class="greek-line" id="greek-42" style="color:#c62828">ἤγαγεν αὐτὸν πρὸς τὸν Ἰησοῦν ἐμβλέψας αὐτῷ ὁ Ἰησοῦς εἶπεν Σὺ εἶ Σίμων ὁ υἱὸς Ἰωάννου σὺ κληθήσῃ Κηφᾶς ὃ ἑρμηνεύεται Πέτρος</div><div class="uncial-line" id="uncial-42" style="display:none;font-family:'Noto Serif',serif;font-size:0.9rem;letter-spacing:1.5px;color:#5d4037;background:#efebe9;padding:6px 10px;border-radius:4px;margin-bottom:0.3rem;overflow-wrap:break-word" title="Así se vería este versículo en un manuscrito uncial del siglo IV — todo mayúsculas, sin espacios (puntos medios añadidos como ayuda visual)">ΗΓΑΓΕΝ·ΑΥΤΟΝ·ΠΡΟΣ·ΤΟΝ·ΙΗΣΟΥΝ·ΕΜΒΛΕΨΑΣ·ΑΥΤΩΙ·Ο·ΙΗΣΟΥΣ·ΕΙΠΕΝ·ΣΥ·ΕΙ·ΣΙΜΩΝ·Ο·ΥΙΟΣ·ΙΩΑΝΝΟΥ·ΣΥ·ΚΛΗΘΗΣΗΙ·ΚΗΦΑΣ·Ο·ΕΡΜΗΝΕΥΕΤΑΙ·ΠΕΤΡΟΣ<span style="display:block;font-size:0.65rem;margin-top:4px;letter-spacing:0;opacity:0.7"><a href="https://www.codexsinaiticus.org/en/manuscript.aspx?book=33&chapter=1&verse=42" target="_blank" style="color:#5d4037">Ver en Codex Sinaiticus →</a></span></div><div style="display:flex;gap:0.3rem;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:0.3rem;align-items:center"><div class="rvr-btn" onclick="toggleRVR(42)">RVR60</div><div class="rvr-btn" onclick="document.getElementById('uncial-42').style.display=document.getElementById('uncial-42').style.display==='none'?'':'none'" style="background:#efebe9;color:#5d4037;border-color:#bcaaa4" title="Escritura uncial: así se veía el NT en los manuscritos del s.IV (Vaticanus, Sinaiticus). Todo mayúsculas, sin espacios entre palabras (scriptio continua), sin acentos ni puntuación. La iota subscrita se escribe explícitamente.">Uncial</div><div class="rvr-btn" onclick="document.getElementById('pron-42').style.display=document.getElementById('pron-42').style.display==='none'?'':'none'" style="background:#e3f2fd;color:#1565c0;border-color:#90caf9" title="Pronunciación bizantina aproximada (griego moderno/eclesiástico)">Pronunciación</div><div class="rvr-btn vbtn-patr" onclick="toggleSection('patr-42')" title="21 citas patrísticas disponibles">✝ 21 patrística</div><div class="rvr-btn vbtn-exeg" onclick="toggleSection('exeg-42')" title="3 comentarios exegéticos">📚 3 exégesis</div><div class="rvr-btn vbtn-ver" onclick="document.getElementById('vers-42').classList.toggle('collapsed')" title="9 versiones disponibles">📖 versiones</div><div class="rvr-btn" onclick="copyVerse(42)" title="Copiar versículo al portapapeles" style="background:#f3e5f5;border-color:#ce93d8;color:#6a1b9a">📋</div><div class="rvr-btn" onclick="document.getElementById('notes-42').style.display=document.getElementById('notes-42').style.display==='none'?'':'none'" title="Notas personales" style="background:#fff8e1;border-color:#ffd54f;color:#f57f17">📝</div></div><div id="rvr-42" class="collapsed spanish-line" style="color:#c62828">Y le trajo a Jesús. Y mirándole Jesús, dijo: Tú eres Simón, hijo de Jonás; tú serás llamado Cefas (que quiere decir, Pedro). Jesús llama a Felipe y a Natanael</div><div id="vers-42" class="collapsed" style="margin-bottom:0.5rem"><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">RVR60</span>Y le trajo a Jesús. Y mirándole Jesús, dijo: Tú eres Simón, hijo de Jonás; tú serás llamado Cefas (que quiere decir, Pedro). Jesús llama a Felipe y a Natanael</div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">RVR1909</span>Y le trajo á Jesús. Y mirándole Jesús, dijo: Tú eres Simón, hijo de Jonás: tú serás llamado Cephas (que quiere decir, Piedra).</div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">KJV</span>And he brought him to Jesus. And when Jesus beheld him, he said, Thou art Simon the son of Jona: thou shalt be called Cephas, which is by interpretation, A stone.</div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">ASV</span>He brought him unto Jesus. Jesus looked upon him, and said, Thou art Simon the son of John: thou shalt be called Cephas (which is by interpretation, Peter). </div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">BSB</span>Andrew brought him to Jesus, who looked at him and said, “You are Simon son of John. You will be called Cephas” (which is translated as Peter). </div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">Darby</span>And he led him to Jesus. Jesus looking at him said, Thou art Simon, the son of Jonas; thou shalt be called Cephas (which interpreted is stone). </div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">LITV</span>And he led him to Jesus. And looking at him, Jesus said, You are Simon the son of Jonah; you shall be called Cephas (which translated is Peter).   </div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">YLT</span>and he brought him unto Jesus: and having looked upon him, Jesus saith, `Thou art Simon, the son of Jonas, thou shalt be called Cephas,' (which is interpreted, A rock.)</div><div class="ver-line"><span class="ver-label">Vulgate</span>et adduxit eum ad Iesum intuitus autem eum Iesus dixit tu es Simon filius Iohanna tu vocaberis Cephas quod interpretatur Petrus</div></div><div id="pron-42" style="display:none;font-size:0.85rem;color:#1565c0;background:#e3f2fd;padding:4px 8px;border-radius:4px;margin-bottom:0.3rem;font-style:italic" class="pron-line"></div><div id="notes-42" style="display:none;margin-bottom:0.5rem"><textarea id="note-input-42" style="width:100%;min-height:50px;border:1px solid #ccc;border-radius:6px;padding:4px 8px;font-size:0.82rem;resize:vertical" placeholder="Tus notas para v.42..." oninput="saveNote(42)"></textarea></div><div class="section-toggle" onclick="toggleSection('exeg-42')" role="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="exeg-42" tabindex="0"><span class="section-icon">📜</span> Exégesis del Griego <span class="arrow" id="arrow-exeg-42">▶</span></div><div id="exeg-42" class="collapsed section-content exeg-section" role="region"><div class="exeg-summary"><strong style="color:#1b5e20">Alford:</strong> ] ἴδιον not merely &ldquo; for the possessive pronoun &rdquo; (according to Winer, § 22.<br><strong style="color:#1b5e20">Bengel:</strong> Σίμων ὁ υἱὸς Ἰωνᾶ , Simon, son of Jona ) These names no one had told the Saviour: and so by this address by name He took complete possession of Peter; comp.<br><strong style="color:#1b5e20">Robertson:</strong> In the ancient Greek πετρα was used for the massive ledge of rock like Stone Mountain while πετρος was a detached fragment of the ledge, though itself large.<br></div><div class="comm-item"><div class="comm-header" onclick="document.getElementById('uexv42_0').classList.toggle('collapsed')"><strong>Alford's Greek Testament</strong> <span style=color:#888;font-size:0.75rem>(1863)</span><span class="small-arrow">ver completo ▼</span></div><div id="uexv42_0" class="collapsed comm-body">42. ] ἴδιον not merely &ldquo; for the possessive pronoun &rdquo; (according to Winer, § 22. 7), but referring to πρῶτον , and furnishing a reason for it. μεσσίαν = מָשִׁיחַ = not ὁ χριστός , but χριστός : being the identification simply of the two words , not here of the two titles .</div></div><div class="comm-item"><div class="comm-header" onclick="document.getElementById('uexv42_1').classList.toggle('collapsed')"><strong>Bengel's Gnomon</strong> <span style=color:#888;font-size:0.75rem>(1742)</span><span class="small-arrow">ver completo ▼</span></div><div id="uexv42_1" class="collapsed comm-body">[John 1:42] . Ἐμβλέψας , having gazed earnestly at him [fixing His eye upon him] An effectual look. Σίμων ὁ υἱὸς Ἰωνᾶ , Simon, son of Jona ) These names no one had told the Saviour: and so by this address by name He took complete possession of Peter; comp. [John 1:48] [His similarly winning Nathanael by showing His omniscience, &ldquo;Before that Philip called thee, when thou wast under the fig-tree, I saw thee.&rdquo;] Κηφᾶς , Cephas ) Peter was ever afterwards called by this name, which is a Syriac one, especially when he was staying in Syria.</div></div><div class="comm-item"><div class="comm-header" onclick="document.getElementById('uexv42_2').classList.toggle('collapsed')"><strong>Robertson's Word Pictures</strong> <span style=color:#888;font-size:0.75rem>(1933)</span><span class="small-arrow">ver completo ▼</span></div><div id="uexv42_2" class="collapsed comm-body">Looked upon him ( εμβλεψας αυτω ). See verse [John 1:36] for same word and form of John's eager gaze at Jesus. Luke uses this word of Jesus when Peter denied him ([Luke 22:61]). He brought him ( ηγαγεν αυτον ). Effective second aorist active indicative of αγο as if Andrew had to overcome some resistance on Simon's part. Thou shalt be called Cephas ( συ κληθηση Κηφας ). Apparently before Simon spoke. We do not know whether Jesus had seen Simon before or not, but he at once gives him a nickname that will characterize him some day, though not yet, when he makes the noble confession ([Matthew 16:17]), and Jesus will say, "Thou art Peter." Here the future passive indicative of καλεω is only prophecy. The Aramaic Χηφας (rock) is only applied to Simon in John except by Paul ([1 Corinthians 1:12]; [Galatians 1:18], etc.). But the Greek Πετρος is used by all. In the ancient Greek πετρα was used for the massive ledge of rock like Stone Mountain while πετρος was a detached fragment of the ledge, though itself large. This distinction may exist in [Matthew 16:17], except that Jesus probably used Aramaic which would not have such a distinction.</div></div><div class="themes-divider">🔍 Temas discutidos</div><div class="theme-card"><div class="theme-word">ἐμβλέψας</div><div class="consensus-bar"><div style="width:85%;background:#4caf50"></div></div><div class="opinion" style="border-left-color:#4caf50">✅ <strong>Bengel</strong> Describe ἐμβλέψας como una mirada eficaz que toma posesión completa de Pedro</div><div class="opinion" style="border-left-color:#4caf50">✅ <strong>Robertson</strong> Conecta ἐμβλέψας con la mirada ansiosa de Juan hacia Jesús en v.36 y la mirada de Jesús a Pedro en Lucas 22:61</div><div class="theme-summary">Ambos comentaristas coinciden en que ἐμβλέψας indica una mirada intensa y significativa de Jesús hacia Pedro.</div></div></div><div class="section-toggle" onclick="toggleSection('patr-42')" role="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="patr-42" tabindex="0"><span class="section-icon">👨‍🏫</span> Patrística · 5 temas · 21 citas <span class="arrow" id="arrow-patr-42">▶</span></div><div id="patr-42" class="collapsed section-content patr-section" role="region"><div class="patr-theme"><div class="patr-theme-header" onclick="document.getElementById('pt97e2a050').classList.toggle('collapsed');this.querySelector('.arrow').classList.toggle('open')"><strong>Conocimiento divino y predestinación</strong><div class="consensus-bar" style="width:60px"><div style="width:85%;background:#4caf50"></div></div><span class="consensus-label" style="color:#4caf50">alto</span><span class="arrow">▼</span></div><div id="pt97e2a050" class="collapsed" style="padding:0.5rem 0.2rem 0"><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>Augustine of Hippo</strong> (s.IV-V (354-430)) <span class="patr-meta">— Obispo de Hipona, Doctor de la Iglesia, discípulo de Ambrosio de Milán (Hipona)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agust%C3%ADn_de_Hipona" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.42]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"There was nothing very great in our Lord saying whose son he was, for our Lord knew the names of all His saints, having predestinated them before the foundation of the world."</div></div><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>Augustine of Hippo</strong> (s.IV-V (354-430)) <span class="patr-meta">— Obispo de Hipona, Doctor de la Iglesia, discípulo de Ambrosio de Milán (Hipona)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agust%C3%ADn_de_Hipona" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.42]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"He knew all the names of His own saints, whom He predestinated before the foundation of the world; and dost thou wonder that He said to one man, Thou art the son of this man, and thou shalt be called this or that?"</div></div><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> (s.V (c.376-444)) <span class="patr-meta">— Patriarca de Alejandría, Concilio de Éfeso (Alejandría)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cirilo_de_Alejandr%C3%ADa" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.42]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"He after a Divine sort looketh upon him, Who seeth the hearts and reins; and seeth to how great piety the disciple will attain, of how great virtue he will be possessed, and at what consummation he will leave off. For He Who knoweth all things before they be is not ignorant of ought."</div></div><div class="patr-resumen">Los padres concuerdan en que Cristo demostró su conocimiento divino al conocer inmediatamente a Pedro y predecir su futuro. Este conocimiento se fundamenta en la predestinación divina desde antes de la fundación del mundo.</div></div></div><div class="patr-theme"><div class="patr-theme-header" onclick="document.getElementById('pta1aace11').classList.toggle('collapsed');this.querySelector('.arrow').classList.toggle('open')"><strong>El cambio de nombre de Simón a Pedro y su significado eclesiológico</strong><div class="consensus-bar" style="width:60px"><div style="width:85%;background:#4caf50"></div></div><span class="consensus-label" style="color:#4caf50">alto</span><span class="arrow">▼</span></div><div id="pta1aace11" class="collapsed" style="padding:0.5rem 0.2rem 0"><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>Augustine of Hippo</strong> (s.IV-V (354-430)) <span class="patr-meta">— Obispo de Hipona, Doctor de la Iglesia, discípulo de Ambrosio de Milán (Hipona)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agust%C3%ADn_de_Hipona" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.42]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"But it was a great thing for our Lord to change his name from Simon to Peter. Peter is from petra, rock, which rock is the Church: so that the name of Peter represents the Church."</div></div><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#ff9800"><div class="patr-cite-header">⚠️ <strong>John Chrysostom</strong> (s.IV-V (c.349-407)) <span class="patr-meta">— Patriarca de Constantinopla, "Boca de Oro", discípulo de Diodoro de Tarso (Antioquía/Constantinopla)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Juan_Cris%C3%B3stomo" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.42]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"But Peter makes no reply to these words; as yet he knew nothing clearly, but still was learning. And observe, that not even the prediction is fully set forth; for Jesus did not say, 'I will change thy name to Peter, and upon this rock I will build My Church,' but, 'Thou shalt be called Cephas.'"</div></div><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>John Chrysostom</strong> (s.IV-V (c.349-407)) <span class="patr-meta">— Patriarca de Constantinopla, "Boca de Oro", discípulo de Diodoro de Tarso (Antioquía/Constantinopla)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Juan_Cris%C3%B3stomo" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.42]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"What then says Christ? You are Simon, the son of Jonas; you shall be called Cephas. Thus since you have proclaimed my Father, I too name him that begot you; all but saying, As you are son of Jonas, even so am I of my Father."</div></div><div class="patr-resumen">Los padres ven el cambio de nombre como proféticamente significativo, aunque Crisóstomo nota que la revelación fue gradual. Agustín conecta directamente a Pedro con la roca que es la Iglesia.</div></div></div><div class="patr-theme"><div class="patr-theme-header" onclick="document.getElementById('pt1ce76406').classList.toggle('collapsed');this.querySelector('.arrow').classList.toggle('open')"><strong>La revelación gradual de la divinidad de Cristo</strong><div class="consensus-bar" style="width:60px"><div style="width:85%;background:#4caf50"></div></div><span class="consensus-label" style="color:#4caf50">alto</span><span class="arrow">▼</span></div><div id="pt1ce76406" class="collapsed" style="padding:0.5rem 0.2rem 0"><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>John Chrysostom</strong> (s.IV-V (c.349-407)) <span class="patr-meta">— Patriarca de Constantinopla, "Boca de Oro", discípulo de Diodoro de Tarso (Antioquía/Constantinopla)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Juan_Cris%C3%B3stomo" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.42]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"He begins from this time forth to reveal the things belonging to His Divinity, and to open It out little by little by predictions. So He did in the case of Nathaniel and the Samaritan woman. For prophecies bring men over not less than miracles; and are free from the appearance of boasting."</div></div><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>John Chrysostom</strong> (s.IV-V (c.349-407)) <span class="patr-meta">— Patriarca de Constantinopla, "Boca de Oro", discípulo de Diodoro de Tarso (Antioquía/Constantinopla)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Juan_Cris%C3%B3stomo" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.42]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"The former speech would have expressed too great authority and power; for Christ does not immediately nor at first declare all His power, but speaks for a while in a humbler tone"</div></div><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> (s.V (c.376-444)) <span class="patr-meta">— Patriarca de Alejandría, Concilio de Éfeso (Alejandría)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cirilo_de_Alejandr%C3%ADa" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.42]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"And herein does He specially instruct him that is called, that being Very God, He hath knowledge untaught. For not having needed a single word, nor even sought to learn who or whence the man came to Him"</div></div><div class="patr-resumen">Los padres reconocen que Cristo reveló su divinidad de manera progresiva, comenzando con profecías y conocimiento sobrenatural. Esta pedagogía divina evitaba la apariencia de jactancia mientras demostraba su naturaleza divina.</div></div></div><div class="patr-theme"><div class="patr-theme-header" onclick="document.getElementById('ptad7d22c0').classList.toggle('collapsed');this.querySelector('.arrow').classList.toggle('open')"><strong>La obediencia y disposición de los discípulos</strong><div class="consensus-bar" style="width:60px"><div style="width:85%;background:#4caf50"></div></div><span class="consensus-label" style="color:#4caf50">alto</span><span class="arrow">▼</span></div><div id="ptad7d22c0" class="collapsed" style="padding:0.5rem 0.2rem 0"><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>John Chrysostom</strong> (s.IV-V (c.349-407)) <span class="patr-meta">— Patriarca de Constantinopla, "Boca de Oro", discípulo de Diodoro de Tarso (Antioquía/Constantinopla)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Juan_Cris%C3%B3stomo" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.42]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"And behold, I beg of you, the mind of Peter obedient and tractable from the very beginning; he ran to Him without any delay; 'He brought him,' saith St. John, 'to Jesus.'"</div></div><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>Thomas Aquinas</strong> (s.XIII (1225-1274)) <span class="patr-meta">— Teólogo escolástico, Doctor de la Iglesia, discípulo de Alberto Magno (Italia)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tom%C3%A1s_de_Aquino" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.42]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"This gives recognition to Peter's obedience, for he came at once, without delay. And consider the devotion of Andrew: for he brought him to Jesus and not to himself (for he knew that he himself was weak); and so he leads him to Christ to be instructed by him."</div></div><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>John Chrysostom</strong> (s.IV-V (c.349-407)) <span class="patr-meta">— Patriarca de Constantinopla, "Boca de Oro", discípulo de Diodoro de Tarso (Antioquía/Constantinopla)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Juan_Cris%C3%B3stomo" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.43]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"Wherefore it does not occur to me any more to wonder how Philip followed Christ. Andrew was persuaded when he had heard from John, and Peter the same from Andrew, but Philip not having learned anything from any but Christ who said to him only this, 'Follow Me,' straightway obeyed"</div></div><div class="patr-resumen">Los padres elogian la inmediata obediencia de los discípulos, especialmente Pedro y Felipe. Esta prontitud para seguir a Cristo se ve como evidencia de corazones bien dispuestos y de la gracia divina operando en ellos.</div></div></div><div class="patr-theme"><div class="patr-theme-header" onclick="document.getElementById('pta422b503').classList.toggle('collapsed');this.querySelector('.arrow').classList.toggle('open')"><strong>Felipe como predicador y el testimonio de las Escrituras</strong><div class="consensus-bar" style="width:60px"><div style="width:85%;background:#4caf50"></div></div><span class="consensus-label" style="color:#4caf50">alto</span><span class="arrow">▼</span></div><div id="pta422b503" class="collapsed" style="padding:0.5rem 0.2rem 0"><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>John Chrysostom</strong> (s.IV-V (c.349-407)) <span class="patr-meta">— Patriarca de Constantinopla, "Boca de Oro", discípulo de Diodoro de Tarso (Antioquía/Constantinopla)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Juan_Cris%C3%B3stomo" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.45]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"He says this, to make his preaching credible, which it must be if it rests on Moses and the Prophets besides, and by this to abash his hearer. For since Nathanael was an exact man, and one who viewed all things with truth, as Christ also testified and the event showed, Philip with reason refers him to Moses and the Prophets"</div></div><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> (s.V (c.376-444)) <span class="patr-meta">— Patriarca de Alejandría, Concilio de Éfeso (Alejandría)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cirilo_de_Alejandr%C3%ADa" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.45]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"Exceeding swift was the disciple unto the bearing fruit, that hereby he might shew himself akin in disposition to them that had preceded. For he findeth Nathanael, not simply meeting him coming along, but making diligent search for him. For he knew that he was most painstaking and fond of learning."</div></div><div class="patr-citation" style="border-left-color:#4caf50"><div class="patr-cite-header">✅ <strong>Thomas Aquinas</strong> (s.XIII (1225-1274)) <span class="patr-meta">— Teólogo escolástico, Doctor de la Iglesia, discípulo de Alberto Magno (Italia)</span> <a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tom%C3%A1s_de_Aquino" target="_blank" class="patr-link">📖</a> <span class="patr-verse-ref">[v.42]</span></div><div class="patr-cite-text">"This shows us that the efforts and the aim of preachers should not be to win for themselves the fruits of their preaching, i.e., to turn them to themselves, but to lead them to Christ"</div></div><div class="patr-resumen">Los padres alaban la sabiduría de Felipe al fundamentar su predicación en las Escrituras y su celo evangelístico. Su método de referirse a Moisés y los profetas se considera apropiado para convencer a Natanael, un hombre instruido en la ley.</div></div></div></div><div class="section-toggle" onclick="toggleSection('tc-42')" role="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="tc-42" tabindex="0"><span class="section-icon">⚖️</span> Crítica Textual <span style="font-size:0.7rem;color:#ff9800">(4 lecturas)</span> <span class="arrow" id="arrow-tc-42">▶</span></div><div id="tc-42" class="collapsed section-content tc-section"><div class="tc-verdict" style="margin-bottom:0.8rem"><div class="tc-verdict-text"><strong>Veredicto:</strong> La lectura #42.2 (Ἰωνᾶ) es probablemente original, con amplio apoyo manuscrito incluyendo A, B2, Δ, Ψ y la mayoría bizantina. La lectura #42.1 (Ἰωάννου) representa una armonización con Mateo 16:17 donde Pedro es llamado 'Simón Bar-Jonás'. La lectura #42.3 (Ἰωαννᾶ) es una variante menor de #42.1. La lectura #42.4 que añade 'hermano de Andrés' es una glosa explicativa.</div><div class="consensus-bar" style="max-width:200px"><div style="width:60%;background:#ff9800"></div></div><span style="font-size:0.72rem;color:#ff9800;font-weight:600;margin-left:4px">Confianza: media</span><div class="tc-impact"><strong>Impacto:</strong> La diferencia entre las formas del nombre del padre de Pedro (Jonás vs Juan) afecta la identificación genealógica, pero no tiene implicaciones teológicas mayores. La variación puede reflejar diferentes tradiciones sobre el nombre del padre de Pedro.</div></div><div style="overflow-x:auto;margin-bottom:0.5rem"><table class="tc-table"><tr class="tc-header"><th class="tc-ms-col">MS</th><th class="tc-ctx">ὁ</th><th class="tc-ctx">υἱὸς</th><th class="tc-var-col" title="Columna variante 1: Ἰωάννου">⚡1</th><th class="tc-var-col" title="Columna variante 2: Θ">⚡2</th><th class="tc-ctx">κληθήσῃ</th><th class="tc-ctx">Κηφᾶς</th></tr><tr><td class="tc-ms-cell" onclick="showMSSPanel('𝔓66, 75, 106 א (B* Ἰωάνου) L Wsupp 33 ita, b, f, ff2, l, r1 vgmss copsa, bo Jerome')"><span class="ms-chip" style="background:#b71c1c;color:white;font-weight:700" data-s="𝔓66" onclick="event.stopPropagation();showMSInfo(this,'𝔓66');">𝔓66</span><span class="ms-chip" style="background:#e3f2fd;color:#1565c0" data-s="75" onclick="event.stopPropagation();showMSInfo(this,'75');">75</span><span class="ms-chip" style="background:#e3f2fd;color:#1565c0" data-s="106" onclick="event.stopPropagation();showMSInfo(this,'106');">106</span><span class="ms-chip" style="background:#1565c0;color:white;font-weight:700" data-s="א" onclick="event.stopPropagation();showMSInfo(this,'א');">א</span><span class="ms-chip" style="background:#1565c0;color:white;font-weight:700" data-s="L" onclick="event.stopPropagation();showMSInfo(this,'L');">L</span><span class="ms-chip" style="background:#fff3e0;color:#e65100" data-s="Wsupp" onclick="event.stopPropagation();showMSInfo(this,'Wsupp');">Wsupp</span><span class="ms-chip" style="background:#e3f2fd;color:#1565c0" data-s="33" onclick="event.stopPropagation();showMSInfo(this,'33');">33</span><span class="ms-chip" style="background:#f3e5f5;color:#6a1b9a" data-s="ita" onclick="event.stopPropagation();showMSInfo(this,'ita');">ita</span><span class="ms-chip" style="" data-s="b" onclick="event.stopPropagation();showMSInfo(this,'b');">b</span><span class="ms-chip" style="" data-s="f" onclick="event.stopPropagation();showMSInfo(this,'f');">f</span><span class="ms-chip" style="background:#f3e5f5;color:#6a1b9a" data-s="ff2" onclick="event.stopPropagation();showMSInfo(this,'ff2');">ff2</span><span cla